\phantom{A}}}{\\bbox[2pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147148 1072 #uuid "812e5cc8-ff6f-5e67-84bf-f012dec63466" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147148 1074 "Interpreting the fraction as division, we can write our expression as:\n\n$$\n5\\div 4\\times 8\n$$\n\nWe can write an equivalent expression by swapping the $\\div 4$ and the $\\times 8{:}$\n\n$$\n5\\times 8\\div 4\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the $8\\div 4$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n5\\times \\dfrac{\\bbox[2pt, border:1pt solid black]{8}}{\\bbox[2pt, border:1pt solid black]{4}}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147149 1069 "e-12573" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147149 1071 "Evaluate $21 \\times \\dfrac{7}{3}$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147149 1072 #uuid "dfa40a5c-e8ed-55c0-83e7-bd701eae0da5" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147149 1074 "Interpreting the fraction as division, we can write our expression as:\n$21\\times 7\\div 3$\n\nWe can write an equivalent expression by swapping the $\\times 7$ and the $\\div 3{:}$\n$21 \\div 3\\times 7$\n\nExpressing the division as a fraction, we arrive at:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{21}{3}\\times 7\n$$\n\nWe now solve:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{21}{3}}_{7}\\times 7 = 7\\times 7 = 49\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147150 1069 "e-13053" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147150 1071 "What is the outlier of the data set given below?\n$28, \\quad 11, \\quad 17, \\quad 841, \\quad 33, \\quad 41, \\quad 50$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147150 1072 #uuid "7b4e23ec-8296-5259-a615-02a122cd6dde" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147150 1074 "An outlier is a value that is either much larger or much smaller than all other values of a data set.\n\nLet's begin by writing our data set in ascending order:\n\n$$\n11, \\quad 17, \\quad 28, \\quad 33, \\quad 41, \\quad 50, \\quad {\\color{blue}\\underline{841}}\n$$\n\nIn the given data set, the value $\\color{blue}841$ is much larger than all other values. So, $\\color{blue}841$ is an outlier." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147151 1069 "e-13054" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147151 1071 "What are the outliers of the data set given below?\n$289, \\quad 927, \\quad 221, \\quad 201, \\quad 209, \\quad 11, \\quad 291, \\quad 217$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147151 1072 #uuid "fdefb9c7-657c-5d2b-b6a2-ff37e20136ff" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147151 1074 "An outlier is a value that is either much larger or much smaller than all other values of a data set.\n\nLet's begin by writing our data set in ascending order.\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\underline{11}}, \\quad 201, \\quad 209, \\quad 217, \\quad 221, \\quad 289, \\quad 291, \\quad{\\color{blue}\\underline{927}}\n$$\n\nIn the given data set, the value $\\color{blue}11$ is much smaller than all of the other values, and the value ${\\color{blue}927}$ is much larger than all other values. So, $\\color{blue}11$ and ${\\color{blue}927}$ are outliers." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147152 1069 "e-13064" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147152 1071 "What is the missing number?\n\n$\\qquad$ $0$ of $20 = 35$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147152 1072 #uuid "2ed2e15c-4ac7-5cd8-bfda-65573ff6fb44" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147152 1074 "We can write the given problem as the following product:\n\n$$\n0\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write our problem as follows:\n$0$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we write an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{35}{20} = \\dfrac{35 \\times 5}{20 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{175}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{175}{100} = 175 \\%\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147153 1069 "e-13065" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147153 1071 "If $0$ then $0$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147153 1072 #uuid "146d6a70-f2ed-5f9a-9d6a-5e4c856acda0" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147153 1074 "Using the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write our problem as follows:\n$0$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{35} = \\dfrac{7 \\div 7}{35 \\div 7} = \\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we write an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 20}{5 \\times 20} = \\dfrac{20}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{20}{100} = 20\\%\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147154 1069 "e-13066" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147154 1071 "If $0$ then what is the missing number?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147154 1072 #uuid "e650884c-e858-5213-8ff4-a39f9358edc0" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147154 1074 "We are given that the part is $36$, the percentage is $50\\%$, and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $50\\%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{50}{100} = \\dfrac{50\\div 50}{100\\div 50} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nwhole &= 36 \\div 50% \\\\\n&= 36 \\div \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 36 \\cdot \\frac{2}{1} \\\\\n&= 36 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 72\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147155 1069 "e-13067" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147155 1071 "$34\\%$ of a number is $170$. What is the number?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147155 1072 #uuid "4bfda07b-18fd-541a-a627-ef7f1e47f41a" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147155 1074 "We're given that the part is $170$, the percentage is $34\\%$, and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $34\\%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{34}{100} = \\dfrac{34\\div 2}{100\\div 2} = \\dfrac{17}{50}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nwhole &= 170 \\div 34% \\\\\n&= 170 \\div \\frac{17}{50} \\\\\n&= 170 \\cdot \\frac{50}{17} \\\\\n&= \\frac{170}{17} \\cdot 50 \\\\\n&= 10 \\cdot 50 \\\\\n&= 500\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147156 1069 "e-13068" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147156 1071 "In the math club, there are $16$ girls and some boys. If the girls represent $40\\%$ of the total members of the club, how many members does the math club have?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147156 1072 #uuid "bd7aaf49-0da5-53c8-a5ee-863523548957" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147156 1074 "We're given a part and a percentage, and we need to find the whole.\n\nIn this case, the part is the number of girls in the math club, which is $16$. The percentage is $40\\%$, and the whole is the total number of club members.\n\nTo find the whole, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $40\\%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{100} = \\dfrac{40\\div 20}{100\\div 20} = \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nwhole &= 16 \\div 40% \\\\\n&= 16 \\div \\frac{2}{5} \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{2} \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 8 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the club has $40$ members." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147157 1069 "e-13085" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147157 1071 "What is $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ as a percentage?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147157 1072 #uuid "2c5b2a8e-df1c-5bab-b55e-5ecea02d85f3" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147157 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nas an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2} = \\dfrac{3 \\times 50}{2 \\times 50} = \\dfrac{150}{100}\n$$\n\nWe can now write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{150}{100} = 150 \\%\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147158 1069 "e-13087" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147158 1071 "To pass a theoretical driving test, John must correctly answer $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ of the total number of questions. What percentage of questions does John need to answer correctly to pass the test?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147158 1072 #uuid "985da25e-5aff-5e16-8ade-b5c5314c017d" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147158 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nas an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{3 \\times 25}{4 \\times 25} = \\dfrac{75}{100}\n$$\n\nWe can now write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{75}{100} = 75 \\%\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147159 1069 "e-13088" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147159 1071 "What is $8.7 \\%$ as a decimal?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147159 1072 #uuid "1f9f01b2-0f85-5fd2-8e71-f35d69462c71" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147159 1074 "To convert $8.7 \\%$ into a decimal, we divide $8.7$ by $100$.\n\nRemember that to divide a number by $100$, we move the decimal point two places to the left:\n\n$$\n8.7 \\div 100 = 0.087\n$$\n\nSo, $8.7 \\%$ is the same as $0.087$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147160 1069 "e-13089" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147160 1071 "What is $60\\%$ as a fraction?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147160 1072 #uuid "2f242225-45e2-509b-adeb-b99fa21d8a28" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147160 1074 "First, we write the number in front of the percent sign ($60$) as a fraction with denominator $100{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{60}{100}\n$$\n\nWe now need to simplify this fraction. Dividing the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{60}{100} = \\dfrac{60\\div 10}{100\\div 10} = \\dfrac{6}{10}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{10} = \\dfrac{6\\div 2}{10\\div 2} = \\dfrac35\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n60\\% = \\dfrac35\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147161 1069 "e-13090" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147161 1071 "Express $180 \\%$ as a decimal." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147161 1072 #uuid "b97e6087-4a6c-504b-8f3c-86847e72245d" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147161 1074 "To convert $180 \\%$ into a decimal, we divide $180$ by $100$.\n\nRemember that to divide a number by $100$, we move the decimal point two places to the left:\n\n$$\n180 \\div 100 = 1.8\n$$\n\nSo, $180 \\%$ is the same as $1.8$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147162 1069 "e-13091" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147162 1071 "Donald took $35 \\%$ less time than he expected to complete his geometry homework. What is $35 \\%$ expressed as a fraction?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147162 1072 #uuid "3e2ef860-6ff9-5763-926d-f6ff991bcce5" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147162 1074 "First, we write the number in front of the percent sign as a fraction with denominator $100$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{35}{100}\n$$\n\nWe now simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{35}{100} = \\dfrac{35 \\div 5}{100 \\div 5} = \\dfrac{7}{20}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n35 \\% = \\dfrac{7}{20} \\,\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147163 1069 "e-13092" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147163 1071 "Convert $0.7$ to a percentage." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147163 1072 #uuid "a6f3b2f0-58ad-55e9-81d6-8e609baecb3b" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147163 1074 "To convert $0.7$ into a percent, we multiply it by $100$.\n\nRemember that to multiply a number by $100$, we move the decimal point two places to the right:\n\n$$\n0.7 \\times 100 = 70\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the result using the percent sign. So, our final answer is $70 \\%$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147164 1069 "e-13093" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147164 1071 "What is $1.65$ expressed as a percentage?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147164 1072 #uuid "a253e544-8116-5646-b62b-813ded53f906" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147164 1074 "To convert $1.65$ into a percentage, we multiply it by $100$.\n\nRemember that to multiply a number by $100$, we move the decimal point two places to the right:\n\n$$\n1.65 \\times 100 = 165\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the result using the percent sign. So, our final answer is $165 \\%$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147165 1069 "e-13105" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147165 1071 "Suppose that the temperatures registered yesterday in the southwest of the United States were as follows:\n$19^\\circ\\text{C}, \\quad 29^\\circ\\text{C}, \\quad 15^\\circ\\text{C}, \\quad 12^\\circ\\text{C}, \\quad 16^\\circ\\text{C}, \\quad 26^\\circ\\text{C}, \\quad 29^\\circ\\text{C}$\nWhat is the range of temperatures?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147165 1072 #uuid "2f39e8f7-7433-5573-810a-8fb344f56174" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147165 1074 "Our data set is not arranged from smallest to greatest. So, let's first order the numbers:\n\n$$\n12, \\: 15, \\: 16, \\: 19, \\: 26, \\: 29, \\: 29\n$$\n\nNow, we see that the smallest value is $\\color{red}12$ and the greatest is ${\\color{blue}29}$:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\underline{12}}, \\: 15, \\: 16, \\: 19, \\: 26, \\: 29, \\: {\\color{blue}\\underline{29}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the data set is:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\text{greatest}} - {\\color{red}\\text{smallest}} = {\\color{blue}29} - {\\color{red}12} = 17\n$$\n\nSo, the range of temperatures is $17^{\\circ}\\text{C}$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147166 1069 "e-13106" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147166 1071 "Arthur recorded how many hours he worked per day each day over a period of $6$ days and got the following data:\n$7, \\: 5, \\: 6, \\: 7, \\: 6, \\: 8$\nFind the lower quartile of this data set." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147166 1072 #uuid "88a2683d-1162-502c-8fa9-f3ba0e1782e0" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147166 1074 "Our data set is not arranged from smallest to greatest. So, let's first order the numbers:\n$5, \\: 6, \\: 6, \\: 7, \\: 7, \\: 8$\n\nNow, we consider the lower half of the data:\n$\\underbrace{5, \\: 6, \\: 6,}_{\\text{lower half}} \\: 7, \\: 7, \\: 8$\n\nNext, we find the median of the lower half. In this case, it's the middle number:\n$5, \\: {\\color{blue}\\underline{6}}, \\:6$\n\nSo, the lower quartile is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}6}$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147167 1069 "e-13107" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147167 1071 "What is the interquartile range of the data set below?\n\n$19, \\: 21, \\: 21, \\: 25, \\: 28, \\: 33, \\: 36$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147167 1072 #uuid "4a4b1f68-d385-554c-86b3-963e15dea523" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147167 1074 "Notice that our data set is already arranged from smallest to greatest.\n\nThe median of the data set is the middle number ($\\color{blue}25$), so the lower and upper halves of the data set are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{19, \\: 21, \\: 21,}_{\\text{lower half}} \\: {\\color{blue}{25}}, \\: \\underbrace{28, \\: 33, \\: 36}_{\\text{upper half}}\n$$\n\nThe lower quartile is the median of the lower half:\n$19, \\: {\\color{red}\\underline{21}}, \\: 21$\nSo, the lower quartile is $21$.\n\nLikewise, the upper quartile is the median of the upper half:\n$28, \\: {\\color{red}\\underline{33}}, \\: 36$\nSo, the upper quartile is $33$.\n\nFinally, the interquartile range of the data set is:\n\n$$\n\\text{IQR} = \\text{upper quartile} - \\text{lower quartile} = 33 - 21 =12\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147168 1069 "e-13108" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147168 1071 "What is $4 \\%$ of $35$?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147168 1072 #uuid "034e763d-a3e1-52e8-90a7-9fac9ce11083" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147168 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}= \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is $35$, and the percentage is $4 \\%$.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n4 \\% = 4 \\div 100 = 0.04\n$$\n\nThen, we apply our formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 4% \\cdot 35 \\\\\n&= 0.04 \\cdot 35 \\\\\n&= 1.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4 \\%$ of $35$ is $1.4$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n4 \\% = \\dfrac{4}{100}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply our formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 4% \\cdot 35 \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{100} \\cdot 35 \\\\\n&= \\frac{140}{100} \\\\\n&= 1.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4 \\%$ of $35$ is $1.4$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147169 1069 "e-13109" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147169 1071 "What is $140 \\%$ of $30$?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147169 1072 #uuid "a5fc07bc-6168-5525-acfb-a36ee021773d" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147169 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}= \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is $30$, and the percentage is $140\\%$.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n140 \\% = 140 \\div 100 = 1.4\n$$\n\nThen, we apply our formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 140% \\cdot 30 \\\\\n&= 1.4 \\cdot 30 \\\\\n&= 42\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $140 \\%$ of $30$ is $42$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n140 \\% = \\dfrac{140}{100} = \\dfrac{140 \\div 20}{100\\div 20} = \\dfrac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply our formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 140% \\cdot 30 \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{5} \\cdot 30 \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot \\frac{30}{5} \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 6 \\\\\n&= 42\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $140 \\%$ of $30$ is $42$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147170 1069 "e-13110" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147170 1071 "What is $3.6 \\%$ of $25$?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147170 1072 #uuid "8dde0f33-28c8-569b-a1f8-9390d8dafc35" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147170 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}= \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is $25$, and the percentage is $3.6 \\%$.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n3.6 \\% = 3.6 \\div 100 = 0.036\n$$\n\nThen, we apply our formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 3.6% \\cdot 25 \\\\\n&= 0.036 \\cdot 25 \\\\\n&= 0.9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3.6 \\%$ of $25$ is $0.9$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n3.6 \\% = \\dfrac{3.6}{100} = \\dfrac{3.6 \\times 10}{100 \\times 10} = \\dfrac{36}{1,000}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply our formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 3.6% \\cdot 25 \\\\\n&= \\frac{36}{1, 000} \\cdot 25 \\\\\n&= \\frac{900}{1, 000} \\\\\n&= 0.9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3.6 \\%$ of $25$ is $0.9$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147171 1069 "e-13122" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147171 1071 "What is the mean of the following data set?\n$9, \\: 10, \\: 8, \\: 11, \\: 12$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147171 1072 #uuid "dda23f4a-d2a4-52e0-8d32-3feaf51d5c27" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147171 1074 "First, notice that our data set contains $5$ numbers.\n\nNow, let's add all the numbers together:\n\n$$\n9 + 10 + 8 + 11 + 12 = 50\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide the sum by the number of data points:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{50}{5} = 10\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean of the data set is $10$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147172 1069 "e-13124" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147172 1071 "A student obtained the following grades on her last $5$ chemistry quizzes:\n\n$7, \\: 8, \\: 9, \\: 7, \\: 6$\nWhat was the student's mean score?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147172 1072 #uuid "4fd79d57-c9cf-56ce-a58f-bb8dc4d0aa95" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147172 1074 "First, notice that our data set contains $5$ numbers.\n\nNow, let's add all the numbers together:\n\n$$\n7 + 8 + 9 + 7 + 6 = 37\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide the sum by the number of data points:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{37}{5} = 7.4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the student's mean score is $7.4$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147173 1069 "e-13366" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147173 1071 "Marlon asked his friends about their weights and got the following data (in kilograms):\n\n$63.2, \\: 50, \\: 48.4, \\: 45.6, \\: 45, \\: 55, \\: 58.9, \\: 92.1, \\: 88$\n\nWhat is the lower quartile of the given data set?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147173 1072 #uuid "993b6662-4c21-5b8a-af94-397c0a877f91" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147173 1074 "Our data set is not arranged from smallest to greatest. So, let's first order the numbers:\n\n$$\n45, \\: 45.6, \\: 48.4, \\: 50, \\: 55, \\: 58.9, \\: 63.2, \\: 88, \\: 92.1\n$$\n\nThe median of the data set is the middle number ($\\color{blue}55$).\n\nNow, we consider the lower half of the data:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{45, \\: 45.6, \\: 48.4, \\: 50,}_{\\text{lower half}} \\: {\\color{blue}55}, \\: 58.9, \\: 63.2, \\: 88, \\: 92.1\n$$\n\nWe want to find the lower quartile, so we need to find the median of the lower half. In this case, it's the mean of the two middle numbers:\n\n$$\n45, \\: {\\color{red}\\underline{45.6}}, \\: {\\color{red}\\underline{48.4}}, \\: 50\n$$\n\nSo, the lower quartile is:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{red}45.6}+{\\color{red}48.4}}{2} = \\dfrac{94}{2} = 47\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147174 1069 "e-13755" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147174 1071 "What fraction is missing from the statement below?\n\n$0$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147174 1072 #uuid "ba1bbec5-0ff2-52ec-ac2e-876a8ea4c70e" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147174 1074 "The given expression is\n$(15\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 13}}) \\times (4\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 5}})$.\n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can write an equivalent expression by swapping the ${\\color{red}{\\div\\, 5}}$ and the ${\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 13}}$:\n$(15\\, {\\color{red}{\\div\\, 5}}) \\times (4\\, {\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 13}})$\n\nWe can now interpret the divisions as fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15} 5 \\times \\dfrac 4 {13}\n$$\n\nSo the missing fraction is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15} 5\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147175 1069 "e-13758" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147175 1071 "Find an expression of the form $0$ equivalent to $\\dfrac{4}{3}\\times\\dfrac{9}{2}$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147175 1072 #uuid "ebdd2229-6712-561c-821b-7286460a6824" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147175 1074 "Interpreting the fraction as division, we can write our expression as\n$(4\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 3}}) \\times (9\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 2}})$.\n\nWe use parentheses to make it easier to read.\n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can write an equivalent expression by swapping the ${\\color{red}{\\div\\, 3}}$ and the ${\\color{blue}{\\div\\,2}}$:\n$(4\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\,2}}) \\times (9\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 3}})$\n\nFinally, we can write the $4\\div 2$ and $9\\div 3$ as fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{2} \\times \\dfrac{9}{3}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147176 1069 "e-13761" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147176 1071 "Evaluate $\\dfrac{12}{7}\\times \\dfrac{21}{4}$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147176 1072 #uuid "830169cc-f331-5a91-81d6-374aad29e951" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147176 1074 "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n$(12\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}})\\times(21\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}})$.\n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the ${\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}}$ and ${\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}}$:\n$(12\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}})\\times(21\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}})$\n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{4}\\times\\dfrac{21}{7}\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{4}_(⏟)_(3) \\cdot \\frac{21}{7}_(⏟)_(3) = 3 \\cdot 3 = 9\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147177 1069 "e-14182" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147177 1071 "Find the value of $\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^2$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147177 1072 #uuid "a4cc2406-5f53-5a80-9e85-9cafd1fa2576" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147177 1074 "We proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write $\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^2$ with multiplication signs:\n$(\\frac{4}{5})^{2}|= \\frac{4}{5} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5}_(⏟)_([math]{\\color{blue}{2}}[/math] times)$\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n$(\\frac{4}{5})^{2}|= \\frac{4}{5} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5}; = \\frac{4 \\cdot 4}{5 \\cdot 5}; = \\frac{16}{25}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^2= \\dfrac{16}{25}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147178 1069 "e-14184" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147178 1071 "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form, $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3 =$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147178 1072 #uuid "24da41ad-50c6-5654-8ebe-4c47c51ebd5d" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147178 1074 "We proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3$ with multiplication signs:\n$(\\frac{3}{5})^{3}|= \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5}_(⏟)_([math]{\\color{blue}{3}}[/math] times)$\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n$(\\frac{3}{5})^{3}|= \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5}; = \\frac{3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3}{5 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5}; = \\frac{9 \\cdot 3}{25 \\cdot 5}; = \\frac{27}{125}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^3=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} \\dfrac{27}{125}}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147179 1069 "e-14188" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147179 1071 "Evaluate $4^2 +(5-3)$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147179 1072 #uuid "6d89587f-dacf-5ce3-95f0-9dd51d307af8" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147179 1074 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. We start with the **P**arentheses:\n\n$$\n4^2\\,+\\underbrace{(5-3)}_{\\large\\color{blue} 2} =4^2\\,+ {\\color{blue} 2}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{4^2}^{\\large\\color{red} 16}+2 = {\\color{red} 16}+2\n$$\n\nFinally, we perform the **A**ddition:\n\n$$\n16+2 = 18\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4^2 +(5-3)=18\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147180 1069 "e-14191" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147180 1071 "Calculate $28\\div(7-5)^2$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147180 1072 #uuid "b035d6d2-aaca-5ea3-8d34-1748b6acfcd6" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147180 1074 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. We start with the **P**arentheses:\n\n$$\n28\\div \\underbrace{(7-5)}_{\\large\\color{blue} 2}{}^2 =28\\div {\\color{blue} 2}^2\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n28\\div\\overbrace{2^2}^{\\large\\color{red} 4} =28\\div {\\color{red} 4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we perform the **D**ivision:\n$28\\div 4 =7$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n28\\div(7-5)^2=7\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147181 1069 "e-14193" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147181 1071 "Evaluate $5\\times \\left(3+2^2\\right)$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147181 1072 #uuid "ef92369b-53e8-56ec-b1f2-ee36dbf13839" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147181 1074 "According to PEMDAS, we should first calculate the expression inside the **P**arentheses:\n\n$$\n\\left(3+2^2\\right)\n$$\n\nTo evaluate\n\n$$\n\\left(3+2^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe should again apply PEMDAS. We start with the **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n3\\,+\\overbrace{2^2}^{\\large\\color{blue} 4} =3+ {\\color{blue} 4}\n$$\n\nThen, we perform the **A**ddition:\n\n$$\n3 + 4 = {\\color{red} 7}\n$$\n\nSo we have:\n\n$$\n5\\times \\underbrace{\\left(3+2^2\\right)}_{\\large\\color{red} 7} =5\\times {\\color{red} 7}\n$$\n\nNow that we have calculated the expression inside the **P**arentheses, we can finally move on to the **M**ultiplication:\n\n$$\n5 \\times 7 = 35\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n5\\times\\left(3+2^2\\right)=35\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147182 1069 "e-14266" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147182 1071 "The number of hedgehogs $N(t)$ in a particular nature reserve after $t$ years is modeled by the function\n\n$N(t) = 30 \\cdot 2^t$.\n\nHow many years will it take for the population of hedgehogs to increase to $7\\,680$?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147182 1072 #uuid "94d6edd2-52f9-581a-84f0-3003ccbaadd8" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147182 1074 "To find the time it takes for the number of hedgehogs to reach $7\\,680$, we need to solve the equation $N(t)=7\\,680$ for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(t) &= 7680 \\\\\n30 \\cdot 2^{t} &= 7680 \\\\\n2^{t} &= \\frac{7680}{30} \\\\\n2^{t} &= 256 \\\\\n2^{t} &= 2^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it will take $t=8$ years for the population of hedgehogs to increase to $7\\,680$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147183 1069 "e-14267" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147183 1071 "Bob invests $\\[math]250$ into some high-growth stocks, and the value of his investment doubles every year. How long will it take for Bob's investment to increase to $\\[/math]16\\,000$?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147183 1072 #uuid "4a2c8602-04d5-5ec7-846f-2647249994fe" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147183 1074 "We know that Bob's investment doubles every year. So the value of Bob's investment, which we'll call $y(t)$, grows exponentially with a growth factor of $2$.\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny(t) = ab^t\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=250$ is the initial investment (in dollars).\n- $b=2$ is the growth factor.\n- $t$ is the time, in years, since the investment was first made.\n\nTherefore, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t) = 250\\cdot 2^t\n$$\n\nNow, to find the time taken for the investment to reach $\\[math]16\\,000$, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\ny(t) = 16\\,000\n$$\n\nfor $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(t) &= 16000 \\\\\n250 \\cdot 2^{t} &= 16000 \\\\\n2^{t} &= \\frac{16000}{250} \\\\\n2^{t} &= 64 \\\\\n2^{t} &= 2^{6} \\\\\nt &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it will take $6$ years for Bob's investment to grow to $\\[/math]16\\,000$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147184 1069 "e-14271" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147184 1071 "Mary is conducting a scientific experiment with bacteria. She notices that the number of bacteria in a Petri dish grows by $15\\%$ each day. If there were initially $100$ bacteria in the dish, what equation does Mary need to solve to predict how long it will take for the number of bacteria to increase to $535$?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147184 1072 #uuid "9759efbf-fdb7-595c-822d-85c6b5f3549e" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147184 1074 "The number of bacteria $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $15\\%$.\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny = a(1+r)^t\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=100$ is the initial number of bacteria.\n- $r = 0.15$ is the growth rate.\n- $t$ is the time, in days, since the experiment is started.\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t) = 100\\cdot(1+0.15)^t = 100\\cdot (1.15)^t\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the time it will take for the number of bacteria to reach $535$, we would need to solve the equation $y(t)= 535$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n100 \\cdot (1.15)^{t} &= 535\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147185 1069 "e-14276" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147185 1071 "Some scientists are monitoring the growth of the wolf population in a particular nature reserve. The population of wolves $W(t)$ after $t$ decades of observations is given by\n\n$W(t) = W_{0} \\cdot 4^{t}$,\n\nwhere $W_{0}$ represents the wolves' population at the beginning of the study. If there were $3\\,200$ wolves after $3$ decades, how many wolves were there at the beginning of the study?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147185 1072 #uuid "07a30fba-ad4b-56a0-b6ce-21941d106b38" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147185 1074 "There are $3\\,200$ wolves $3$ decades after the scientists started studying the wolf population, so\n\n$$\nW(3) = 3\\,200\n$$\n\nTo find the initial number of wolves $W_0$, we substitute $t=3$ and\n\n$$\nW(3) = 3\\,200\n$$\n\ninto the given equation and solve for $W_0$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(3) &= W_{0} \\cdot 4^{3} \\\\\n3200 &= W_{0} \\cdot 64 \\\\\nW_{0} &= \\frac{3200}{64} \\\\\nW_{0} &= 50\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there were $50$ wolves at the beginning of the study." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147186 1069 "e-14461" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147186 1071 "Given that the mean of the data set below is $5$, what is the mean absolute deviation of the data?\n\n$9, \\: 2, \\: 3, \\: 6$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147186 1072 #uuid "a7afb36a-256c-5a76-b564-841d23f8604f" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147186 1074 "We are given that the mean is ${\\color{blue}5}$.\n\nSo, we subtract the mean from each number in the data set, find the absolute values of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}9 - 5 & + & 2 - 5 & + & 3 - 5 & + & 6 - 5\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}4 & + & -3 & + & -2 & + & 1\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n4 + 3 + 2 + 1 &=  \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation (MAD), we divide the sum obtained above (${\\color{red}10}$) by the total number of data points ($4$):\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}10}{4} = 2.5\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147187 1069 "e-14462" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147187 1071 "Find the mean absolute deviation of the data set below.\n$3, \\: 1, \\: 4, \\: 8$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147187 1072 #uuid "59f74f08-b299-503e-97c1-7a95f0287e79" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147187 1074 "First, we find the mean of the data:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\text{mean}} = \\dfrac{3 + 1 + 4 + 8}{4} = \\dfrac{16}{4} = {\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the mean from each number in the data set, find the absolute values of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}3 - 4 & + & 1 - 4 & + & 4 - 4 & + & 8 - 4\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & + & -3 & + & 0 & + & 4\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n1 + 3 + 0 + 4 &=  \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation (MAD), we divide the sum obtained above (${\\color{red}8}$) by the total number of data points ($4$):\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{4} = 2\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147188 1069 "e-14465" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147188 1071 "The total times of five racers during a Grand Prix were:\n$14 \\,\\text{minutes}, \\quad 16 \\,\\text{minutes}, \\quad 13 \\,\\text{minutes}, \\quad 21 \\,\\text{minutes}, \\quad 19 \\,\\text{minutes}$\nRounded to one decimal place, what is the mean absolute deviation of the times?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147188 1072 #uuid "ada2db5b-9257-5dfe-8322-5efe47c46457" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147188 1074 "First, we find the mean of the data:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\text{mean}} = \\dfrac{14 + 16 + 13 + 21 + 19}{5} = \\dfrac{83}{5} = {\\color{blue}16.6}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the mean from each number in the data set, find the absolute values of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}14 - 16.6 & + & 16 - 16.6 & + & 13 - 16.6 & + & 21 - 16.6 & + & 19 - 16.6\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}-2.6 & + & -0.6 & + & -3.6 & + & 4.4 & + & 2.4\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n2.6 + 0.6 + 3.6 + 4.4 + 2.4 &=  \\\\\n&= 13.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation (MAD), we divide the sum obtained above (${\\color{red}13.6}$) by the total number of data points ($5$):\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}13.6}{5} = 2.72\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest tenth gives:\n$2.72 \\rightarrow 2.7$\n\nTherefore, the mean absolute deviation of the times of the racers is $2.7$ minutes." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147189 1069 "e-14479" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147189 1071 "Evaluate $(0.5)^3$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147189 1072 #uuid "027603b5-e3f3-55a1-836b-d41fb555bfec" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147189 1074 "First, by writing the expression with multiplication signs, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(0.5)^{3} &= 0.5 \\cdot 0.5 \\cdot 0.5_(⏟)_([math]{\\color{blue}{3}}[/math] times)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute this product, we first ignore any decimal points and multiply the factors just as we would with whole numbers. We also ignore any leading zeros during the multiplication:\n\n$$\n3\n$$\n\nWe now count the number of decimal places in the $\\color{blue}3$ factors.\n\nThere is ${\\color{purple}{1}}$ decimal place in $0.5$.\nTherefore, the exponent $(0.5)^{\\color{blue}3}$ will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{1}}\\times {\\color{blue}{3}} = \\color{red}3\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $125$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{red}3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(0.5)^3 = 0.125\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147190 1069 "e-14480" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147190 1071 "Find the value of $(4.2)^2$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147190 1072 #uuid "597f5dfa-9e92-58c0-a3dd-f8205a382dc2" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147190 1074 "First, by writing the expression with multiplication signs, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4.2)^{2} &= 4.2 \\cdot 4.2_(⏟)_([math]{\\color{blue}{2}}[/math] times)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe proceed by multiplying the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; 4\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}2; 8\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}1\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 6\\end{vmatrix}4\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the $\\color{blue}2$ factors.\n\nThere is ${\\color{purple}{1}}$ decimal place in $4.2$. Therefore, the exponent $(4.2)^{\\color{blue}2}$ will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{1}}\\times {\\color{blue}{2}} = \\color{red}2\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $1764$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{red}2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(4.2)^2 = 17.64\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147191 1069 "e-14482" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147191 1071 "Calculate the value of $(3.01)^2$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147191 1072 #uuid "e798b10f-9001-5a30-a696-73523bc629bb" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147191 1074 "First, by writing the expression with multiplication signs, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3.01)^{2} &= 3.01 \\cdot 3.01_(⏟)_([math]{\\color{blue}{2}}[/math] times)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe proceed by multiplying the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math] \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}1; 3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ + & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ + & 9\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid 1\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the $\\color{blue}2$ factors.\n\nThere are ${\\color{purple}{2}}$ decimal places in $3.01$.\nTherefore, the exponent $(3.01)^{\\color{blue}2}$ will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{2}}\\times {\\color{blue}{2}} = \\color{red}4\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $90601$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{red}4$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(30.1)^2 = 9.0601\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147192 1069 "e-14704" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147192 1071 "What is $\\dfrac{11}{30}\\times \\dfrac{12}{7}$, expressed as a fraction in lowest terms?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147192 1072 #uuid "36b559ef-d696-580d-813f-c80fc9bc36b3" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147192 1074 "We need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{\\color{red}30}\\times \\dfrac{\\color{blue}12}{7}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($\\color{red}30$) and the second fraction's numerator ($\\color{blue}12$) have a common\nfactor of $6$.\n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by first swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{7} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{red}12}{\\color{blue}30}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the second fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $6$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{11}{7} \\cdot \\frac{12 \\div 6}{30 \\div 6} &= \\frac{11}{7} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{7}\\times \\dfrac{2}{5} = \\dfrac{11 \\times 2}{7 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{22}{35}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147193 1069 "e-15037" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147193 1071 "Calculate the value of $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^4$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147193 1072 #uuid "902a3945-f3e7-588c-83e3-9d74fcaacc62" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147193 1074 "We proceed in two steps:\n\n- First, we write $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^4$ with multiplication signs:\n$4$\n- Then, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n$(\\frac{3}{8})^{4}|= \\frac{3}{8} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8} \\cdot \\frac{3}{8}; = \\frac{3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3}{8 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 8}; = (3 \\cdot 3^(⏞)^(9) \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3^(⏞)^(9))/(8 \\cdot 8_(⏟)_(64) \\cdot 8 \\cdot 8_(⏟)_(64)); = \\frac{9 \\cdot 9}{64 \\cdot 64}; = \\frac{81}{4096}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right)^4= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{81}{4\\,096}}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147194 1069 "e-1789" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147194 1071 "What expression represents \"*$3$ more than the quotient of $12$ and $6$*\"?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147194 1072 #uuid "4eb3ffe0-edc1-5533-905a-065517d9a1a9" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147194 1074 "\"*The quotient of $12$ and $6$*\" means:\n\n$$\n12 \\div 6\n$$\n\nSince this operation comes first, we use parentheses:\n\n$$\n(12 \\div 6)\n$$\n\n\"*$3$ more than...*\" means that we should add $3$ to the above expression. So we get:\n\n$$\n(12 \\div 6) + 3\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147195 1069 "e-1840" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147195 1071 "Write the following statements as expressions:\n\n1. \"The product of $7$ and $11$.\"\n2. \"The quotient of $28$ and $4$.\"" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147195 1072 #uuid "e0368105-3aca-5724-8a2f-59731b482aa1" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147195 1074 "Remember that the word **product** refers to multiplication, and the word **quotient** refers to division.\n\n1. To find the product of two numbers, we should insert \"$\\times$\" between them. Therefore, \"the product of $7$ and $11$\" can be expressed as: $7\\times11$\n2. To find the quotient of two numbers, we should insert \"$\\div$\" between them without changing the order of the numbers. Therefore, \"the quotient of $28$ and $4$\" can be expressed as: $28 \\div 4$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147196 1069 "e-1841" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147196 1071 "Write the following statements as expressions:\n\n1. \"The sum of $5$ and $13$.\"\n2. \"The difference of $17$ and $9$.\"" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147196 1072 #uuid "eea0eb21-dab6-5eaf-9407-266618f7430a" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147196 1074 "Remember that the word **sum** refers to addition, and the word **difference** refers to subtraction.\n\n1. To find the **sum** of two numbers, we should insert \"$+$\" between them. Therefore, \"the sum of $5$ and $13$\" can be expressed as: $5+13$\n2. To find the **difference** of two numbers, we should insert \"$-$\" between them without changing the order of the numbers. Therefore, \"the difference of $17$ and $9$\" can be expressed as: $17-9$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147197 1069 "e-18934" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147197 1071 "A fan recorded the total number of goals scored by a Spanish and an English soccer team in the last $5$ rounds of the Champions League. The range in the number of goals scored per round by the Spanish team was $4$. The number of goals scored in each round by the English team is shown below:\n\n$4, \\, \\, \\, 6, \\, \\, \\, 2, \\, \\, \\, 8, \\, \\, \\, 10$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The range in the number of goals scored per round by the English team is $6$.\n2. The range for the English team is greater than that of the Spanish team.\n3. A comparison of the ranges suggests that the Spanish team is less consistent (in terms of goals scored per round) than the English team." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147197 1072 #uuid "33be30a3-f9fc-5347-a69d-3994c8086741" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147197 1074 "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Arranging the data for the English team from smallest to greatest, we get the following:\n${\\color{red}\\underline{2}}, \\quad 4, \\quad 6, \\quad 8, \\quad {\\color{blue}\\underline{10}}$\nHere, the smallest value is $\\color{red}2$ and the greatest value is $\\color{blue}10$. So, the range is\n${\\color{blue}10} - {\\color{red}2} = 8\\, \\text{goals}$.\n- Statement II is true. Since $8 > 4$, the range for the English team is greater than that of the Spanish team.\n- Statement III is false. The range is greater for the English team, indicating more variability in the data compared to the Spanish team. Therefore, there is no suggestion that the Spanish team is less consistent regarding goals scored per round.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147198 1069 "e-18935" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147198 1071 "The number of *coconut* ice creams sold per day over a particular eight-day period at Tom's shop is given below:\n\n$22 \\,, \\quad 35 \\,, \\quad 43 \\,, \\quad 52 \\,, \\quad 58\\,, \\quad 65 \\,, \\quad 68\\,, \\quad 72$\n\nThe interquartile range (IQR) of the number of *strawberry* ice creams sold per day over the same eight-day period is $30$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The interquartile range (IQR) of the number of coconut ice creams sold per day is $27.5$\n2. The IQR for strawberry ice creams is greater than for coconut ice creams.\n3. A comparison of the IQRs suggests it is more difficult for Tom to predict how many strawberry ice creams he'll sell tomorrow compared to coconut ice creams." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147198 1072 #uuid "5e7bbd38-ff9f-5548-8bde-97e5741a50e7" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147198 1074 "Let's compute the interquartile range for the number of coconut ice creams sold.\n\nNotice that our data set is already arranged from smallest to greatest, and that\nthe number of data points is even ($8$).\n\n- The lower part is the first half of the data:\n$\\underbrace{22, \\: 35, \\: 43, \\: 52,}_{\\text{lower half}} \\: {58, \\: 65, \\: 68, \\:72}$\nNow, we find the median of the lower part. In this case, it's the mean of the two middle numbers:\n$22, \\: {\\color{red}\\underline{35}}, \\: {\\color{red}\\underline{43}}, \\: 52$\nSo, the lower quartile is\n$\\dfrac{{\\color{red}35}+{\\color{red}43}}{2}=\\dfrac{78}{2}=39$.\n\n- The upper part is the second half of the data:\n${22, \\: 35, \\: 43, \\: 52,} \\: \\underbrace{58, \\: 65, \\: 68, \\:72}_{\\text{upper half}}$\nNow, we find the median of the upper part. In this case, it's the mean of the two middle numbers:\n$58, \\: {\\color{red}\\underline{65}}, \\: {\\color{red}\\underline{68}}, \\: 72$\nSo, the upper quartile is\n$\\dfrac{{\\color{red}65}+{\\color{red}68}}{2}=\\dfrac{133}{2}=66.5$.\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range of the data set is\n\n$$\n\\text{upper quartile} - \\text{lower quartile} = 66.5 - 39= 27.5\n$$\n\nSo, the interquartile range of the number of coconut ice creams sold per day is $27.5$\n\nLet's now examine the statements one by one:\n\n- Statement I is true.\n- Statement II is true. Since $30 > 27.5$, the interquartile range is larger for the strawberry ice creams\n- Statement III is true. The IQR is greater for strawberry ice creams, indicating more variability in the data than for coconut ice creams, suggesting that it's more difficult for Tom to predict the number of strawberry ice creams he'll sell tomorrow.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147199 1069 "e-18940" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147199 1071 "![](images/7f/7f84e1890cf6e29704538e608640b6c0798caa060358ecb92a3dfe3ffe868d57.png)\n\nA fitness center tracked the number of miles each runner completed during two different weeks. This information is displayed using the box plots shown above.\n\nCalculate the range for each dataset and identify which dataset has the greater spread." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147199 1072 #uuid "25c1e9dd-b325-5284-a7e2-29f82bcc2c22" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147199 1074 "Recall that on a box plot,\n\n- the smallest value in the data corresponds to the left whisker, and\n- the greatest value corresponds to the right whisker.\n\nThe range of a dataset is the difference between the greatest and the smallest values.\n\nWith that in mind, let's find the ranges of the given datasets.\n\n- Consider the box plot corresponding to the first week.![](images/12/12a2d26dfb33ddaae846ca332f5a684dca70720af005543aedc7e501192890e8.png)\nHere, we have the following:\n$\\text{Smallest} = 2 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\text{Greatest} = 11$\nSo, the range of the dataset is\n$Range|= Greatest - Smallest; = 11 - 2; = 9$.\n- Consider the box plot corresponding to the second week.![](images/01/01d1ab0ff1e0aa7533e1dd0b7af5efaae56f0034b20c075e6d8c7e9367beb0c6.png)\nHere, we have the following:\n$\\text{Smallest} = 1 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\text{Greatest} = 15$\nSo, the range of the dataset is\n$Range|= Greatest - Smallest; = 15 - 1; = 14$.\n\nSince $9 < 14$, a comparison of the ranges suggests that the dataset corresponding to the second week is more spread out." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147200 1069 "e-18941" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147200 1071 "![](images/40/409018f85f3471f6daff156df3d393eedd891a5d9a5042549d3a22649800bc8e.png)\n\nA chef recorded the number of cupcakes baked by two different assistants in a single month. This information is displayed using the dot plots shown above.\n\nCompare the interquartile ranges of the two data sets." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147200 1072 #uuid "64efdab7-d0dd-5e4f-9cad-3e2bcf3a92ba" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147200 1074 "The interquartile range (IQR) of a dataset is the difference between the upper and the lower quartiles.\n\nWith that in mind, let's find the interquartile ranges of the given datasets.\n\n- Consider the dot plot corresponding to the first assistant.Notice that the number of data points ($15$) is odd. As a result, we can separate the data into its lower and upper parts, as illustrated in the diagram below.![](images/e0/e0966bd1467619b0235b410adf7bed82b6ea862cd128242b6f584dedf1c6e192.png)\nHere, we have the following:The lower quartile is the median of the lower part. In this case, it's just the middle number. So, the lower quartile is\n$\\text{Lower Quartile} = 21$.The upper quartile is the median of the upper part. In this case, it's just the middle number. So, the upper quartile is\n$\\text{Upper Quartile} = 23$.Thus, the interquartile range is\n$IQR|= Upper Quartile - Lower Quartile; = 23 - 21; = 2$.\n- Consider the dot plot corresponding to the second assistant.Notice that the number of data points ($12$) is even. As a result, we can separate the data into its lower and upper parts, as illustrated in the diagram below.![](images/cf/cf9f14b1ec079ae949177dec31c679a10a8dc44c07dc4e6309d5d5065df1871f.png)\nHere, we have the following:The lower quartile is the median of the lower part. In this case, it's the average of the two middle numbers. So, the lower quartile is\n$\\text{Lower Quartile} = \\dfrac{21+22}{2} = \\dfrac{43}{2} = 21.5$.The upper quartile is the median of the upper part. In this case, it's the average of the two middle numbers. So, the upper quartile is\n$\\text{Upper Quartile} = \\dfrac{23+23}{2} = \\dfrac{46}{2} = 23$.Thus, the interquartile range is\n$IQR|= Upper Quartile - Lower Quartile; = 23 - 21.5; = 1.5$.\n\nSince $2 > 1.5$, a comparison of the interquartile ranges suggests that the dataset corresponding to the first assistant is more spread out." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147201 1069 "e-19157" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147201 1071 "![](images/64/643218e2f36a84e765349ac39910fa63d4e60e6b5683f56ed1aa879dc19af0cd.png)\n\nFind the mean and median of the dataset shown above." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147201 1072 #uuid "1e791dc2-0dab-500e-b736-286525a0e40f" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147201 1074 "Recall that when a distribution is perfectly symmetric,\n\n- the mean and the median are equal, and\n- the mean (and median) lie at the midpoint of the distribution.\n\nNotice that this dataset is symmetric.\n\nTo calculate the mean (midpoint) of this distribution, we find the mean of the two most extreme values. The largest value is $55$, and the smallest value is $20$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{mean} = \\dfrac{55+20}{2} = \\dfrac{75}{2} = 37.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is $37.5$ and the median is $37.5$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147202 1069 "e-1964" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147202 1071 "What inequality corresponds to the red colored graph shown in the number line below?\n\n![](images/39/399512ad3596d2d055849d631cfbe45859a74de5e5604fb0da55aadc62a421c9.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147202 1072 #uuid "860804a9-8539-5d19-84f0-28e18f376223" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147202 1074 "The red arrow indicates that $x$ should consist of all the values greater than $2$. In addition, the filled red circle at $2$ implies that $x$ can be equal to $2$.\n\nTherefore, the highlighted region represents the numbers that are greater than or equal to $2$. This corresponds the inequality\n\n$$\nx \\geq 2\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147203 1069 "e-1965" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147203 1071 "Represent $x < -1$ on a number line." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147203 1072 #uuid "b03ae30a-c7f1-5192-8f4f-52bd2aeebed7" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147203 1074 "The symbol \"$<$\" means \"less than.\" So, the inequality $x < -1$ represents the numbers that are less than $-1$.\n\nOn a number line, the numbers less than $-1$ are the numbers that are left of $-1$. So, we should draw an arrow going to the left of $-1$.\n\nHowever, the number $-1$ itself is not less than $-1$, so we should not include it in our representation. To exclude $-1$, we draw an empty circle around $-1$.\n\n![](images/57/57499770d310c5f0912e62ec160accd878a1c169c22ce894a48102a39769d209.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147204 1069 "e-1987" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147204 1071 "A computer has $10$ files infected with a computer virus. If the number of infected files quadruples every hour, find a function that gives the number of infected files after $t$ hours. How many infected files will there be after $6$ hours?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147204 1072 #uuid "7e261e75-a3ec-59cf-a7b7-ad935cb4e55d" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147204 1074 "The number of infected files, $y(t)$, grows exponentially with a growth factor of $4$.\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 10$ is the initial number of infected files\n- $b = 4$ is the growth factor (the number of infected files quadruples)\n- $t=$ the time (in hours)\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny = ab^t\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)= 10 \\cdot 4^{t}\n$$\n\nTo find the number of files infected after $6$ hours, we substitute $t=6$ into the above:\n\n$$\ny(6) = 10 \\cdot 4^{6} = 40\\,960\n$$\n\nTherefore, the computer will have $40\\,960$ infected files after $6$ hours." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147205 1069 "e-1988" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147205 1071 "In the week of opening, the grocery store sold $20$ kilograms of apples. After that, the weekly sales increased by $25 \\%$ every week.\n\nFind a function that models the total weight of apples sold in week $t$ after opening, and use this function to find the total amount of apples sold in the $4$th week." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147205 1072 #uuid "d2d38b6b-8626-5605-835d-86370525d292" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147205 1074 "The number of kilograms of apples sold $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $25 \\%$.\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 20$ is the total weight of the apples (in kilograms) sold initially\n- $r = 0.25$ is the growth rate ($25 \\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n- $t=$ the time (in weeks) after opening\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny = a(1+r)^t\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny = 20 \\cdot (1 + 0.25)^{t}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\ny(t) = 20 \\cdot (1.25)^{t}\n$$\n\nTo find the total weight of apples sold in week $4$, we evaluate the above for $t=4$:\n\n$$\ny(4) = 20 \\cdot (1.25)^{4} \\approx 49\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $49$ kilograms of apples were sold in week $4$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147206 1069 "e-1990" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147206 1071 "The altitude, in meters, of a hot-air balloon is modeled by the function $A(t)=12\\cdot 2^t$, where $t$ represents the number of minutes since the balloon took off from a platform.\n\n1. What does $A(3)$ mean in this context?\n2. How high does the balloon climb between the third minute and the sixth minute of its flight?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147206 1072 #uuid "1bec8657-1367-54e6-a6e3-942d1dcbe76a" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147206 1074 "To answer question I, the value $A(3)$ represents the value of the function $A(t)$ at $t=3$. In this context, it represents the altitude of the balloon $3$ minutes after taking off from the platform.\n\nQuestion II asks for the total height that the balloon climbed between $t=3$ and $t=6$ minutes. To calculate this, we find the balloon's altitude after $6$ minutes, and we subtract its altitude after $3$ minutes. So we need to work out\n$A(6) - A(3)$.\n\nFirst, let's work out $A(6){:}$\n\n$$\nA(6) = 12\\cdot 2^6 = 768\n$$\n\nNext, we work out $A(3){:}$\n\n$$\nA(3) = 12\\cdot 2^3 = 96\n$$\n\nTherefore, the balloon's altitude increased by\n\n$$\n768 - 96 = 672\n$$\n\nmeters between the third and the sixth minute." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147207 1069 "e-1991" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147207 1071 "At the beginning of the year $2000$, only $300$ people in a particular town had a cell phone. Since then, the number of cellphone subscribers doubled every year. What does the expression $300\\cdot 2^5$ mean in this context?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147207 1072 #uuid "af8404c1-6ca6-5d05-89c9-80de4c0e0fda" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147207 1074 "The given expression is an exponential growth expression of the form\n\n$$\ny(t) = a \\cdot b^t\n$$\n\nwhere we have the following:\n\n- $a=300$ represents the initial number of cellphone subscribers.\n- $b = 2$ is the growth factor. This tells us that the number of subscribers doubles every year.\n- $t = 5$ is the time, in years.\n\nSince $t=5$, the expression $300\\cdot 2^5$ gives the total number of cell phone subscribers after $5$ years." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147208 1069 "e-1992" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147208 1071 "A chemical reaction starts with $250$ molecules in a reaction chamber, and this number increases exponentially. If after $5$ hours there are $250\\cdot 2^5$ molecules in the chamber, what does the factor $2^5$ mean in this context?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147208 1072 #uuid "d3ba93df-8786-58c5-84cf-6427d5642459" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147208 1074 "The given expression $250\\cdot 2^5$ is an exponential growth expression of the form\n\n$$\ny(t) = ab^t\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n- $a=250$ represents the initial number of molecules,\n- $b = 2$ is the growth factor, and\n- $t = 5$ is the time since the start of the observations, in hours.\n\nFor the factor $2^5$, the growth rate $(2)$ means that the number of molecules doubles every hour, and the exponent $(5)$ indicates the number of hours.\n\nTherefore, $2^5 = 32$ means that the number of molecules has increased by a factor of $32$ in five hours." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147209 1069 "e-2022" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147209 1071 "Solve $2^y = \\sqrt[3]{2}$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147209 1072 #uuid "ca042188-0d44-5e60-9f2f-35014634ae9f" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147209 1074 "The fractional exponent rule tells us that the radical on the right-hand side can be rewritten using a fractional power, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{2} = 2^{1/3}\n$$\n\nSo, we can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n2^y = 2^{1/3}\n$$\n\nNow that both sides have a common base of $2$, we can equate the powers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^{y} &= 2^{1/3} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147210 1069 "e-2023" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147210 1071 "Find the solution of the equation $4^x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{4}}$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147210 1072 #uuid "4a2eaf92-015a-5837-bd97-7ac1f50e90e2" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147210 1074 "First, we rewrite the radical in the denominator using the fractional power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4^{x} &= \\frac{1}{4^{1/3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we write the expression on the right-hand side using a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4^{x} &= 4^{-1/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that both sides of the equation have the same base $(4)$, we can equate the powers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4^{x} &= 4^{-1/3} \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147211 1069 "e-2024" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147211 1071 "If $5^y = \\sqrt[3]{25}$, find the value of $y$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147211 1072 #uuid "200f97d2-8f56-5e7b-9a1a-263e0cd1ae1c" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147211 1074 "Since the number under the radical is $25$ and the base of the variable exponent expression is ${\\color{blue}5}$, we begin by rewriting $25$ as ${\\color{blue}5}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5^{y} &= \\sqrt[3]{25} \\\\\n5^{y} &= \\sqrt[3]{5^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the radical using the fractional exponent rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5^{y} &= (5^{2})^{1/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5^{y} &= 5^{2 \\cdot 1/3} \\\\\n5^{y} &= 5^{2/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that both sides of the equation share a common base, we can equate the exponents. This gives the solution\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147212 1069 "e-2025" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147212 1071 "What is the solution to $6^x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{36}}$?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147212 1072 #uuid "1260080f-0f34-5d27-99e2-44134ef53d26" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147212 1074 "Since the number under the radical is $36$ and the base of the variable exponent expression is ${\\color{blue}6}$, we begin by rewriting $36$ as ${\\color{blue}6}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6^{x} &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{6^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the radical using the fractional exponent rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6^{x} &= (1)/((6^{2})^{1/4})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6^{x} &= \\frac{1}{6^{2 \\cdot 1/4}} \\\\\n6^{x} &= \\frac{1}{6^{1/2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n6^x =6^{-1/2}\n$$\n\nNow that both sides of the equation share a common base, we can equate the exponents. This gives the solution\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147213 1069 "e-2068" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147213 1071 "The number of members $m$ of a club after $t$ years is given by the formula\n\n$m(t) = 766 \\cdot (\\textrm{___})^{t}$.\n\nIf there were $766$ members initially, and the number of members decreases by $22\\%$ each year, what is the missing value in the expression for $m(t)$?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147213 1072 #uuid "34889441-479d-59e6-b9c6-a3972ff9ea60" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147213 1074 "The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nm(t) = a(1-r)^t\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=766$ is the initial number of members.\n- $r = 0.22$ is the decay rate ($22\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $m$ is\n\n$$\nm(t)= 766\\cdot (1 - 0.22)^{t} = 766\\cdot (0.78)^{t}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $0.78$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147214 1069 "e-2070" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147214 1071 "A mountain has a glacier with a depth of $75\\,\\textrm{m}$. The depth of the glacier reduces by $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ every year. Construct a function that represents the depth $d(t)$ of the glacier after $t$ years." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147214 1072 #uuid "06d40324-f901-5e87-93c1-318a819eb635" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147214 1074 "The depth of the glacier is $75\\,\\textrm{m}$, and this reduces by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nevery year. Therefore, the depth decays exponentially with a decay factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 4\n$$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay factor $b$) is\n\n$$\nd(t) = ab^t\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=75 \\textrm{m}$ is the initial depth of the glacier.\n- $b = \\dfrac 3 4$ is the decay factor.\n- $t =$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for the depth $d(t)$ is\n\n$$\nd(t)= 75 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac 3 4\\right)^{t}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147215 1069 "e-2074" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147215 1071 "The value of a piece of jewelry, in dollars, is given by the expression $600 \\cdot (1.2)^{t}$, where $t$ is the number of years since the piece was purchased. What is the growth rate of the piece?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147215 1072 #uuid "768a334f-5b8f-5ff3-bf08-1230fe3674b9" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147215 1074 "We're asked for the growth rate, and so we use the growth rate formula.\n\nThe given expression is an exponential growth expression of the form\n\n$$\ny(t) = a \\cdot (1+r)^t\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n- $a=600$ represents the initial value of the jewelry, in dollars\n- $1+r = 1.2$, where $r$ is the growth rate\n- $t =$ the time since the piece was purchased, in years\n\nSolving for $r$, we get\n\n$$\n1+r = 1.2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad r = 0.2\n$$\n\nNow, $0.2$ (expressed as a percent) is equal to $20\\%$. Therefore, the value of the jewelry grows by $20\\%$ per year." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147216 1069 "e-20894" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147216 1071 "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$-\\dfrac{y}{x^2} \\textrm{d}x + Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $Q(x,y)$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{1}{x}$\n2. $\\dfrac{y}{x}$\n3. $e^{yx}$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147216 1072 #uuid "39259a31-5d67-5482-b1bb-4fa719ce4f95" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147216 1074 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = -\\dfrac{y}{x^2}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} =\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(-\\dfrac{y}{x^2}\\right)=-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nto each of the given options and determine whether the results match up with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\n$$\n\nabove:\n\n- If $Q(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$, then we get an exact equation:\n$\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\dfrac 1 x\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2} = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- If $Q(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$, then we do *not* get an exact equation:\n$\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\dfrac y x\\right) = -\\dfrac{y}{x^2} \\neq \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- If $Q(x,y) = e^{xy}$, then we do *not* get an exact equation:\n$\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(e^{xy}\\right) = ye^{xy} \\neq \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147217 1069 "e-20895" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147217 1071 "Find the general solution of the exact differential equation\n\n$(y^2-2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (2xy +1)\\textrm{d}y = 0$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147217 1072 #uuid "ca703f4f-5363-5fc1-8372-136e7c87156d" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147217 1074 "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = y^2-2x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y) = 2xy+1\n$$\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = P(x,y),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= \\intP(x, y)dx + f(y) \\\\\n&= \\inty^{2} - 2xdx + f(y) \\\\\n&= xy^{2} - x^{2} + f(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= \\intQ(x, y)dy + g(x) \\\\\n&= \\int2xy + 1dy + g(x) \\\\\n&= xy^{2} + y + g(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x) = -x^2, \\qquad f(y) = y\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y) = c$ is\n\n$$\nxy^2-x^2 + y = c\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147218 1069 "e-20922" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147218 1071 "At a cooking competition, the mean score received by each of the $40$ contestants was $82$. What was the total of all the scores given in the competition?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147218 1072 #uuid "6e431a0f-e853-5bae-ae34-71a6472b4c1d" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147218 1074 "The total of all the scores is the sum of all the scores received by the contestants.\n\nThe sum of the values in a dataset is the product of the mean and the number of data points:\n\n$$\n\\text{sum} = \\text{mean} \\times \\text{number of data points}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the mean is $82$ (points) and there are $40$ data points (contestants). Hence, the sum is\n\n$$\n\\text{sum} = 82 \\cdot 40 = 3,280\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total of all the scores given in the competition is $3,280$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147219 1069 "e-20933" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147219 1071 "Which of the following differential equations is exact?\n\n1. $(2xy)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (x^{2}+4)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$\n2. $(y^{2}+2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (xy+1)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$\n3. $(7y - x)\\,\\textrm{d}y - (y - 3x)\\,\\textrm{d}x = 0$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147219 1072 #uuid "36440ae0-1cee-52c0-b24a-7871e172a341" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147219 1074 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation:\n\n- The first equation is\n$(2xy)\\;\\textrm{d}x + (x^{2}+4)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0$.\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n$P(x,y) = 2xy, \\qquad Q(x,y) = x^{2}+4$.\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy); = 2x$\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + 4); = 2x$\nSo,\n$\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\nThus, equation I is exact.\n- The second equation is\n$(y^{2}+2x)\\;\\textrm{d}x + (xy+1)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0$.\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n$P(x,y) = y^{2}+2x, \\qquad Q(x,y) = xy+1$.\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y^{2} + 2x); = 2y$\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy + 1); = y$\nSo,\n$\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {\\neq} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\nThus, equation II is not exact.\n- The third equation is\n$(7y - x)\\;\\textrm{d}y - (y - 3x)\\;\\textrm{d}x = 0$.\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n$P(x,y) = -(y - 3x) = 3x - y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = 7y - x$.\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x - y); =-1$\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(7y - x); =-1$\nSo,\n$\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\nThus, equation III is exact.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147220 1069 "e-20934" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147220 1071 "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n$y' = \\frac{11+ye^{xy}}{13y - xe^{xy}}$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147220 1072 #uuid "c1b768cb-8709-55f0-878b-d9608ac1c478" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147220 1074 "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nStarting from\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= \\frac{dy}{dx} \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{11 + ye^{xy}}{13y - xe^{xy}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(13y-xe^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(13y-xe^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}y = (11+ye^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we then rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(11+ye^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x + (xe^{xy}-13y)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =11+ye^{xy}, \\qquad Q(x,y) = xe^{xy}-13y\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(11 + ye^{xy}) \\\\\n&= e^{xy} + xye^{xy}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xe^{xy} - 13y) \\\\\n&= e^{xy} + xye^{xy}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = P(x,y),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= \\intP(x, y)dx + f(y) \\\\\n&= \\int(11 + ye^{xy})dx + f(y) \\\\\n&= 11x + e^{xy} + f(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= \\intQ(x, y)dy + g(x) \\\\\n&= \\int(xe^{xy} - 13y)dy + g(x) \\\\\n&= e^{xy} - \\frac{13}{2}y^{2} + g(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x) = 11x, \\quad f(y) =-\\dfrac{13}{2}y^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y) = c$ is\n\n$$\ne^{xy} - \\dfrac{13}{2}y^2 + 11x = c\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147221 1069 "e-21183" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147221 1071 "A podcast was released with $80$ initial listeners. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each week after release, the number of listeners increased by $190\\%$ of the number of listeners at the end of the previous week.\n\nAccording to this model, how many listeners are there estimated to be after $2$ weeks?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147221 1072 #uuid "914dd92e-18b8-5583-8326-f534d1a112f9" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147221 1074 "The number of listeners $L(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $190\\%$.\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a = 80$ is the total number of listeners initially\n- $r = 1.9$ is the growth rate ($190\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n- $t=$ the time (in weeks) after release\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nL = a(1+r)^t\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $L(t)$ is\n\n$$\nL = 80 \\cdot (1 + 1.9)^{t}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nL(t) = 80 \\cdot (2.9)^{t}\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of listeners after $2$ weeks, we evaluate the above for $t=2{:}$\n\n$$\nL(2) = 80 \\cdot (2.9)^{2} \\approx 673\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of listeners after $2$ weeks is $673$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147222 1069 "e-2141" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147222 1071 "The number of participants of a webinar reduces by $\\dfrac 1 8$ every hour. If the webinar started with $900$ participants, find an equation that determines how long it will take for the number of participants to reduce to $140$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147222 1072 #uuid "84d9a18f-bfe6-587e-9c75-fec532d9f541" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147222 1074 "Since the number of participants reduces by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nevery hour, this means that every hour,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{8}\n$$\n\nof the previous hour's participants remain. So the decay factor is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 7 8\n$$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny = ab^t\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=900$ is the initial number of participants.\n- $b = \\dfrac 7 8$ is the decay factor.\n- $t=$ the time, in hours, since the webinar started.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)= 900\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac 7 8\\right)^{t}\n$$\n\nSince we want to know how long it takes for the number of participants to decrease to $140$, we need to solve $y(t)=140$. This gives\n\n$$\n900\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{7}{8}\\right)^t = 140\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147223 1069 "e-2145" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147223 1071 "A car was purchased for $\\[math]13\\,000$. It's known that the value of this particular model depreciates at a rate of $20\\%$ per year. Find an equation that determines the number of years it takes for the car's value to reduce to $\\[/math]8\\, 320$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147223 1072 #uuid "2d96379c-d311-515a-addc-c04061b7e640" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147223 1074 "The value of the car $y(t)$ decreases exponentially with a decay rate of $20\\%$.\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny(t) = a(1-r)^t\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=13\\,000$ is the purchase price, in dollars.\n- $r = 0.2$ is the decay rate ($20\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n- $t=$ the time, in years, since the vehicle was purchased.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)= 13\\, 000 \\cdot (1-0.2)^t = 13\\, 000 \\cdot (0.8)^t\n$$\n\nSince we want to know how long it takes for the car's value to decrease to $\\[math]8\\,320$, we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\ny(t) =8\\,320\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n13\\, 000 \\cdot (0.8)^t = 8\\,320\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147224 1069 "e-2146" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147224 1071 "Due to server migration, the occupied disk space $S(t)$ (measured in $\\textrm{GB}$) of a web server reduces exponentially according to the function\n\n$S(t)= S_0\\left(0.8\\right)^{t}$,\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in hours, since the migration began, and $S_0$ is the amount of disk space that the server occupied initially. If the server occupied $55 \\textrm{GB}$ of storage twelve hours after the migration began, what was the initial amount of disk space that the server used?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147224 1072 #uuid "d8f26c05-d55e-5975-9adb-84c85a47f141" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147224 1074 "We know that $S(12) = 55$, so we substitute this into the formula for $S(t)$. This gives\n\n$$\n55 =S_0\\left(0.8\\right)^{12}\n$$\n\nWe now solve for $S_0$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(55)/((0.8)^{12}) &= (S_{0}(0.8)^{12})/((0.8)^{12}) \\\\\n(55)/((0.8)^{12}) &= (S_{0}(0.8)^{12})/((0.8)^{12}) \\\\\n(55)/((0.8)^{12}) &= S_{0} \\\\\nS_{0} &= (55)/((0.8)^{12})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this expression on a calculator, we get\n\n$$\nS_0\\approx 800\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the initial disk usage of the server was around $800\\,\\textrm{GB}$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147225 1069 "e-21598" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147225 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = 2$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147225 1072 #uuid "40068330-4d79-501b-afbc-a96f8e210c37" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147225 1074 "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty)$, the Laplace transform of $f$, denoted $\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ or $F(s)$, is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\} = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite.\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nf(t)=2\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\int_{0}^{∞}2e^{-st}dt\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= \\int_{0}^{∞}2e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= 2lim_(a → ∞)\\int_{0}^{a}e^{-st}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether\n\n$$\ns \\neq 0\n$$\n\nor $s = 0$.\n\n- If $s \\neq 0$, we have\n$F(s)|= 2lim_(a → ∞)\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{a}e^{-st}dt; = \\left.2lim_(a → ∞)(-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-st})\\right|_{0}^{a}; =-\\frac{2}{s}(lim_(a → ∞)e^{-sa} - 1)$.\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n$lim_(a → ∞)e^{-sa} = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}s > 0, \\\\ ∞,\\end{vmatrix}s < 0$.\n- If $s = 0$, we have\n$F(0)|= 2lim_(a → ∞)\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{a}e^{-0t}dt; = 2lim_(a → ∞)\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{a}dt; = \\left.2lim_(a → ∞)t\\right|_{0}^{a}; = 2lim_(a → ∞)(a - 0); = 2lim_(a → ∞)a; = ∞$.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s = 0$, we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is divergent\n- For $s > 0$, we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is convergent\n- For $s < 0$, we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is divergent\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s > 0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= -\\frac{2}{s}(lim_(a → ∞)e^{-sa} - 1) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{s}(0 - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\nf(t)=2\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nF(s) =\\dfrac{2}{s}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s > 0$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147226 1069 "e-21599" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147226 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{-2t}$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147226 1072 #uuid "54b1f55e-6525-557c-9dbc-869264259455" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147226 1074 "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty)$, the Laplace transform of $f$, denoted $\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ or $F(s)$, is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\} = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite.\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nf(t)=e^{-2t}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st} \\cdot e^{-2t}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st - 2t}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{(-2 - s)t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{(-2 - s)t}dt \\\\\n&= lim_(a → ∞)\\int_{0}^{a}e^{(-2 - s)t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether\n\n$$\ns \\neq -2\n$$\n\nor $s = -2$.\n\n- If $s \\neq -2$, we have\n$F(s)|= lim_(a → ∞)\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{a}e^{(-2 - s)t}dt; = \\left.lim_(a → ∞)(\\frac{1}{-2 - s}e^{(-2 - s)t})\\right|_{0}^{a}; = \\frac{1}{-2 - s}(lim_(a → ∞)e^{(-2 - s)a} - 1); = \\frac{1}{s + 2}(1 - lim_(a → ∞)e^{(-2 - s)a}); = \\frac{1}{s + 2} - \\frac{1}{s + 2} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)e^{(-2 - s)a}$\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n$lim_(a → ∞)e^{(-2 - s)a} = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}s > - 2, \\\\ ∞,\\end{vmatrix}s < - 2$.\n- If $s = -2$, we have\n$F(-2)|= lim_(a → ∞)\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{a}e^{(-2 - (-2))t}dt; = lim_(a → ∞)\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{a}e^{0t}dt; = lim_(a → ∞)\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{a}dt; = \\left.lim_(a → ∞)t\\right|_{0}^{a}; = lim_(a → ∞)(a - 0); = lim_(a → ∞)a; = ∞$.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s \\leq -2$ the integral is divergent.\n- For $s > -2$ the integral is convergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s > -2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= \\frac{1}{s + 2} - \\frac{1}{s + 2} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)e^{(-2 - s)a} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s + 2} - \\frac{1}{s + 2} \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s + 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\nf(t)=e^{-2t}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nF(s) =\\dfrac{1}{s+2}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s > -2$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147227 1069 "e-21602" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147227 1071 "What is the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{2\\textrm i t}$, where $t, s \\in\\mathbb R$?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147227 1072 #uuid "92327ab7-0ae5-54ce-92d1-a3365407dd74" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147227 1074 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f(t)} &= F(s) = \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}f(t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nf(t)=e^{2\\textrm i t}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}e^{2\\textrm{i}t}dt = \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{(2\\textrm{i} - s)t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= \\int_{0}^{∞}e^{(2\\textrm{i} - s)t}dt \\\\\n&= lim_(a → ∞)\\int_{0}^{a}e^{(2\\textrm{i} - s)t}dt \\\\\n&= \\left.lim_(a → ∞)(\\frac{1}{2\\textrm{i} - s}e^{(2\\textrm{i} - s)t})\\right|_{0}^{a} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2\\textrm{i} - s}(lim_(a → ∞)e^{(2\\textrm{i} - s)a} - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}}(1 - lim_(a → ∞)e^{(2\\textrm{i} - s)a}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}} - \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}}(lim_(a → ∞)e^{-sa} \\cdot e^{2\\textrm{i}a})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall Euler's formula:\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm i\\theta} = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm i \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)= \\dfrac{1}{s-2\\textrm i} - \\dfrac{1}{s-2\\textrm i}\\left[\\lim_{a\\to \\infty}e^{-sa}\\cdot \\left(\\cos(2a) + \\textrm i\\sin(2a)\\right) \\right]\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s < 0$, the term $e^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\cos(2a) + \\textrm i\\sin(2a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to\\infty$. Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s < 0$.\n- For $s = 0$, we have simply $\\cos(2a) + \\textrm i\\sin(2a)$, which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to\\infty$. Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s = 0$.\n- For $s > 0$, the term $e^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\cos(2a) + \\textrm i\\sin(2a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to\\infty$. Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s > 0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}} - \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}}[lim_(a → ∞)e^{-sa} \\cdot (\\cos (2a) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (2a))] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}} - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $e^{2\\textrm i t}$ is\n\n$$\nF(s) =\\dfrac{1}{s-2\\textrm i}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s>0$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147228 1069 "e-2161" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147228 1071 "Which of the following is **not** a polygon?\n\n![](images/55/5517adb9f80d1fb1a0423ca1c59ebd2bda45cf71c6e48ab9b7cb4648b2830e7d.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147228 1072 #uuid "522fe123-9393-57f8-bb3d-3076500c12bc" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147228 1074 "A polygon is any shape where all the sides are straight.\n\nThe shape below has curved sides.\n\n![](images/b9/b9a7f6f87555bf3c1e07409e1f68572bca76f64e1bdd2aa637e61be01796cd62.png)\n\nTherefore, III is not a polygon." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147229 1069 "e-2162" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147229 1071 "How many edges does the polygon shown below have?\n\n![](images/75/75c3f4c414e7fc1bb21a109d6dffe379878e3552b0d36b79782cec116bdf16d0.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147229 1072 #uuid "789fecc5-ee14-5725-8584-7409a67a27a7" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147229 1074 "Edges are straight line segments that connect the vertices of a polygon.\n\nThe polygon has $7$ edges in total, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c8/c84ef850b260fc59e50d748aaa3e5f446342220fcbd4f35528659550acdacbf6.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147230 1069 "e-2163" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147230 1071 "What is the name of the polygon shown below?\n\n![](images/96/969c0c69217cefc0411f5bfaaf1c8b3f8cf3d9e02126bfca190f6876b918e418.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147230 1072 #uuid "3e7d88d1-e303-5666-bcf7-d8baaea377ef" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147230 1074 "The polygon has $4$ vertices, as shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc19901cca6b8f2e6291b110c5670383b06b46ff32421c9f2d96a0a3bca070b8.png)\n\nThe polygon also has $4$ straight sides connecting its vertices.\n\nTherefore, it's a **quadrilateral**." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147231 1069 "e-2164" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147231 1071 "Which of the following is a **nonagon**?\n\n![](images/cb/cb234d98c06d652fc0b0b5f59808952af65ce1df3240312b0308959404630762.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147231 1072 #uuid "f68aa5e8-32ff-5fbe-bbf6-063955392279" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147231 1074 "A nonagon has $9$ vertices and $9$ straight sides. Therefore, the answer is II.\n\nNotice that shape I has $10$ vertices, and shape III is not a polygon since it has a curved side." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147232 1069 "e-2175" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147232 1071 "Find the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose fourth term is $2$ and whose seventh term is $-686$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147232 1072 #uuid "9d8383ad-c617-5b01-8055-214a8c639f12" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147232 1074 "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms $a_m$ and $a_n$, we use the formula\n\n$$\nr^{n-m} = \\dfrac{a_n}{a_m}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given $a_7 = -686$ and $a_4 = 2$. Substituting $n=7$ and $m=4$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{7 - 4} &= \\frac{a_{7}}{a_{4}} \\\\\nr^{3} &= \\frac{-686}{2} \\\\\nr^{3} &= -343\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by solving the equation for $r$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{3} &= -343 \\\\\nr^{3} &= (-7)^{3} \\\\\nr &= -7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147233 1069 "e-21778" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147233 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$y'' + 16y = x\\sin(2x)$.\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\sin(4x)$ and $y_2(x) = \\cos(4x)$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\nwhere $u_1 = u_1(x), u_2 = u_2(x)$, that satisfies the constraint\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating $u_1'$ and $u_2'{:}$\n\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147233 1072 #uuid "76b310bf-b592-541c-82e8-93d72bc688f6" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147233 1074 "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)y_1(x)+u_2(x)y_2(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2 y_2'\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1 = \\sin(4x), \\qquad y_2 = \\cos(4x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for $y_p$, we get\n\n$$\ny_p =u_1\\sin(4x)+u_2\\cos(4x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for $y_p'$, we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1(\\sin(4x))'+u_2 (\\cos(4x))'\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates $u_1'$ and $u_2'$ by differentiating $y_p$ and equating this expression to our constraint on $y_p'$.\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′} &= (u_{1}\\sin (4x) + u_{2}\\cos (4x))^{′} \\\\\n&= (u_{1}\\sin (4x))^{′} + (u_{2}\\cos (4x))^{′} \\\\\n&= u_{1}^{′}\\sin (4x) + u_{1}(\\sin (4x))^{′} + u_{2}^{′}\\cos (4x) + u_{2}(\\cos (4x))^{′} \\\\\n&= u_{1}^{′}\\sin (4x) + 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) + u_{2}^{′}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for $y_p'$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{1}^{′}\\sin (4x) + 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) + u_{2}^{′}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x) &= u_{1}(\\sin (4x))^{′} + u_{2}(\\cos (4x))^{′} \\\\\nu_{1}^{′}\\sin (4x) + 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) + u_{2}^{′}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x) &= 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x) \\\\\nu_{1}^{′}\\sin (4x) + 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) + u_{2}^{′}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x) &= 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\nu_1' \\sin(4x) + u_2' \\cos(4x) = 0\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147234 1069 "e-21779" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147234 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$y'' + 5y' - 6y = p(x)$,\nwhose corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions\n$y_1(x)=e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x)=e^{-6x}$.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\n\nwhere $u_1 = u_1(x), u_2 = u_2(x)$, that satisfies the constraint\n\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y = y_p$ and $y' = y_p'$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between $u_1', u_2'$, and $p(x){:}$\n\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147234 1072 #uuid "8587baf6-8f11-56eb-8afa-dd385c0aeaeb" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147234 1074 "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)y_1(x)+u_2(x)y_2(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2 y_2'\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1 = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2 = e^{-6x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for $y_p$, we get\n\n$$\ny_p =u_1e^{x}+u_2e^{-6x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for $y_p'$, we get\n\n$$\ny_p' =u_1(e^{x})'+u_2 (e^{-6x})'\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y = y_p$ and $y' = y_p'$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{″} + 5y^{′} - 6y &= p(x) \\\\\n(y_{p}^{′})^{′} + 5y_{p}^{′} - 6y_{p} &= p(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our expressions for $y_p$ and $y_p'$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(u_{1}(e^{x})^{′} + u_{2}(e^{-6x})^{′})^{′} + 5(u_{1}(e^{x})^{′} + u_{2}(e^{-6x})^{′}) - 6(u_{1}e^{x} + u_{2}e^{-6x}) &= p(x) \\\\\n(u_{1}e^{x} - 6u_{2}e^{-6x})^{′} + 5u_{1}e^{x} - 30u_{2}e^{-6x} - 6u_{1}e^{x} - 6u_{2}e^{-6x} &= p(x) \\\\\n&= u_{1}^{′}e^{x} + u_{1}e^{x} - 6u_{2}^{′}e^{-6x} + 36u_{2}e^{-6x} \\\\\n+ 5u_{1}e^{x} - 30u_{2}e^{-6x} - 6u_{1}e^{x} - 6u_{2}e^{-6x} &= p(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group all terms proportional to $u_1$ and $u_2{:}$\n\n$$\nu_{1}(e^{x} + 5e^{x} - 6e^{x})_(⏟)_(0) + u_{2}(36e^{-6x} - 30e^{-6x} - 6e^{-6x})_(⏟)_(0) + u_{1}^{′}e^{x} - 6u_{2}^{′}e^{-6x} = p(x)\n$$\n\nNote that the expressions in parentheses are both zero. Therefore, the equation reduces to\n\n$$\nu_1'e^{x} - 6u_2'e^{-6x} = p(x)\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147235 1069 "e-2178" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147235 1071 "The third term of a geometric sequence is $80$ and its fifth term is $20$. Find the common ratio $r$ of this sequence given that $r$ is positive." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147235 1072 #uuid "7aa4b1ba-4362-5a4b-88d4-25d9ebe2cef7" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147235 1074 "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms $a_m$ and $a_n$, we use the formula\n\n$$\nr^{n-m} = \\dfrac{a_n}{a_m}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given $a_5 = 20$ and $a_3 = 80$. Substituting $n=5$ and $m=3$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{5 - 3} &= \\frac{a_{5}}{a_{3}} \\\\\nr^{2} &= \\frac{20}{80} \\\\\nr^{2} &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving the equation for $r$ and considering that $r$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} &= \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\nr^{2} &= (\\frac{1}{2})^{2} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147236 1069 "e-2179" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147236 1071 "Find the equation for the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose $4$th term is $6$ and whose $9$th term is $192$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147236 1072 #uuid "84364858-da8e-5241-85ca-ec84057bc6e0" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147236 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1r^{n-1}\n$$\n\nApplying this formula with $n=4$ and $n=9$, respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{4} &= a_{1}r^{4 - 1} = a_{1}r^{3}, \\\\\na_{9} &= a_{1}r^{9 - 1} = a_{1}r^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $a_4 = 6$ and $a_9 = 192$, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{a_{1}r^{3} = 6; a_{1}r^{8} = 192\n$$\n\nFinally, by dividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a_{1}r^{8}}{a_{1}r^{3}} &= \\frac{192}{6} \\\\\n\\frac{a_{1}r^{8}}{a_{1}r^{3}} &= \\frac{192}{6} \\\\\n\\frac{r^{8}}{r^{3}} &= 32 \\\\\nr^{5} &= 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147237 1069 "e-21832" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147237 1071 "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\sinh(3t)$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147237 1072 #uuid "3ee74da8-3a3b-5119-b34f-aee3f5770302" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147237 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\alpha f(t) + \\beta g(t)\\} = \\alpha\\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\} + \\beta\\cdot \\mathcal L \\{g(t)\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\sinh(3t) = \\dfrac{e^{3t} - e^{-3t}}{2}\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{\\sinh (3t)} &= L{\\frac{e^{3t} - e^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\\n&= L{\\frac{e^{3t}}{2} - \\frac{e^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\\n&= L{\\frac{e^{3t}}{2}} - L{\\frac{e^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}L{e^{3t}} - \\frac{1}{2}L{e^{-3t}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\left\\{e^{kt}\\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-k}, \\qquad s > k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{e^{3t}} &= \\frac{1}{s - 3}, s > 3 \\\\\nL{e^{-3t}} &= \\frac{1}{s + 3}, s > - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\sinh(3t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{\\sinh (3t)} &= \\frac{1}{2}L{e^{3t}} - \\frac{1}{2}L{e^{-3t}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{s - 3} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{s + 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s - 3} - \\frac{1}{s + 3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(((s + 3) - (s - 3))/((s - 3)(s + 3))) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{6}{s^{2} - 9}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{s^{2} - 9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\mathcal L\\{e^{3t}\\}$ is defined for $s\\in (3,\\infty)$ and $\\mathcal L \\{e^{-3t}\\}$ is defined for $s\\in (-3,\\infty)$, we have that $\\mathcal L\\{\\sinh(3t)\\}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(3,\\infty) \\cap (-3,\\infty) = (3,\\infty)\n$$\n\nthat is, $s > 3$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147238 1069 "e-21851" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147238 1071 "$x^2y'' + x(x-3)y' - 6y = 0$\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147238 1072 #uuid "babd5e8c-e5e9-5e5b-9b83-198bbccf41dd" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147238 1074 "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ and $a_n$ for\n\n$$\nn=0,1,2,\\ldots\n$$\n\nare constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary $a_0$, and\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n \\geq 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n = \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^{\\lambda+n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives $y'$ and $y''{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= (∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{λ + n})^{′} = ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n - 1} \\\\\ny^{″} &= (∑_(n = 0)^(∞)(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n - 1})^{′} = ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)(λ + n - 1)(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}y^{″} &= x^{2} \\cdot ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)(λ + n - 1)(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n - 2} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n}x^{λ + n}, \\\\\nx(x - 3)y^{′} &= (x^{2} - 3x) \\cdot ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n - 1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n + 1} - ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)3(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n}, \\\\\n- 6y &= - ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)6a_{n}x^{λ + n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the $y'$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing\n\n$$\nm=n+1\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\nx(x-3)y' = \\sum_{m=1}^\\infty (\\lambda+m-1)a_{m-1}x^{\\lambda+m} - \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty 3(\\lambda+n)a_nx^{\\lambda+n}\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}n=0}\n$$\n\nterm gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x - 3)y^{′} &= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(λ + n - 1)a_{n - 1}x^{λ + n} - ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)3(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(λ + n - 1)a_{n - 1}x^{λ + n} - 3λa_{0}x^{λ} - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)3(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n} \\\\\n&=-3λa_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[(λ + n - 1)a_{n - 1} - 3(λ + n)a_{n}]x^{λ + n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}n=0}\n$$\n\nterms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}y^{″} &= (λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n}x^{λ + n}, \\\\\n- 6y &= -6a_{0}x^{λ} - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)6a_{n}x^{λ + n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0$.\n\nCollecting the terms for\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}n=0}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ} - 3λa_{0}x^{λ} - 6a_{0}x^{λ} &=  \\\\\n(λ^{2} - λ - 3λ - 6)a_{0}x^{λ} &=  \\\\\n&= (λ^{2} - 4λ - 6)a_{0}x^{λ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nwe obtain an infinite series of the form\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x^{\\lambda}\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(\\lambda)x^n\n$$\n\nfor some $b_n(\\lambda)$,\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(λ^{2} - 4λ - 6)a_{0}x^{λ} + x^{λ}∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}(λ)x^{n} &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} - 4λ - 6)a_{0} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}(λ)x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0$.\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary\n\n$$\na_0\\neq0\n$$\n\nwe obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(λ^{2} - 4λ - 6)a_{0} &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} - 4λ - 6 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147239 1069 "e-21862" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147239 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem.\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t} - 2y = -5\\sin t, \\qquad y(0)=1$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms. You may make use of the following results:\n\n$\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad \\mathcal L\\{\\cos\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad s > 0$.\n\nLet $Y(s) = \\mathcal L \\{y(t)\\}$. Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y$, we have\n\n$AAAAA_{A}^{A}AA$\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n$AAAAA_{A}^{A}AA$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$AAAAA_{A}^{A}AA$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147239 1072 #uuid "abd7efc4-f397-564d-a471-17b1ffc93fb3" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147239 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{y'\\} - 2\\mathcal{L}\\{y\\} = -5\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin t\\}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{y'\\} = sY(s) - y(0)\n$$\n\nand the standard trigonometric transform\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin t\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s^2+1}\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(sY(s) - 1) - 2Y(s) &= -5 \\cdot \\frac{1}{s^{2} + 1} \\\\\n(s - 2)Y(s) - 1 &= -\\frac{5}{s^{2} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s)$. First, add $1$ to the right-hand side and find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(s - 2)Y(s) &= 1 - \\frac{5}{s^{2} + 1} \\\\\n(s - 2)Y(s) &= \\frac{s^{2} + 1}{s^{2} + 1} - \\frac{5}{s^{2} + 1} \\\\\n(s - 2)Y(s) &= \\frac{s^{2} - 4}{s^{2} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing by $(s-2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s) =\\dfrac{s^2 - 4}{(s - 2)(s^2 + 1)} = \\dfrac{(s-2)(s+2)}{(s - 2)(s^2 + 1)} = \\dfrac{s+2}{s^2 + 1}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{-1}{\\frac{s}{s^{2} + 1}} &= \\cos t \\\\\nL^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s^{2} + 1}} &= \\sin t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(t) &= L^{-1}{\\frac{s}{s^{2} + 1}} + 2L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s^{2} + 1}} \\\\\ny(t) &= \\cos t + 2\\sin t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147240 1069 "e-21897" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147240 1071 "$12x^2y'' + 7xy' + (2x-3)y = 0$\nFind the possible values of $\\lambda$ for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above, and write the corresponding Frobenius series forms.\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n$12x^{2}y^{″}|= 12(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)12((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n}x^{λ + n},; 7xy^{′}|= 7λa_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)7(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n},; (2x - 3)y|=-3a_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(2a_{n - 1} - 3a_{n})x^{λ + n}$.*" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147240 1072 #uuid "46371f2c-f7f3-5302-81b8-8ab1a21cfff8" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147240 1074 "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ and $a_n$ for\n\n$$\nn=0,1,2,\\ldots\n$$\n\nare constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary $a_0$, and\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n \\geq 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}n=0\n$$\n\nterm isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12x^{2}y^{″} &= 12(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)12((λ + n)^{2} - (λ + n))a_{n}x^{λ + n}, \\\\\n7xy^{′} &= 7λa_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)7(λ + n)a_{n}x^{λ + n}, \\\\\n(2x - 3)y &= -3a_{0}x^{λ} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(2a_{n - 1} - 3a_{n})x^{λ + n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0$.\n\nCollecting the terms for\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}n=0}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12(λ^{2} - λ)a_{0}x^{λ} + 7λa_{0}x^{λ} - 3a_{0}x^{λ} &=  \\\\\n(12λ^{2} - 12λ + 7λ - 3)a_{0}x^{λ} &=  \\\\\n&= (12λ^{2} - 5λ - 3)a_{0}x^{λ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nwe obtain an infinite series of the form\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x^{\\lambda}\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(\\lambda)x^n\n$$\n\nfor some $b_n(\\lambda)$,\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(12λ^{2} - 5λ - 3)a_{0}x^{λ} + x^{λ}∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}(λ)x^{n} &= 0 \\\\\n(12λ^{2} - 5λ - 3)a_{0} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}(λ)x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0$.\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary\n\n$$\na_0\\neq0\n$$\n\nwe obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(12λ^{2} - 5λ - 3)a_{0} &= 0 \\\\\n12λ^{2} - 5λ - 3 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine the possible values of $\\lambda$. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12λ^{2} - 5λ - 3 &= 0 \\\\\n(4λ - 3)(3λ + 1) &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= -\\frac{1}{3}, \\frac{3}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions have the forms\n\n$$\ny(x) = ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n - 1/3}andy(x) = ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n + 3/4}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants $a_n$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147241 1069 "e-22017" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147241 1071 "Emma and Sofia are art students. In the previous semester, $18 \\%$ of the paintings created by Emma were landscapes, while the decimal proportion of Sofia’s paintings that were landscapes was\n$0.2$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. Less than half of Emma's paintings were landscapes.\n2. More than half of Sofia's paintings were landscapes.\n3. Emma had a greater percentage of landscape paintings compared to Sofia." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147241 1072 #uuid "4016fd3c-d8b2-5ef2-8554-47ac5c183950" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147241 1074 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n$18\\% < 50\\%$,\nless than half of Emma's paintings were landscapes.\n- Statement II is false. Notice that\n$0.2 < 0.5$.\nSo, less than half of Sofia's paintings were landscapes.\n- Statement III is false. We can compare $18\\%$ and $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ by converting the decimal into a percent. To do this, we multiply it by $100{:}$\n$0.2 \\times 100 = 20\\%$\nSince $18\\% < 20 \\%$, Emma had a **smaller** percentage of landscape paintings compared to Sofia.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147242 1069 "e-22045" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147242 1071 "What is the smallest integer solution to $x \\geq 3$?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147242 1072 #uuid "2ab99a58-20d3-5a5a-a553-f4cc4084e11f" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147242 1074 "The condition\n\n$$\nx \\geq 3\n$$\n\nimplies that $x$ can be any value that is greater than or equal to $3$.\n\nTo represent this on a number line:\n\n- We draw a closed circle at $x=3$.\n- We draw an arrow starting from $3$ and going to the right.\n\n![](images/2c/2ca089e8dadc619ada73e69a97a90810210642eb19b9792219cbe9224b55bd88.png)\n\nFrom the number line, we see that **integer** solutions to\n\n$$\nx \\geq 3\n$$\n\nare\n\n$$\n3, \\, 4, \\, 5, \\, 6, \\, 7, \\, 8, \\, 9, \\, \\ldots\n$$\n\nAmong them, the **smallest** is $3$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147243 1069 "e-2214" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147243 1071 "A farmer wants to divide a field that is $\\dfrac 45$ acres into $3$ equal lots. How many acres would each lot be?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147243 1072 #uuid "b1c77a97-dda2-59f8-88e7-329a9807ac5a" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147243 1074 "To find the area of each lot, we need to divide\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nby $3$. That is, we need to compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {4}{5} \\div 3 \\,\n$$\n\nTo divide a fraction by a whole number, we instead multiply the fraction by the reciprocal of the whole number.\n\nThe reciprocal of $3$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {4}{5} \\div 3 = \\dfrac {4}{5} \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} \\,\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {4}{5} \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac {4 \\times 1} {5 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{4}{15}\n$$\n\nTherefore, each lot will be\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{15}\n$$\n\nacres." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147244 1069 "e-2216" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147244 1071 "Perl made a presentation of her trip around the world. The presentation lasts $6$ minutes, and each slide lasts $\\dfrac{3}{8}$ minutes. How many slides does her presentation have?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147244 1072 #uuid "dcf474c8-0bc3-51f2-b89a-1f750bcc76cf" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147244 1074 "To determine the number of slides, we need to divide $6$ by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nThat is, we need to compute\n\n$$\n6 \\div \\dfrac{3}{8} \\,\n$$\n\nTo divide a whole number by a fraction, we instead multiply the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nFirst, we write $6$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n6=\\dfrac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n6 \\div \\dfrac 3 8 = \\dfrac 6 1 \\div \\dfrac{3}{8} = \\dfrac 6 1 \\times \\dfrac{8}{3} \\,\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 6 {1} \\times \\dfrac{8}{3} = \\dfrac {6 \\times 8} {1 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{48}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{48}{3} = 48 \\div 3 = 16\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $16$ slides." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147245 1069 "e-2217" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147245 1071 "The area of a rectangular plantation is $3 \\, \\text{km}^2$ and its width is $\\dfrac{3}{2} \\, \\text{km}$. What is its length?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147245 1072 #uuid "3653a2e3-ead5-5bc2-803f-2af230687e57" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147245 1074 "To find out the length, we need to divide $3$ by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/bc/bc87033e9c14fdf1e5b277f0a46b79b13eec9db846784782a6d3349b5f1bec7d.png)\n\nThat is, we need to compute\n\n$$\n3 \\div \\dfrac{3}{2} \\,\n$$\n\nTo divide a whole number by a fraction, we instead multiply the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nFirst, we write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\dfrac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n3 \\div \\dfrac 3 2 = \\dfrac 3 1 \\div \\dfrac{3}{2} = \\dfrac 3 1 \\times \\dfrac{2}{3} \\,\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 {1} \\times \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac {3 \\times 2} {1 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{6}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{3} = 6 \\div 3 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length is $2 \\, \\text{km}$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147246 1069 "e-2218" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147246 1071 "Paul's internet connection allows him to download a complete file of a certain size in $\\dfrac{8}{3}$ minutes. If he has a total of $12$ minutes to download some files of the same size, how many complete files can he download?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147246 1072 #uuid "65692643-aa7f-5383-afe5-94bc9b78fab9" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147246 1074 "To determine the number of files Paul can download, we need to divide $12$ by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{3}\n$$\n\nThat is, we need to compute\n\n$$\n12 \\div \\dfrac{8}{3} \\,\n$$\n\nTo divide a whole number by a fraction, we instead multiply the whole number by the reciprocal of the fraction.\n\nFirst, we write $12$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n12=\\dfrac{12}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we note that the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{3}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n12 \\div \\dfrac{8}{3} = \\dfrac {12} 1 \\div \\dfrac{8}{3} = \\dfrac {12} 1 \\times \\dfrac{3}{8} \\,\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12} {1} \\times \\dfrac{3}{8} = \\dfrac {12 \\times 3} {1 \\times 8} = \\dfrac{36}{8}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{36}{8} = \\dfrac{36 \\div 4}{8 \\div 4} = \\dfrac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the result as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{2} = 4 \\, \\text{R} \\, 1 = 4 \\, \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Paul can download $4$ *complete* files in $12$ minutes." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147247 1069 "e-2297" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147247 1071 "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$f(x,y) = \\dfrac{xy^2 + x^2y}{x+y}\\,$?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147247 1072 #uuid "f0b5ae35-9ec6-5127-88e0-6d12ded41a43" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147247 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that\n\n- the numerator of $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $3$, and\n- the denominator of $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $1$.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $3-1 = 2$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty) = t^nf(x,y)\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty)$, and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(tx, ty) &= ((tx)(ty)^{2} + (tx^{2})(ty))/(tx + ty) \\\\\n&= (t^{3}xy^{2} + t^{3}x^{2}y)/(t(x + y)) \\\\\n&= (t^{3}(xy^{2} + x^{2}y))/(t(x + y)) \\\\\n&= (t^{2}(xy^{2} + x^{2}y))/(x + y) \\\\\n&= t^{2} \\cdot \\frac{xy^{2} + x^{2}y}{x + y} \\\\\n&= t^{2}f(x, y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty) = t^2 f(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ is $2$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147248 1069 "e-2298" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147248 1071 "Is the function $f(x,y,z) = x^3+2y^3+z^3$ homogeneous? If so, what is its degree?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147248 1072 #uuid "f0a5e360-e989-5252-b4dc-d53336e982fe" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147248 1074 "A function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty, tz) = t^nf(x,y, z)\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty,tz)$, and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n f(x,y,z)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(tx, ty, tz) &= (tx)^{3} + 2(ty)^{3} + (tz)^{3} \\\\\n&= t^{3}x^{3} + 2t^{3}y^{3} + t^{3}z^{3} \\\\\n&= t^{3}(x^{3} + 2y^{3} + z^{3}) \\\\\n&= t^{3}f(x, y, z)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz) = t^3 f(x,y,z)\n$$\n\nand so the function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $3$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147249 1069 "e-2299" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147249 1071 "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{y}{x}\\right)$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147249 1072 #uuid "9a070d56-1aac-5f5d-885c-790d75571c63" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147249 1074 "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^ng(x,y)\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty)$, and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(tx, ty) &= \\frac{1}{tx}\\cos (\\frac{ty}{tx}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{tx}\\cos (\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{t} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\cos (\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= t^{-1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\cos (\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= t^{-1}g(x, y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^{-1} g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ is $-1$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147250 1069 "e-2300" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147250 1071 "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n1. $f(x,y) = x^2+x+y$\n2. $g(x,y) = x^6-2x^3y^3 + y^6$\n3. $h(x,y) = 6$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147250 1072 #uuid "5def8755-3a1d-5f9c-a9f1-ac51d4ed5b13" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147250 1074 "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(x,y)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $2$, but not all the terms have the same degree. The first term has degree $2$, while the other terms have degree $1$.\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $6$. It is a polynomial of degree $6$, and all of its terms are of degree $6$.\n- The function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $0$. It is a polynomial of degree $0$, and all of its terms are of degree $0$. (There is only one term, and it has degree $0$.)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147251 1069 "e-2317" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147251 1071 "Compare $0.35$ and $30\\%$ by filling in the missing parts in the statement below.\n\n$\\qquad$\n$0.35$ is equivalent to $AA$ which is $AAAAAA$ $30\\%$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147251 1072 #uuid "7dd3b106-ac45-522a-97ea-6dc4355f0247" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147251 1074 "To convert the decimal to a percent, we multiply it by $100{:}$\n\n$$\n0.35 \\times 100 = 35\\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.35$ is equivalent to $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{35\\%}$,\nwhich is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{greater than}}$ $30\\%$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147252 1069 "e-2318" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147252 1071 "Compare $60\\%$ and $\\dfrac{11}{15}$ by filling in the missing parts in the statement below.\n\n$\\qquad$\n$60\\%$ is equivalent to $AA$ which is $AAAAAA$ $\\dfrac{11}{15}$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147252 1072 #uuid "22bfefb3-89b4-5d42-b305-732790c89fbd" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147252 1074 "To convert the percent to a fraction, we first write $60\\%$ as a fraction with a denominator $100{:}$\n\n$$\n60\\% = \\dfrac{60}{100}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $10{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{60}{100} = \\dfrac{60 \\div 10}{100 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{6}{10}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{10} = \\dfrac{6 \\div 2}{10 \\div 2} = \\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can write this with a denominator of $15$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5} = \\dfrac{3 \\times 3}{5 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{9}{15}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{15} \\lt \\dfrac{11}{15}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $60\\%$ equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gray]{\\dfrac{9}{15}}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gray]{\\text{less than}}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{15}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147253 1069 "e-2319" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147253 1071 "Noah and Lucas have the same number of books. A total of $68 \\%$ of Noah's books are fiction, while $\\dfrac{7}{10}$ of Lucas's books are fiction.\n\nCompare the number of books Noah and Lucas have by filling in the missing parts in the statement below.\n\n$\\qquad$\nA total of $AA$ of Lucas's books are fiction.\n\n$\\qquad$\nTherefore, he has $AAAAAA$ fiction books compared to Noah." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147253 1072 #uuid "e6b550b1-cd66-5a70-9d11-d37733ac4c5f" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147253 1074 "We need to compare\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{10}\n$$\n\nwith $68 \\%$. We can do this by writing\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{10}\n$$\n\nas a percent.\n\nFirst, we write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{10}\n$$\n\nas an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{10} = \\dfrac{7 \\times 10}{10 \\times 10} = \\dfrac{70}{100}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{70}{100} = 70 \\%\n$$\n\nSince $70 \\% > 68 \\%$, we have the following:\n\n- A total of $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{70\\%}$ of Lucas's books are fiction.\n- Therefore, he has $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{more}}$ fiction books compared to Noah." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147254 1069 "e-2327" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147254 1071 "In a garden, there are $40$ flowers, $30 \\%$ of which are roses. How many roses are there in the garden?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147254 1072 #uuid "f888277f-085b-5c6a-b50c-8ffd9095bc53" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147254 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}= \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the number of flowers in the garden, which is $40$ flowers. The percentage is $30 \\%$, and the part is the number of roses in the garden.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n30 \\% = 30 \\div 100 = 0.3\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 30% \\cdot 40 \\\\\n&= 0.3 \\cdot 40 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $12$ roses in the garden.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n30 \\% = \\dfrac{30}{100} = \\dfrac{30 \\div 10}{100 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 30% \\cdot 40 \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{10} \\cdot 40 \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot \\frac{40}{10} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are $12$ roses in the garden." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147255 1069 "e-2329" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147255 1071 "Dora works $800$ hours per year. She rests for $12.5 \\%$ ​​of her total working hours. How much time does Dora rest per year at work?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147255 1072 #uuid "03c003db-96d2-5588-a1fa-944b9305cf6f" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147255 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}= \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the total number of hours Dora works yearly, which is $800$ hours. The percentage is $12.5 \\%$, and the part is the number of hours she rests.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n12.5 \\% = 12.5 \\div 100 = 0.125\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 12.5% \\cdot 800 \\\\\n&= 0.125 \\cdot 800 \\\\\n&= 100\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Dora rests for $100$ hours.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n12.5 \\% = \\dfrac{12.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 12.5% \\cdot 800 \\\\\n&= \\frac{12.5}{100} \\cdot 800 \\\\\n&= 12.5 \\cdot \\frac{800}{100} \\\\\n&= 12.5 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 100\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Dora rests for $100$ hours." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147256 1069 "e-2330" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147256 1071 "Marcus has a tank with a capacity of $1,500$ liters. He decides to buy a second tank whose capacity is $130 \\%$ of the capacity of the first tank. What is the capacity of the second tank?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147256 1072 #uuid "f409e34f-c8f7-5e21-b024-a0c7012c496d" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147256 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part}= \\textrm{percentage}\\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the capacity of the first tank, which is $1,500$ liters. The percentage is $130 \\%$, and the part is the capacity of the second tank.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n130 \\% = 130 \\div 100 = 1.3\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 130% \\cdot 1, 500 \\\\\n&= 1.3 \\cdot 1, 500 \\\\\n&= 1, 950\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second tank has a capacity of $1,950$ liters.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n130 \\% = \\dfrac{130}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 130% \\cdot 1, 500 \\\\\n&= \\frac{130}{100} \\cdot 1, 500 \\\\\n&= 130 \\cdot \\frac{1, 500}{100} \\\\\n&= 130 \\cdot 15 \\\\\n&= 1, 950\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second tank has a capacity of $1,950$ liters." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147257 1069 "e-2373" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147257 1071 "The point $A(-3,-4)$ is reflected in the $y$-axis. What are the coordinates of the resulting point?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147257 1072 #uuid "9aa22e7f-264e-50a1-afc3-c894eb0953a7" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147257 1074 "The point $A$ has coordinates $(-{\\color{blue}{3}},-4)$. So, to reflect $A$ in the $y$-axis, we\n\n- move it ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ units right to reach the $y$-axis, and then\n- move it ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ units right again on the other side of the axis.\n\n![](images/f1/f1e5c76243aeb199d30dc3211924d2251b2f40ec2cb3ad260bce85b1070a903e.png)\n\nThe resulting point $A'$ has coordinates $({\\color{blue}{3}},-4)$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147258 1069 "e-2374" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147258 1071 "The point $A$ is reflected in $y$-axis to make a new point $B$. The point $B$ is then reflected in the $x$-axis to make a new point $C$. What are the coordinates of $C$?\n\n![](images/4f/4f5abfc51fa141754867005f3ba0dbc31375e38e37eef956d05ddadeeece1e73.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147258 1072 #uuid "b43d5e4d-358e-5f82-8af4-084c859ba452" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147258 1074 "The point $A$ has coordinates $(-{\\color{blue}{4}},-{\\color{red}{2}})$.\n\nTo reflect the point $A$ in the $y$-axis, we move it ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ units right, and then ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ units right again.\n\n![](images/d5/d55fabc76a32557dc8e19dfba2dabbd6bd541dcc724b2aa3b7b970e16d24f157.png)\n\nThe resulting point $B$ has coordinates $({\\color{blue}{4}},-{\\color{red}{2}})$.\n\nThen, we reflect point $B({\\color{blue}{4}},-{\\color{red}{2}})$ in the $x$-axis.\nTo reflect the point $B$ in the $x$-axis, we move it ${\\color{red}{2}}$ units up, and then ${\\color{red}{2}}$ units up again.\n\n![](images/b4/b4a51a90cbafc065313b95a205810bef2f882ddbe3a79936e6c6f3d5eacf8b0d.png)\n\nThe resulting point $C$ has coordinates $({\\color{blue}{4}},{\\color{red}{2}})$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147259 1069 "e-2377" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147259 1071 "The point $A(3,2)$ is reflected in the $y$-axis to make a new point $B$. The point $B$ is then reflected in the $y$-axis. What are the coordinates of the final point?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147259 1072 #uuid "eaf66088-e7c2-553d-a697-054c2ff532c3" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147259 1074 "The point $A$ has coordinates $({\\color{blue}{3}}, 2)$.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo reflect the point $A$ in the $y$-axis, we move it ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ units left, and then ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ units left again.\n\n![](images/c9/c969da23ee925dccd35d209bc73ece4215a4e6302794f91dd68924ea96a14109.png)\n\nThe resulting point $B$ has coordinates $(-{\\color{blue}{3}}, 2)$.\n\nTo reflect the resulting point $B$ in the $y$-axis, we move it ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ units right, and then ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ units right again.\n\n![](images/30/301c8e9db4e7ff0585a73a333c09d317883f984eff0ad7c58949427e384525b9.png)\n\nNote that after the double reflection in the $y$-axis, we return to the initial point $A$. It has coordinates $({\\color{blue}{3}}, 2)$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo reflect $A$ point in the $y$-axis, we change the sign of its $x$-coordinate:\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{3}}, 2) \\longrightarrow (-{\\color{blue}{3}}, 2)\n$$\n\nTo reflect reflect the resulting point in the $y$-axis again, we again change the sign of its $x$-coordinate:\n$(-{\\color{blue}{3}}, 2) \\longrightarrow ({\\color{blue}{3}}, 2)$\n\nAfter the double reflection in the $y$-axis, we return to the initial point $A({\\color{blue}{3}}, 2)$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147260 1069 "e-2378" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147260 1071 "The diagram shows a map of a district. What is the distance between the church and the TV station?\n\n![](images/1a/1a6622a0e2a6f992611598cc58a7e4cf16092ba751d4faf71891c395870ff8f7.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147260 1072 #uuid "e58aec37-8365-5ee9-a067-18ec9e48be1e" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147260 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment connecting the church and the TV station is $8$ units.\n\n![](images/00/00345efcfd5e381b79ea20c0af2c119c86a357d1f65c3229e3019b86f66a8b32.png)\n\nOne unit is $1$ kilometer, so the distance is\n\n$$\n8 \\times 1 \\,\\textrm{km} = 8 \\,\\textrm{km}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFrom the diagram, we can see that the church has coordinates $(-3,-3)$ and the TV station has coordinates $(-3,5)$.\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points in this case by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](images/e9/e9206f0e25b3336f60ec1b96001ba4d8d139a6b0870340f9af5aae1b6b777bc0.png)\n\nThe point $(-3,{\\color{red}{-3}})$ is at a distance of $\\color{red}{3}$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(-3,{\\color{blue}{5}})$ is at a distance of $\\color{blue}{5}$ from the $x$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the distance between the two points is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{3}} + {\\color{blue}{5}} = 8\n$$\n\nOne unit is $1$ kilometer, so the total distance is\n\n$$\n8 \\times 1 \\,\\textrm{km} = 8 \\,\\textrm{km}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147261 1069 "e-2380" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147261 1071 "The point $P$ is initially in the third quadrant. Then, it is reflected in the $y$-axis. In which quadrant does the resulting point lie?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147261 1072 #uuid "32948b5b-c8f1-51c9-bd0c-0e3220498579" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147261 1074 "To reflect the point $P$ in the $y$-axis, we move it right to the $y$-axis, and then the same distance right again. We see that the resulting point is in the fourth quadrant.\n\n![](images/8e/8ed514abd3ad25c3f02acda7357ccbcd49615957db8ab3914d5ff466c701f3f5.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147262 1069 "e-2385" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147262 1071 "The graph shows the average daily temperature $T$ (measured in $^{\\circ}\\text{C}$) and the deviation from the normal pressure $P$ (measured in $\\text{mbar}$) in Anchorage for several days. Which quadrant contains data for the days that are with a negative deviation from the normal pressure but with a positive average daily temperature?\n\n![](images/e5/e520bd7bfc44519041853b306ac4e2326e64b1e94b7e38ccb9f738d5ed5e5fd2.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147262 1072 #uuid "db8be4bd-91e0-5696-ae15-1981eb664466" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147262 1074 "In the given coordinate plane,\n\n- the horizontal $x$-axis corresponds to the deviation from the normal pressure, and\n- the vertical $y$-axis corresponds to the average daily temperature.\n\nSo, we need to find the quadrant containing points that have negative $x$-coordinates and positive $y$-coordinates.\n\n![](images/99/99e89cd9368a220a9d57fddf22a9747aa415ac652706dcdbb4c3b7d868a71dca.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that this data is represented by points in the second quadrant." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147263 1069 "e-2386" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147263 1071 "A plane took off from JFK airport (New York, USA) bound for Madrid, Spain. Every $90$ minutes the GPS navigator recorded the coordinates of the plane's position on a map. The data that it collects is shown in the table below. The coordinate system is set up so that the positive horizontal axis is aligned with the East, and the positive vertical axis is aligned with the North.\n\n| New York local time | Coordinates |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $12\\!:\\!00$ am | $(-5,3)$ |\n| $1\\!:\\!30$ am | $(-2,3)$ |\n| $3\\!:\\!00$ am | $(1,2)$ |\n| $4\\!:\\!30$ am | $(3,2)$ |\n| $6\\!:\\!00$ am | $(4,-3)$ |\n| $7\\!:\\!30$ am | $(5,-4)$ |\n\nDuring what period of time did the plane travel strictly East?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147263 1072 #uuid "8be1b106-6175-5393-8fa4-ad2f6b51021a" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147263 1074 "Plotting the coordinates from the table on the map, we get:\n\n![](images/cc/cc9036a3d18443c7b55539440bb13ad81f6d727eb7bf5daab09da0b7790dea93.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the plane traveled East from $12\\!\\mathbin:\\!00$ to $1\\!\\mathbin:\\!30$ and from $3\\!\\mathbin:\\!00$ to $4\\!\\mathbin:\\!30$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147264 1069 "e-2388" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147264 1071 "Orlando works delivering newspapers every morning. Every minute he recorded his coordinates on a map using a GPS navigator. The results are given in the table below. The coordinates that he records are set up so that a difference of one unit corresponds to $1$ meter.\n\n| Time | Coordinates |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $7\\!:\\!00$ am | $(10,-30)$ |\n| $7\\!:\\!01$ am | $(30,-30)$ |\n| $7\\!:\\!02$ am | $(30,40)$ |\n| $7\\!:\\!03$ am | $(-30,40)$ |\n| $7\\!:\\!04$ am | $(-40,-50)$ |\n\nAssuming that Orlando traveled at a constant speed from $7\\!:\\!02$ am to $7\\!:\\!03$ am, what was his speed during this time interval?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147264 1072 #uuid "e752d648-3371-5893-bd0a-5d1b437cf35d" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147264 1074 "Plotting the coordinates from the table on the map, we get:\n\n![](images/b9/b9ab0c3445d710e756efcda419b8f8007886ca5c7c3c9f5291e7e33447972854.png)\n\nIn the interval from $7\\!:\\!02$ am to $7\\!:\\!03$ am, Orlando traveled $60$ units. Since one unit corresponds to $1$ meter, then Orlando traveled $60 \\, \\textrm{m}$.\n\nTherefore, Orlando traveled at a speed of $60$ meters per minute during this time period." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147265 1069 "e-2416" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147265 1071 "Which symbols could replace the empty box below to make the statement true?\n$4^2 \\,\\,\\square\\,\\, 1^7$\n\n1. $>$\n2. $<$\n3. $=$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147265 1072 #uuid "81ca2697-32fe-5dff-b04d-0c3895ccea9c" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147265 1074 "First, we calculate $4^2$. We have\n$2$\n\nThen, for $1^7$, we have\n\n$$\n1^{7} = 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1_(⏟)_([math]{\\color{red}7} [/math] times) = 1\n$$\n\nBecause $16 > 1$, the correct statement must be\n$4^2 > 1^7 \\,$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147266 1069 "e-2417" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147266 1071 "Which symbols could replace the empty box below to make the statement true?\n$\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 \\,\\,\\square\\,\\, \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^1$\n\n1. $>$\n2. $<$\n3. $=$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147266 1072 #uuid "81efbdf3-8a19-5b11-b3c3-c25abf854d50" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147266 1074 "First, we calculate\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1}{3})^{2} &= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}_(⏟)_([math]{\\color{blue}2} [/math] times) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 \\cdot 1}{3 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, for\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^1\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{4})^{1} = \\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nBecause\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9} < \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nthe correct statement must be\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 < \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^1 \\,\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147267 1069 "e-2418" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147267 1071 "Which symbols could replace the empty box below to make the statement true?\n$-2^3 \\,\\,\\,\\square\\,-3^2$\n\n1. $>$\n2. $<$\n3. $=$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147267 1072 #uuid "6890fc72-51e7-5bd6-9c50-b7b75a94d985" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147267 1074 "**Watch Out!** Remember that,\n\n- $-2^3$ is shorthand for $-\\!\\left(2^3\\right)$,\n- similarly, $-3^2$ is shorthand for $-\\!\\left(3^2\\right)$.\n\nFirst, we calculate $-2^3$. We ignore the negative sign and calculate the positive exponent first:\n$3$\n\nTherefore, $-2^3 = -8$.\n\nNow, to compute $-3^2$, we again ignore the negative sign and calculate the positive exponent first:\n\n$$\n3^{2} = 3 \\cdot 3_(⏟)_([math]{\\color{red}2} [/math] times) = 9\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-3^2 = -9$.\n\nFinally, let's look at the number line to compare $-8$ and $-9$.\n\n![](images/52/529d74b465ca32e111aac2a138222228b1aba1c06d7baf92bf8c5a84b3d78e09.png)\n\nSince $-8 > -9$, the correct statement must be\n$-2^3 > -3^2 \\,$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147268 1069 "e-2516" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147268 1071 "Find the value of $q$ given that $p = q + 6$ and $p = 19$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147268 1072 #uuid "c9ec522a-a42b-5289-b35f-19f7a28dc177" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147268 1074 "To find the value of $q$, we first substitute $p = 19$ into this equation:\n\n$$\n19 = q + 6\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $q$ by subtracting $6$ from both sides of this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n19 &= q + 6 \\\\\n19 - 6 &= q + 6 - 6 \\\\\n13 &= q + 0 \\\\\n13 &= q \\\\\nq &= 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147269 1069 "e-2518" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147269 1071 "Find the value of $w$ given that $z = \\dfrac{w}{4}$ and $z = 7$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147269 1072 #uuid "a9780fc1-aceb-5bda-972e-72c3bdac3921" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147269 1074 "To find the value of $w$, we first substitute $z = 7$ into this equation:\n\n$$\n7 = \\dfrac{w}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $w$ by multiplying both sides of this equation by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7 &= \\frac{w}{4} \\\\\n7 \\cdot 4 &= \\frac{w}{4} \\cdot 4 \\\\\n28 &= \\frac{4w}{4} \\\\\n28 &= w \\\\\nw &= 28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147270 1069 "e-2519" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147270 1071 "Solve for $a$ if $3a = b + 5$ and $b = 10$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147270 1072 #uuid "2e94a6c7-d6e9-5432-9ec0-5c911ea09770" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147270 1074 "To find the value of $a$, we first substitute $b = 10$ into this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3a &= 10 + 5 \\\\\n3a &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $a$ by dividing both sides of this equation by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3a &= 15 \\\\\n\\frac{3a}{3} &= \\frac{15}{3} \\\\\n\\frac{3a}{3} &= 5 \\\\\na &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147271 1069 "e-2520" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147271 1071 "At the beginning of the year, there were $n$ students in the sixth-grade class. There are currently $m$ students in this class. The relationship between $n$ and $m$ is given by the equation $m = n + 7$. If there are currently $38$ students in the sixth-grade class, how many students did this class have at the beginning of the year?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147271 1072 #uuid "1c16b235-d710-55f1-be58-13e64cf8f452" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147271 1074 "If there are currently $38$ students in the sixth-grade class, we have that $m = 38$.\nSo, we substitute $m = 38$ into the equation\n\n$$\nm = n + 7\n$$\n\nand get:\n\n$$\n38 = n + 7\n$$\n\nWe solve for $n$ by subtracting $7$ from both sides of this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n38 &= n + 7 \\\\\n38 - 7 &= n + 7 - 7 \\\\\n31 &= n + 0 \\\\\n31 &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there were $31$ students in the sixth-grade class at the beginning of the year." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147272 1069 "e-2521" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147272 1071 "| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $4$ | $0$ |\n| $6$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ | $4$ |\n| $10$ | $6$ |\n\nThe equation that represents the relationship between $y$ and $x$ is $0$ What is the missing number?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147272 1072 #uuid "ee226d3a-a013-570c-806d-05ee759b71c7" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147272 1074 "We notice that, for every $x$ value, we subtract $4$ to get the corresponding $y$ value.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $4$ | $4 - {\\color{blue}4} = 0$ |\n| $6$ | $6 - {\\color{blue}4}=2$ |\n| $8$ | $8-{\\color{blue}4}=4$ |\n| $10$ | $10-{\\color{blue}4}=6$ |\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny = x - {\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is $4$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147273 1069 "e-2522" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147273 1071 "| $a$ | $b$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $10$ | $2$ |\n| $20$ | $4$ |\n| $30$ | $6$ |\n| $40$ | $8$ |\n\nThe equation that represents the relationship between $b$ and $a$ is $0$ Find the missing number." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147273 1072 #uuid "60a61ab4-6500-5b90-86c2-fa4977a2b7c1" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147273 1074 "We notice that, for every $a$-value, we divide by $\\color{blue}5$ to get the corresponding $b$-value.\n\n| $a$ | $b$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $10$ | $\\dfrac{10}{\\color{blue}5} = 2$ |\n| $20$ | $\\dfrac{20}{\\color{blue}5}=4$ |\n| $30$ | $\\dfrac{30}{\\color{blue}5}=6$ |\n| $40$ | $\\dfrac{40}{\\color{blue}5}=8$ |\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\nb = \\dfrac{a}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is $5$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147274 1069 "e-2523" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147274 1071 "| $p$ | $q$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ |  |\n| $3$ | $12$ |\n| $4$ | $16$ |\n|  | $20$ |\n\nThe table represents the equation $q = 4p$. From **top to bottom**, what are the missing numbers?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147274 1072 #uuid "f6ad9efc-d385-5f16-9050-cbdbe70effef" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147274 1074 "To calculate the missing value of $q$, we substitute $p=2$ into the relationship $q = 4p{:}$\n\n$$\nq = 4 \\times 2 = {\\color{blue}{8}}\n$$\n\nTo find the second missing value, we substitute $q=20$ into the relationship $q = 4p{:}$\n$20 = 4p$\n\nNow, we solve this equation by dividing both sides by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20 &= 4p \\\\\n\\frac{20}{4} &= \\frac{4p}{4} \\\\\n5 &= \\frac{4p}{4} \\\\\n5 &= p\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, from top to bottom, the missing numbers are $8$ and $5$.\n\n| $p$ | $q$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | ${\\color{blue}{8}}$ |\n| $3$ | $12$ |\n| $4$ | $16$ |\n| ${\\color{red}{5}}$ | $20$ |" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147275 1069 "e-2524" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147275 1071 "| $z$ | $t$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $3$ | $1$ |\n| $6$ | $2$ |\n| $9$ | $3$ |\n| $12$ | $4$ |\n\nWhat equation represents the relationship between $z$ and $t$?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147275 1072 #uuid "03406eac-db96-5784-ba2b-63ee3184f368" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147275 1074 "Note that, for every $z$-value, we divide by $\\color{blue}3$ to get the corresponding $t$-value.\n\n| $z$ | $t$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $3$ | $3 \\div {\\color{blue}3} = 1$ |\n| $6$ | $6 \\div {\\color{blue}3} = 2$ |\n| $9$ | $9 \\div {\\color{blue}3} = 3$ |\n| $12$ | $12 \\div {\\color{blue}3} = 4$ |\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{z}{3}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147276 1069 "e-2532" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147276 1071 "Which of the following values of $x$ satisfy the inequality $2x < 10\\,$?\n\n1. $x=0$\n2. $x=2$\n3. $x=5$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147276 1072 #uuid "e31d3537-bb56-5352-8e1b-5a9adcbda6af" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147276 1074 "Let's go through the possibilities one at a time.\n\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}0}$ into the given inequality, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2x & < 10 \\\\ 2 \\cdot x & < 10 \\\\ 2 \\cdot 0 & < 10 \\\\ 0 & < 10✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a *true* statement, so $x=0$ *satisfies* this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}2}$ into the given inequality, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2x & < 10 \\\\ 2 \\cdot x & < 10 \\\\ 2 \\cdot 2 & < 10 \\\\ 4 & < 10✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a *true* statement, so $x=2$ *satisfies* this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}5}$ into the given inequality, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2x & < 10 \\\\ 2 \\cdot x & < 10 \\\\ 2 \\cdot 5 & < 10 \\\\ 10 & < 10\\cdot\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a *false* statement, so $x=5$ *does not satisfy* this inequality.\n\nTherefore, only I and II satisfy the inequality." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147277 1069 "e-2533" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147277 1071 "The value $a = 2$ satisfies which of the following inequalities?\n\n1. $a + 2 > 5$\n2. $2a < 6$\n3. $3a - 1 < 10$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147277 1072 #uuid "b48c9a74-1618-542e-9504-57542c7465cb" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147277 1074 "Let's go through the possibilities one at a time.\n\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}a} = {\\color{blue}2}$ into the inequality I, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a + 2 & > 5 \\\\ 2 + 2 & > 5 \\\\ 4 & > 5\\cdot\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a *false* statement, so $a = 2$ *does not satisfy* this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}a}={\\color{blue}2}$ into the inequality II, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2a & < 6 \\\\ 2 \\cdot a & < 6 \\\\ 2 \\cdot 2 & < 6 \\\\ 4 & < 6✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a *true* statement, so $a = 2$ *satisfies* this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}a}={\\color{blue}2}$ into the inequality III, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3a - 1 & < 10 \\\\ 3 \\cdot a - 1 & < 10 \\\\ 3 \\cdot 2 - 1 & < 10 \\\\ 5 & < 10✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a *true* statement, so $a = 2$ *satisfies* this inequality.\n\nTherefore, the value $a=2$ only satisfies inequalities II and III." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147278 1069 "e-2534" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147278 1071 "Which of the following values of $t$ satisfy the inequality $\\dfrac{2t}{3} < 8\\,$?\n\n1. $t=9$\n2. $t=6$\n3. $t=3$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147278 1072 #uuid "85c6f882-5906-5285-844c-4da0b3ed7042" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147278 1074 "Let's go through the possibilities one at a time.\n\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}t}={\\color{blue}9}$ into the given inequality, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2t}{3} & < 8 \\\\ \\frac{2 \\cdot t}{3} & < 8 \\\\ \\frac{2 \\cdot 9}{3} & < 8 \\\\ \\frac{18}{3} & < 8 \\\\ 6 & < 8✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a *true* statement, so $t=9$ *satisfies* this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}t}={\\color{blue}6}$ into the given inequality, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2t}{3} & < 8 \\\\ \\frac{2 \\cdot t}{3} & < 8 \\\\ \\frac{2 \\cdot 6}{3} & < 8 \\\\ \\frac{12}{3} & < 8 \\\\ 4 & < 8✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a *true* statement, so $t=6$ *satisfies* this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}t}={\\color{blue}3}$ into the given inequality, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2t}{3} & < 8 \\\\ \\frac{2 \\cdot t}{3} & < 8 \\\\ \\frac{2 \\cdot 3}{3} & < 8 \\\\ \\frac{6}{3} & < 8 \\\\ 2 & < 8✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a *true* statement, so $t=3$ *satisfies* this inequality.\n\nTherefore, I, II, and III all satisfy the inequality." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147279 1069 "e-2535" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147279 1071 "The value $z = 1$ satisfies which of the following inequalities?\n\n1. $5z + 4 < 8$\n2. $z + \\dfrac{1}{3} < \\dfrac{2}{3}$\n3. $\\dfrac{z}{2} < 1$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147279 1072 #uuid "9b0efeac-2897-5607-80ce-bee640ff98ef" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147279 1074 "Let's go through the possibilities one at a time.\n\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}z}={\\color{blue}1}$ into inequality I, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5z + 4 & < 8 \\\\ 5 \\cdot z + 4 & < 8 \\\\ 5 \\cdot 1 + 4 & < 8 \\\\ 9 & < 8\\cdot\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a *false* statement, so $z = 1$ *does not satisfy* this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}z}={\\color{blue}1}$ into inequality II, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}z + \\frac{1}{3} & < \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 1 + \\frac{1}{3} & < \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1 \\cdot 3 + 1}{3} & < \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & < \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a *false* statement, so $z = 1$ *does not satisfy* this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}z}={\\color{blue}1}$ into inequality III, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{z}{2} & < 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & < 1✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a *true* statement, so $z = 1$ *satisfies* this inequality.\n\nTherefore, the value $z=1$ only satisfies inequality III." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147280 1069 "e-2536" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147280 1071 "Determine the area of the parallelogram.\n\n![](images/ed/ed4b1b874e7bd5f95f902df95e687da30f20c9c9a5f0ca60d5ed85326961aebf.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147280 1072 #uuid "bbdf8752-8157-5ee6-88b3-72f0d28bd7e6" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147280 1074 "First, we find the base and the height of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nbase &= 6 \\\\\nheight &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the parallelogram can be found by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area} = \\textrm{base} \\times \\textrm{height}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the values of the base and height, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea &= 6 \\cdot 3 = 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the units. Since base and height are measured in centimeters, the area is measured in square centimeters. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area} = 18 \\, \\textrm{cm}^2\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147281 1069 "e-2537" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147281 1071 "What is the area of the parallelogram?\n\n![](images/02/022d0067894acfa22e35ec7548288cf7abf5757b61178e6cbf49df1eebfc94c4.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147281 1072 #uuid "31d4ff87-2aa4-5b98-9b64-3c9ca37a5a5c" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147281 1074 "First, we find the height and base of the parallelogram.\n\nRecall that two opposite sides of a parallelogram have the same length. So we can label another side, as follows:\n\n![](images/06/06e4acb9990e9deb892727a27e604d2ef212778c2faa954e91ae92350db600d0.png)\n\nRemember that the base is the length of a side of the parallelogram, while the height is at a right angle to the base. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nbase &= 4 \\\\\nheight &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the area of the parallelogram can be found by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area} = \\textrm{base} \\times \\textrm{height}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the values of the base and height, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea &= 4 \\cdot 3 = 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147282 1069 "e-2538" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147282 1071 "Find the area of the parallelogram.\n\n![](images/71/71caea2c3cf6e70b924864db4760e59489b249731ac642ce52250846c47836ae.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147282 1072 #uuid "946edf5f-de53-5207-97db-bba91b3aab1a" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147282 1074 "First, we find the base and height of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nbase &= 5 \\\\\nheight &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the parallelogram can be found by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area} = \\textrm{base} \\times \\textrm{height}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the values of the base and height, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea &= 5 \\cdot 2 = 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the units. Since base and height are measured in inches, the area is measured in square inches. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area} = 10 \\, \\textrm{in}^2\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147283 1069 "e-2539" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147283 1071 "What the perimeter of the parallelogram given that its area is $48$?\n\n![](images/22/2286d87e4cf1629cdf2cc026fd02f3ae67b07907950586b2612cc61de0053542.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147283 1072 #uuid "7c061ae4-d4ff-5ebf-b08a-1bab7eb1c228" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147283 1074 "Let's write down what we know:\n\n$$\nArea = 48; base =?; height = 4; side = 5\n$$\n\nThe area of the parallelogram can be found by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area} = \\textrm{base} \\times \\textrm{height}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the values of the area and height, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n48 &= base \\cdot 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can solve for the base:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{base} = 48 \\div 4 = 12\n$$\n\nFinally, the perimeter of the parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPerimeter &= (base + side) \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= (12 + 5) \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 17 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 34\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147284 1069 "e-2643" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147284 1071 "Determine the area of the shaded region shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/eadd2a317ad1fa0b6c9534827e1ea56046a2951b691681967a56f39de8863315.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147284 1072 #uuid "f7995cf5-8a53-5b31-b9ac-346b63a3cd93" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147284 1074 "This shape is a parallelogram with a rectangle cut out.\n\nTo find the total area, we need to subtract the area of the inner shape (the rectangle) from the area of the outer shape (the parallelogram).\n\nFirst, we find the area of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea of Parallelogram &= base \\cdot height \\\\\n&= 12 \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&= 108\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the area of the rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea of Rectangle &= base \\cdot height \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the total area, we subtract the area of the rectangle from the area of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTotal Area &= Area of Parallelogram - Area of Rectangle \\\\\n&= 108 - 18 \\\\\n&= 90\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147285 1069 "e-2644" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147285 1071 "Margaret used two triangles to form the letter *v*. The letter is shown in the diagram below. What is the area of this letter?\n\n![](images/32/3283b5aa07c8dac9913f894fdb540a367f8e9e81541928947b185e391463803b.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147285 1072 #uuid "0ab26f23-67af-517f-92a9-9d9a67daec4f" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147285 1074 "The shaded region is a large triangle with a smaller triangle cut out of it.\n\nTo find the total area, we need to subtract the area of the inner triangle from the area of the outer triangle.\n\nFirst, we find the area of the outer triangle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea of Outer Triangle &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot base \\cdot height \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 8 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= \\frac{64}{2} \\\\\n&= 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the area of the inner triangle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea of Inner Triangle &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot base \\cdot height \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 4 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{2} \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the total area, we subtract the area of the inner triangle from the area of the outer triangle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTotal Area &= Area of Outer Triangle - Area of Inner Triangle \\\\\n&= 32 - 8 \\\\\n&= 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the units. Since all lengths are measured in centimeters, the area is measured in square centimeters. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Total Area} = 24 \\, \\textrm{cm}^2\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147286 1069 "e-2645" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147286 1071 "Rose pitches a tent in her back yard, shown below. What area of the yard isn't taken up by the tent?\n\n![](images/b9/b9d2c1110823a295ae3f6c767657415c2a200f97a17a324a5e1123e2d7506cb4.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147286 1072 #uuid "411a9410-ee93-57b3-8773-67993ca3eb1d" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147286 1074 "The area of the yard not taken up by Rose's tent corresponds to the shaded region. This region is a rectangle with a triangle cut out.\n\nTo find the total area, we need to subtract the area of the inner shape (the triangle) from the area of the outer shape (the rectangle).\n\nFirst, we find the area of the rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea of Rectangle &= base \\cdot height \\\\\n&= 12 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 96\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the area of the triangle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea of Triangle &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot base \\cdot height \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the total area, we subtract the area of the triangle from the area of the rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTotal Area &= Area of Rectangle - Area of Triangle \\\\\n&= 96 - 2 \\\\\n&= 94\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the units. Since all lengths are measured in meters, the area is measured in square meters. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Total Area} = 94 \\, \\textrm{m}^2\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147287 1069 "e-2646" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147287 1071 "Find the area of the shaded region.\n\n![](images/31/31793f8ec07adf13b446fac4b1b3abe498e385f701b211ca1cddc4413d04d6de.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147287 1072 #uuid "9a541769-2bc0-5a46-a71e-c96a165ee71d" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147287 1074 "This shaded region is a trapezoid with a square cut out.\n\nTo find the total area, we need to subtract the area of the inner shape (the square) from the area of the outer shape (the trapezoid).\n\nFirst, we find the area of the trapezoid. The trapezoid consists of a square and a triangle.\n\n![](images/82/82a0a59e94a60361cdfbf6e493d0ed2aa219fd5bfdff5a55eb1eea283b94fcc0.png)\n\nThe area of the rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea of Rectangle &= base \\cdot height \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&= 54\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea of Triangle &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot base \\cdot height \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&= \\frac{54}{2} \\\\\n&= 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the area of the trapezoid, we add the area of the rectangle and the area of the triangle together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea of Trapezoid &= 54 + 27 = 81\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the area of the square is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea of Square &= base \\cdot height \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the total area of the shaded region, we subtract the area of the square from the area of the trapezoid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTotal Area &= Area of Trapezoid - Area of Square \\\\\n&= 81 - 9 \\\\\n&= 72\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the units. Since all lengths are measured in millimeters, the area is measured in square millimeters. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Total Area} = 72 \\, \\textrm{mm}^2\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147288 1069 "e-2652" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147288 1071 "A triangle $ABC$ has vertices at $A(-2,0)$, $B(4,0)$, and $C(1,3)$. Plot the triangle." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147288 1072 #uuid "93918a56-0264-5b6a-8477-e168ba324b04" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147288 1074 "First, we plot the given points.\n\n![](images/81/813488d80c0452bf815da96c03c327e4e2ccc920ee98931a9fdebded03db8fa3.png)\n\nThen, we connect the consecutive points by line segments.\n\n![](images/e4/e434e30054a9026ede912a44a830985489a3dd0dc6dbe877f8f241283d0450dd.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147289 1069 "e-2653" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147289 1071 "A parallelogram $KLMN$ has vertices at $K(-7,0)$, $L(-4,0)$, $M(-2,5)$, and $D(-5,5)$. What is the length of the side $MN$?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147289 1072 #uuid "6f564697-8f2f-58da-81ba-27c7e834a7b1" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147289 1074 "Let's draw this parallelogram in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/75/75d0645d88d46ec7432cc60e8699c1c6e861f431d57301c21fb46d5275a6bf57.png)\n\nThe length of $MN$ and the length of $KL$ are the same.\n\nFrom the diagram, we can see that\n\n- the distance from $L$ to the $y$-axis is $\\color{red}4$, and\n- the distance from $K$ to the $y$-axis is $\\color{blue}7$.\n\nTherefore, the length of both $MN$ and $KL$ is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{7}}-{\\color{blue}{4}}=3\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147290 1069 "e-2654" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147290 1071 "A square $ABCD$ has vertices at $A(2,-2)$, $B(8,-2)$, $C(8,4)$, and $D(2,4)$. What is the length of the side $BC$?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147290 1072 #uuid "affa7053-406e-54ca-be3e-fe330b227b40" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147290 1074 "Let's draw this square in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/32/322e87308370105e789cd19f8bfcc292be0466deb909368b63c60f034711cf3e.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we can see that\n\n- the distance from $B$ to the $x$-axis is $\\color{red}2$, and\n- the distance from $C$ to the $x$-axis is $\\color{blue}4$.\n\nTherefore, the length of $BC$ is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}+{\\color{blue}{4}}=6\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147291 1069 "e-2656" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147291 1071 "A rectangle $KLMN$ has vertices at $K(-2,1)$, $L(3,1)$, and $M(3,4)$. What are the coordinates of the vertex $N$?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147291 1072 #uuid "cb3d6ef7-59ae-5937-b68d-d642176760c5" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147291 1074 "First, we plot the points $K(-2,1)$, $L(3,1)$, and $N(3,4)$ and connect them:\n\n![](images/c7/c7f28c0a3c6803a72162e13441a67e433de4fe56c3725d8c28a929f03d1e3a47.png)\n\nTo find $N$ we draw a vertical line from $K$ and a horizontal line from $M$.\n\n![](images/e8/e8b809cab3df88bf7c04f5237e50790672bdb6a5f5d724449e823d88e7fe80ef.png)\n\nThe coordinates of $N$ are $({\\color{blue}{-2}},{\\color{red}{4}})$.\n\nNotice that $N$ has the same $x$-coordinate as $K$ and the same $y$-coordinate as $M$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147292 1069 "e-2657" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147292 1071 "What is the perimeter of the rectangle $ABCD$?\n\n![](images/2f/2f619855103420a223fcc1a0b1d93b9565b88129d3334e369c6c89898ee67042.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147292 1072 #uuid "e6ee2442-6533-524c-b27e-a336ae5d316f" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147292 1074 "The rectangle has vertices $A(3,1)$, $B(5,1)$, $C(5,4)$, and $D(3,4)$.\n\nIts base is $2$ and its height is $3$.\n\n![](images/ee/ee4cd98acc76802d5d0f7fd0da80164779b28ff05ae7937d57d6d389e35b6d86.png)\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the rectangle is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPerimeter &= 2 \\cdot (base + height) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (2 + 3) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147293 1069 "e-2658" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147293 1071 "Find the area of the triangle $BCD$.\n\n![](images/7a/7a897c8c7311411c468a2f3809d2391b7e36d7138fa3841a7235f1571c8d42c8.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147293 1072 #uuid "249b3255-a4f9-5cb2-af5a-1eb186bc20c2" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147293 1074 "The triangle has vertices $B(5,1)$, $C(5,-4)$, and $D(-1,-4)$.\n\nIts base is $6$ and its height is $5$.\n\n![](images/76/766ef5d1491bb8157bdcd1f6b770e24de83b587eb2ff032fe2b9eab79119d87e.png)\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot base \\cdot height \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147294 1069 "e-2660" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147294 1071 "A parallelogram $ABCD$ has vertices at $A(2,-1)$, $B(5,-1)$, $C(4,-4)$, and $D(1,-4)$. What is its area?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147294 1072 #uuid "da374ee9-5a09-52a2-b4cd-7065cbd0ae03" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147294 1074 "First, we plot the parallelogram:\n\n![](images/7a/7a579246b61b9da7c70c02949063fde9941bd3fc44f49f38ae297b6f493c211c.png)\n\nThe parallelogram has a base of $3$ and a height of $3$.\n\n![](images/95/9552f8974df6f4239cb4c6fa855b0308a379959d49bddfe707b2d42b752916e7.png)\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea &= base \\cdot height \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147295 1069 "e-2661" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147295 1071 "Sophia's garden is shaped like the figure shown above. To water her garden, she uses $5\\,\\textrm{l}$ of water for every square meter. How much water does Sophia use to water her entire garden?\n\n![](images/4b/4b7b6799501266ed8be70c3b3e66ecd1a5aa50a355e56aeeda13e0a67c567493.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147295 1072 #uuid "8b57c547-0c50-52d6-9219-67b8eab00335" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147295 1074 "We can see that the garden is shaped like a right triangle with a base of $6\\,\\textrm{m}$ and a height of $4\\,\\textrm{m}$.\n\n![](images/98/980dfd2ab2afdb2191809aa64a4805c1b5a211ae6f586bdc4dbb7e61e215eee1.png)\n\nSo, the area of the garden is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot base \\cdot height \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the units. Since base and height are measured in meters, the area is measured in square meters. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area} = 12 \\, \\textrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nSince Sophia needs $5$ liters of water to water every square meter of her garden, the total amount of water she will need to water her garden is\n\n$$\n5 \\times 12 = 60 \\, \\textrm{l}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147296 1069 "e-2662" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147296 1071 "What is the name of the polyhedron shown below?\n\n![](images/b6/b6b4021c8add7d92bfbc98c23f9c9e56e4fd0c8fa152e60c04ca78c5c5b594cd.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147296 1072 #uuid "319d252c-a8c4-5a5f-ad2b-f4bdb5702b75" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147296 1074 "The polyhedron is a **heptagonal prism** since it has two identical heptagonal faces connected by equal parallel line segments.\n\nAlso, notice that:\n\n- The solid is not an octagonal (pentagonal or hexagonal) prism since it does not have an octagonal (pentagonal or hexagonal) base.\n- The solid is not a cube since not all of its faces are squares." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147297 1069 "e-2668" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147297 1071 "Which of the following is **not** a polyhedron?\n\n![](images/ce/ceedc03f96a3c801f31069f5551a8c0e5241dbabfc5c9463240a1eb1698a7105.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147297 1072 #uuid "0583c82a-049c-51e6-aebc-bcd32423824e" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147297 1074 "Each face in a polyhedron must be a polygon. Therefore, the only option that is **not** a polyhedron is:\n\n![](images/ae/aea63ad8ff248347de606d37112355cdecc30ab5ae631b987b87f0b9f9d68f17.png)\n\nNotice that one of its faces is not a polygon (it has curved sides)." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147298 1069 "e-2676" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147298 1071 "Which of the following are statistical questions?\n\n1. How many people typically watch ABC News on a Saturday evening?\n2. How old was Abraham Lincoln when he became president of the United States of America?\n3. How tall was Miles on his 5th birthday?\n4. What is the height of a randomly selected oak tree?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147298 1072 #uuid "dfe4d0e0-e56d-5f06-8809-e48c2cafdbb4" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147298 1074 "Let's examine the items one by one:\n\n- Question I is a statistical question:We can answer the question by collecting data. In this case, we need to collect data on the number of people who watch the news.There is variability in the data. We expect the number of people who watch the news on Saturday evening will vary from week to week.\n- Question II is not a statistical question. There is only one answer (Abraham Lincoln was $52$ when he became president), and there is no variability.\n- Question III is not a statistical question. There is only one answer, and there is no variability.\n- Question IV is a statistical question:We can answer the question by collecting data. In this case, we need to collect data about the heights of oak trees.There is variability in the data. The height of an oak tree varies from tree to tree.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147299 1069 "e-2677" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147299 1071 "Which of the following are **NOT** statistical questions?\n\n1. How many words are there in the poem \"From a Railway Carriage\" by Robert Louis Stevenson?\n2. How many yards are in a mile?\n3. How long does it typically take to read the novel \"Charlotte's Web\" by E. B. White?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147299 1072 #uuid "b5b64699-9bbd-5c5d-a513-ca07d9854797" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147299 1074 "Let's examine the items one by one:\n\n- Question I is not a statistical question. There is only one answer, and there is no variability in that answer.\n\n- Question II is not a statistical question. There is only one answer ($1\\, 760$ yards equals one mile), and there is no variability to that answer.\n- Question III is a statistical question:We can answer the question by collecting data. In this case, we should collect data from several people who have read the book.There is variability in the data. The time spent reading the book will depend on each person.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147300 1069 "e-2757" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147300 1071 "![](images/be/beed0799de4f42cde58bf0c85f31a1a6850ef1227ea2827e9819a4edeb3ede4a.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the polyhedron shown above?\n\n1. Hexagonal prism\n2. Hexagonal pyramid\n3. Rectangular prism" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147300 1072 #uuid "dc558592-e65f-5369-890d-fffc633f2a3b" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147300 1074 "The solid is a **hexagonal pyramid** since its base is a hexagon and all other $6$ faces meet at a single point\n\nAlso, notice that:\n\n- The solid is not a hexagonal prism since it doesn't have two identical hexagonal faces connected by equal parallel lines.\n- The solid is not a rectangular prism since not all of its faces are rectangles." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147301 1069 "e-2759" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147301 1071 "The net of which polyhedron is shown below?\n\n![](images/96/96e7889a74d2d5bd35634ec022d3c9020d23fd7292b3bd7c05f8722860389645.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147301 1072 #uuid "8bca178c-5495-5205-b213-c75b8a4df3de" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147301 1074 "We fold the net along its edges to form a solid shape, as shown below.\n\n![](images/98/98ee3216b37bdce0081dd4daad1895cace7998a03d41c183184d07f6afaac126.png)\n\nAs a result, we get a triangular pyramid:\n\n![](images/32/32bdc8f811ca46a184307fa5521cb145cb0add8a0b4ee2bfae25add881561475.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147302 1069 "e-2760" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147302 1071 "What is the surface area of the triangular prism represented by the net below?\n\n![](images/da/dafb07440f60418465b579aad73119cd47d6def34ed0cf1229afc9affbb177e4.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147302 1072 #uuid "0803c5b9-51e2-5d4e-9c8f-cd8d575a7bec" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147302 1074 "The surface area of our prism consists of $2$ triangles (base and top) and $3$ rectangles (side faces).\n\nAdding the areas, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSurface area &= 2 \\cdot (Area of base) + 3 \\cdot (Area of side) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 12 + 3 \\cdot 48 \\\\\n&= 24 + 144 \\\\\n&= 168cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147303 1069 "e-2761" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147303 1071 "![](images/f2/f233556f9055563f3956eea7eaf9744fb20993084f4c3eeb5e2e35d730d8d2aa.png)\n\nWhich of the nets above is the net of a rectangular prism?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147303 1072 #uuid "4501bf84-de3a-581f-b717-10996a285921" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147303 1074 "![](images/5f/5fc1630e4e44bf68f833004ab975e406b8734fe062849484165310e093d14155.png)\n\nIf we were to fold the net above along its edges, then we'd get a rectangular prism, as shown below:\n\n![](images/c2/c2f6985ca8cd95a71409936ede80c6f257e863bd459fe5a27178a04ea3edb88c.png)\n\nNotice that the other two nets have triangular faces, so they cannot be the nets of a rectangular prism.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is I." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147304 1069 "e-2762" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147304 1071 "Maggy is building a pyramid from a cardboard net, shown below. What will be the total surface area of the pyramid?\n\n![](images/29/29c918514e60da13363883770befd9d80098deaba0b45cfcafa3255feb143261.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147304 1072 #uuid "c91d73c6-cb1f-55d2-986b-83f94a6a66ed" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147304 1074 "The net shown corresponds to a triangular pyramid. The surface area of our pyramid consists of $1$ triangular base and $3$ triangular sides.\n\nAdding the areas, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSurface area &= 1 \\cdot (Area of base) + 3 \\cdot (Area of side) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 10 + 3 \\cdot 12 \\\\\n&= 10 + 36 \\\\\n&= 46in^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147305 1069 "e-2764" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147305 1071 "What is the volume of the rectangular prism shown below?\n\n![](images/55/5505bfbdc07b237e53681dba06ee62a2e3ea5b3be821d5f7201a860f8f57c0ce.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147305 1072 #uuid "a3f5f627-cfc6-5214-970f-248a1454bfe1" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147305 1074 "The rectangular prism has a length of $2\\,\\text{cm}$, a width of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\\,\\text{cm}\n$$\n\n, and a height of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\text{cm}\n$$\n\nSo, the volume of the rectangular prism is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= length \\cdot width \\cdot height \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{3}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{1} \\cdot \\frac{3}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2}{1 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now simplify the above fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $12$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12 \\div 12}{12 \\div 12}= \\dfrac{1}{1} = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the rectangular prism is $1 \\,\\text{cm}^3$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147306 1069 "e-2766" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147306 1071 "![](images/84/84f39f9d765a61db544fdddc7ee79859ab4a54c2e6f0e5018512723ca0dfcfd9.png)\n\nWhich of the nets above is **not** the net of a pyramid?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147306 1072 #uuid "1e23f3ac-bcec-5070-b135-b2285d326bc4" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147306 1074 "![](images/72/72462d252e84e20d567ccd10c2be12a6496f442f08d6e6baa609c5fb74f6fcc5.png)\n\nIf we were to fold the net above along its edges, then we'd get a triangular prism, as shown below:\n\n![](images/8e/8e77623f767c9cea456b787a09465397a4360915c547911a55927d8f7c9b4885.png)\n\nNotice that the other two nets give us pyramids. Therefore, the correct answer is I." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147307 1069 "e-2767" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147307 1071 "Find the volume of the figure shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6238c3cc741d89a1720297ba483bfc34a1e5325c8a089a89f715ce811c796f3.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147307 1072 #uuid "c39bd1e8-7857-55ad-aa87-a987f8efb186" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147307 1074 "The picture shows a rectangular prism, where the area of the base is $4 \\, \\text{m}^2$ and the height is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3} \\, \\text{m}\n$$\n\nSo, the volume of the rectangular prism is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= base \\cdot height \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 \\cdot 4}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{16}{3} \\, \\text{m}^3\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147308 1069 "e-2769" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147308 1071 "![](images/1b/1b090ba72b23a7f291238a7dc09d9461c7f85aac8df6d276bc37fd44563345e3.png)\n\nDetermine the volume of the figure shown above." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147308 1072 #uuid "5b7f3a39-81a2-52ec-9a44-c5fc918666f9" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147308 1074 "The rectangular prism has a length of $3 \\, \\text{in}$, a width of\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac{1}{3} \\,\\text{in}\n$$\n\n, and a height of $2\\,\\text{in}$.\n\nSo, the volume of the rectangular prism is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= length \\cdot width \\cdot height \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 1\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{(1 \\times 3) + 1}{3} = \\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 3 \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 2}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{24}{3} \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is $8 \\,\\text{in}^3$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147309 1069 "e-2773" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147309 1071 "![](images/e4/e427a98c2953792d799190b7fc20a7dbf12ce014655316e21fb809e0abc9cdc5.png)\n\nWhich of the statements is true about the solids shown above?\n\n1. The net of solid I consists of triangles only.\n2. The net of solid II consists of $4$ triangles and $2$ rectangles.\n3. The net of solid III consists of triangles only." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147309 1072 #uuid "1b29a574-ac81-53cf-8dd7-3030e1f9bef3" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147309 1074 "Let's sketch the net of each solid.\n\n![](images/02/023f11b6924e6cf7318f54f5acdf910b1f688ee8ac40ec8f3b3ab7cd9c13c8df.png)\n\nNow, let's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false because the net of solid I consists of rectangles.\n- Statement II is false because the net of solid II consists of $2$ triangles and $3$ rectangles.\n- Statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147310 1069 "e-2780" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147310 1071 "![](images/fe/fe43d65b35d583b21f25977a738e758a31d6b04eb08957877879cc54eef3f45f.png)\n\nBy assuming that all of the side faces have the same height $(8\\,\\text{in})$, find the total surface area of the square pyramid above by considering its net." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147310 1072 #uuid "80466688-43e1-5697-976c-6ba63844fed9" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147310 1074 "The net of our pyramid is as follows:\n\n![](images/c4/c4a5fe995aaaf47b7ab2b90492059d739fc2352955dc1e12a71852fc13046a33.png)\n\nFirst, notice that the base of the pyramid is a square with sides equal to $5 \\, \\text{in}$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of square} = 5 \\times 5 = 25 \\, \\text{in}^2\n$$\n\nEach side face of our solid is triangle, where the base is $5\\,\\text{in}$ and the height is $8\\,\\text{in}$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of triangle} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 5 \\times 8 = 20 \\, \\text{in}^2\n$$\n\nFinally, the surface area of the pyramid consists of $1$ square (the base) and $4$ triangles (the side faces).\n\nAdding the areas, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSurface area &= 1 \\cdot (Area of the square) + 4 \\cdot (Area of the triangle) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 25 + 4 \\cdot 20 \\\\\n&= 25 + 80 \\\\\n&= 105in^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147311 1069 "e-2781" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147311 1071 "Heather surveyed the amount of milk consumed per week by several households. The results are shown in the following frequency table.\n\n| Liters of Milk | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $4$ |\n| $4$ | $6$ |\n| $6$ | $10$ |\n| $8$ | $15$ |\n\nHow many respondents said they consumed $6$ liters of milk per week?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147311 1072 #uuid "c2d8c3fc-6ab2-5a7e-88c6-629c3210d43d" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147311 1074 "First, let's highlight the relevant row in the table.\n\n| Liters of Milk | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $2$ | $4$ |\n| $4$ | $6$ |\n| $\\color{red}6$ | $\\color{blue}10$ |\n| $8$ | $15$ |\n\nFrom the table, we can see that $\\color{blue}10$ households consume $\\color{red}6$ liters of milk per week." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147312 1069 "e-2782" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147312 1071 "Mary asked some family members how many glasses of water they drink daily. The results are shown in the following frequency table.\n\n| Glasses of Water | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1-3$ | $4$ |\n| $4-6$ | $8$ |\n| $7-9$ | $5$ |\n| $10-12$ | $1$ |\n\nHow many family members did Mary consult?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147312 1072 #uuid "4e15ecdc-55ab-5b5b-b732-2a130dbd80a9" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147312 1074 "From the frequency table:\n\n- $\\color{blue}4$ of Mary's family members drink between $1$ and $3$ glasses per day\n- $\\color{blue}8$ of Mary's family members drink between $4$ and $6$ glasses per day\n- $\\color{blue}5$ of Mary's family members drink between $7$ and $9$ glasses per day\n- $\\color{blue}1$ of Mary's family members drink between $10$ and $12$ glasses per day\n\nTo find out the total number of family members that Mary consulted, we add up the frequencies:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}4} + {\\color{blue}8} + {\\color{blue}5} + {\\color{blue}1} = 18\n$$\n\nSo, Mary consulted a total of $18$ members of her family." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147313 1069 "e-2783" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147313 1071 "Mike, a sales consultant, recorded the number of smartphones sold by a store each hour over a certain period. The results are given below:\n\n$5, \\: 15, \\: 22, \\: 27, \\: 35, \\: 40, \\: 8, \\: 19$\n\nWhat is the missing number in the frequency table below representing the same data?\n\n| Number of Smartphones | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1-15$ | $3$ |\n| $16-30$ | $0$ |\n| $31-45$ | $2$ |" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147313 1072 #uuid "fdcf8f92-9272-52b8-8d78-96a3a7ef1aad" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147313 1074 "We need to count the number of smartphones for the group $16-30$.\n\nFrom the data, there were $\\color{blue}3$ hours when the number of smartphones sold was between $16$ and $30$:\n\n$$\n5, \\: 15, \\: {\\color{blue}\\underline{22}}, \\: {\\color{blue}\\underline{27}}, \\: 35, \\: 40, \\: 8, \\: {\\color{blue}\\underline{19}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing value is $\\color{blue}3$.\n\n| Number of Smartphones | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1-15$ | $3$ |\n| $16-30$ | $3$ |\n| $31-45$ | $2$ |" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147314 1069 "e-2786" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147314 1071 "The following frequency table shows the number of home runs scored by the players of a baseball team during some playoffs. How many players batted between $4$ and $9$ home runs?\n\n| Home Runs | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1-3$ | $3$ |\n| $4-6$ | $2$ |\n| $7-9$ | $4$ |\n| $10-12$ | $1$ |" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147314 1072 #uuid "7ccb9299-b0d6-5e56-bf39-41a51637d3b9" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147314 1074 "| Home Runs | Frequency |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1-3$ | $3$ |\n| $4-6$ | $\\color{blue}2$ |\n| $7-9$ | $\\color{blue}4$ |\n| $10-12$ | $1$ |\n\nFrom the frequency table:\n\n- $\\color{blue}2$ players batted between $4-6$ home runs\n- $\\color{blue}4$ players batted between $7-9$ home runs\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} +{\\color{blue}4} =6\n$$\n\nplayers batted between $4$ and $9$ home runs." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147315 1069 "e-2788" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147315 1071 "The dot plot below gives the distance (to the nearest kilometer) ran by a group of joggers one day. Each dot represents a single jogger.\n\n![](images/c1/c1b346441fb986f7a09c7393f8dc1c41454cbf206db2eb799ea194f04b18847e.png)\n\nThe frequency table below represents the same information. What number is missing from the table?\n\n| Distance (in kilometers) | Number of Joggers |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $0$ |\n| $4$ | $1$ |" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147315 1072 #uuid "9449b21d-de5b-528c-bbf6-a84e773d39e7" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147315 1074 "![](images/da/dacb292d6648b6298b6dca3e631291d5c383c4f4ee55946c7ab660aeaa789445.png)\n\nNotice the $3$rd column of the dot plot. It tells us that there is $\\color{blue}1$ jogger who ran $3$ kilometers.\n\nTherefore, the missing value is $\\color{blue}1$.\n\n| Distance (in kilometers) | Number of Joggers |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $1$ |\n| $4$ | $1$ |" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147316 1069 "e-2790" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147316 1071 "![](images/69/69c93780f7b3e1b09d6742d85033671ac9637a9a12ae6c43da1c9bc850cb7b9a.png)\n\nThe dot plot above shows the number of aces scored by each participant in a tennis tournament. Each dot represents a single competitor. What is the mean number of aces the competitors scored?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147316 1072 #uuid "9418c009-c5a0-5399-86d7-dce4a5ab8a9b" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147316 1074 "The dot plot tells us the following:\n\n- $\\color{blue}3$ competitors scored $1$ ace\n- $\\color{blue}2$ competitors scored $4$ aces\n- $\\color{blue}2$ competitors scored $5$ aces\n\nSo, we get the following data:\n\n$$\n1, \\: 1, \\: 1, \\: 4, \\: 4, \\ 5, \\ 5\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the mean:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmean &= ((1 \\cdot 3) + (4 \\cdot 2) + (5 \\cdot 2))/(7) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + 8 + 10}{7} \\\\\n&= \\frac{21}{7} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean number of aces is $3$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147317 1069 "e-2791" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147317 1071 "The dot plot below shows the results of a survey of a group of people, asking them how many hours per day they spend reading. How many respondents spend no more than one hour per day reading?\n\n![](images/ab/ab9361eabadfa20752280181b463ea2a68838d29aa623652fccedab9e18cb403.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147317 1072 #uuid "493361d2-1654-5623-bb95-45ac070719a6" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147317 1074 "The dot plot tells us the following:\n\n- $\\color{blue}4$ of the respondents spend $0$ hours per day reading ($4$ dots over $0$)\n- $\\color{blue}5$ of the respondents spend $1$ hour per day reading ($5$ dot over $1$)\n- $3$ of the respondents spend $2$ hours per day reading ($3$ dots over $2$)\n- $2$ of the respondents spend $3$ hour per day reading ($2$ dot over $3$)\n- $1$ of the respondents spends $4$ hours per day reading ($1$ dots over $4$)\n\n![](images/55/55b512528410aacb4dbf71015ba6546213ece4866a1cf9dc293d55745e6b9202.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}4} + {\\color{blue}5} = 9\n$$\n\nof the respondents spend no more than one hour per day reading." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147318 1069 "e-2793" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147318 1071 "The dot plot below shows the distribution of scores on a language quiz. Each dot represents a single student. What is the modal score?\n\n![](images/16/16409f7823875546013a01ee60f5c6c0b3bc9e4dbcd66fe764d2ddd8b90c9bf3.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147318 1072 #uuid "5cba0e78-332c-50a8-8ace-80c238178fb6" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147318 1074 "The mode is the value that occurs most often in the data set.\n\nAccording to our plot, the value $\\color{blue}80$ appears more frequently than the others ($\\color{red}4$ times in total).\n\n![](images/e0/e02384c1eecdf92096446cdca1144cbc943f8339db077a32cc87249dda8435ff.png)\n\nTherefore, the modal score is $80$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147319 1069 "e-2796" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147319 1071 "Draw a dot plot that represents the data set shown below.\n$2, \\: 1, \\: 1, \\: 2, \\: 5, \\: 1, \\: 2, \\: 2$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147319 1072 #uuid "80931f4d-7a89-59f8-bde8-398eb5cf049f" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147319 1074 "First, notice that we have three values in our data:\n\n$\\qquad$ $1$, $2$, and $5$.\n\nNext, we draw a number line from the smallest to the greatest value:\n\n![](images/ca/ca6c267167536ddc7dfb4b42518de69e973742e98cfb4c238203bf564e982ce6.png)\n\nThe data tells us the following:\n\n- The number $1$ appears three times. So, there will be $\\color{blue}3$ dots over $1$ on the number line.\n$2, \\: {\\color{blue}\\underline{1}}, \\: {\\color{blue}\\underline{1}}, \\: 2, \\: 5, \\: {\\color{blue}\\underline{1}}, \\: 2, \\: 2$\n- The number $2$ appears four times. So, there will be $\\color{blue}4$ dots over $2$ on the number line.\n${\\color{blue}\\underline{2}}, \\: 1, \\: 1, \\: {\\color{blue}\\underline{2}}, \\: 5, \\: 1, \\: {\\color{blue}\\underline{2}}, \\: {\\color{blue}\\underline{2}}$\n- The number $5$ appears once. So, there will be $\\color{blue}1$ dot over $5$ on the number line.\n$2, \\: 1, \\: 1, \\: 2, \\: {\\color{blue}\\underline{5}}, \\: 1, \\: 2, \\, 2$\n\nTherefore, we get the dot plot shown below.\n\n![](images/c8/c8f2f68473568bf728f1bb1fea99c6288ddaa761d0e6bdc9edeeec55c292e690.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147320 1069 "e-2797" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147320 1071 "The dot plot below shows the number of circus tickets bought by families in a particular neighborhood. Each dot represents a single family. What is the median of the distribution?\n\n![](images/ef/efbfbb3487ee1f18947756103e6a5d3af9a0416cf40f4350e08e536e8e034295.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147320 1072 #uuid "3e1804bb-4d93-54f4-81af-0acf29b30ba6" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147320 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nSince we have an even number of data points ($16$ in total), the median is the mean of the two middle values (the $\\color{blue}8$th and $\\color{blue}9$th, respectively).\n\n![](images/12/1297a72f30bf3fadbbab1da1bc6d9bfa888ea7c08bd811a8b14f529931c848c8.png)\n\nTherefore, the median is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{median} = \\dfrac{3+3}{2} = \\dfrac62 = 3\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nThe dot plot tells us the following:\n\n- $\\color{blue}2$ families purchased $1$ ticket\n- $\\color{blue}3$ families purchased $2$ tickets\n- $\\color{blue}5$ families purchased $3$ tickets\n- $\\color{blue}6$ families purchased $4$ tickets\n\nThis gives the following data:\n$1, \\: 1, \\: 2, \\: 2, \\: 2, \\: 3, \\: 3, \\: {\\color{blue}\\underline{3}}, \\: {\\color{blue}\\underline{3}}, \\: 3, \\: 4, \\: 4, \\: 4, \\: 4, \\: 4, \\: 4$\nSince we have an even number of data points ($16$ in total), the median is the mean of the two middle values (the $\\color{blue}8$th and $\\color{blue}9$th, respectively).\n\nTherefore, the median is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{median} = \\dfrac{3+3}{2} = \\dfrac62 = 3\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147321 1069 "e-2800" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147321 1071 "![](images/a1/a1e96120abfc8beaca73ed8922ceb252683126b1a7e101a6687cf21b70c68a0a.png)\n\nHow many outliers are there for the data set whose dot plot is shown above?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147321 1072 #uuid "11968f90-41f3-53fc-8d00-30f3a41da034" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147321 1074 "Notice that most values are concentrated between $25$ and $40$, but one value is separated from the main group.\n\n![](images/3a/3a7597e6ebc6e5ac7ad0e11513f0d9649b63d7148d16d1d527f00b1249bc7535.png)\n\nThe value $\\color{blue}5$ is the outlier of the distribution. Therefore, there is $1$ outlier." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147322 1069 "e-2802" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147322 1071 "The dot plot below shows the number of games a group of competitors won during a chess tournament. Each dot represents a different competitor. What is the range in the number of games won by the members of this group?\n\n![](images/94/94aa86fd0eca249f915feb93a72e93fecefbee4f5adde18d626206824aade9ad.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147322 1072 #uuid "43b6cb02-4054-5031-be12-100138be47ec" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147322 1074 "The range of a data set is the difference between the greatest and the smallest values.\n\nAccording to the plot, the smallest value is $\\color{red}8$ and the greatest is $\\color{blue}16$.\n\n![](images/5d/5d1833aa3f288c273b2a54045a6f59269f69328406fec363f4c610552fc70d38.png)\n\nTherefore, the range of the data set is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\text{greatest}} - {\\color{red}\\text{smallest}} = {\\color{blue}16} - {\\color{red}8} = 8\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147323 1069 "e-2804" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147323 1071 "The box plot below represents the distribution of the daily wages of the workers in a fast-food restaurant. What is the median of the data set?\n\n![](images/40/4096acf86e77474ed46b9c17ee6550090efacc098dfae204d050d8b6532e0d7e.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147323 1072 #uuid "3803a537-4e6b-55f0-afd8-161384f26ee6" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147323 1074 "The median is represented by the vertical line that goes through the box.\n\nIn our case, the median line corresponds to $60$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/76/76984f522ca60b5b7d60e394e2e487522ca10ae7e173e37128000349e299e5eb.png)\n\nTherefore, the median daily wage of the workers is $\\[math] 60$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147324 1069 "e-2806" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147324 1071 "A certain data set has the features listed in the table below.\n\n| **Smallest Value** | **Lower Quartile** | **Median** | **Upper Quartile** | **Greatest Value** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $4$ | $6$ | $9$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n\nDraw a box plot for this data." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147324 1072 #uuid "63188b8d-5acb-5223-a6a2-bf37153c65f0" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147324 1074 "Using the data from the table, we obtain the following boxplot:\n\n![](images/13/136486e2f7bbbda1319836e1c1113ef85b5e32f3fc8a29a54ed49123e9fa8c68.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147325 1069 "e-2807" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147325 1071 "![](images/d8/d851d3de6d66138dc1d99d739707ac76053351f5acb3ae754f4b39c4948aa76b.png)\n\nThe dot plot above shows the working hours of a small group of employees. Each dot represents a single employee. What is the shape of the distribution?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147325 1072 #uuid "d73e7f41-bb33-5475-83be-1a0d9a57d0c9" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147325 1074 "From the dot plot, we see that the distribution is left-skewed. It has a long tail on the left-hand side.\n\n![](images/d6/d6f32ecdfe818266326384b62428c92ea5c5846b314b5db1a1b90cceb5ca8838.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147326 1069 "e-2808" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147326 1071 "The dot plot below shows the total time members of a rock band practiced their instruments last week. Each dot represents a different band member. What is the mean absolute deviation in the band members' practice times?\n\n![](images/c0/c02f5d5c0919d53727de8dced67d986e10c6fdaf3f0dc8288c0a29edfc4c54d7.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147326 1072 #uuid "0eb9dcfe-52d1-5c63-8d9d-bc90d45724cc" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147326 1074 "The dot plot tells us the following:\n\n- $1$ member practiced for $3$ hours\n- $1$ member practiced for $4$ hours\n- $1$ member practiced for $5$ hours\n- $2$ members practiced for $6$ hours\n- $1$ member practiced for $9$ hours\n\nSo, we get the following data:\n\n$$\n3, \\: 4, \\: 5, \\: 6, \\: 6, \\: 9\n$$\n\nNow, we find the mean of the data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmean &= ((3 \\cdot 1) + (4 \\cdot 1) + (5 \\cdot 1) + (6 \\cdot 2) + (9 \\cdot 1))/(6) \\\\\n&= \\frac{33}{6} \\\\\n&= 5.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the mean from each number in the data set, find the absolute value of the results, and add all these together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}3 - 5.5 & + & 4 - 5.5 & + & 5 - 5.5 & + & 6 - 5.5 & + & 6 - 5.5 & + & 9 - 5.5\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}-2.5 & + & -1.5 & + & -0.5 & + & 0.5 & + & 0.5 & + & 3.5\\end{vmatrix} &=  \\\\\n2.5 + 1.5 + 0.5 + 0.5 + 0.5 + 3.5 &=  \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the mean absolute deviation (MAD), we divide the sum obtained above (${\\color{red}9}$) by the total number of data points ($6$). This gives\n\n$$\n\\text{MAD} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}9}{6} = 1.5\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147327 1069 "e-2810" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147327 1071 "The frequency table below gives the weights, measured to the nearest kilogram, of some students in a statistics class.\n\n| Weight (kg) | Number of Students |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $50{-}59$ | $6$ |\n| $60{-}69$ | $10$ |\n| $70{-}79$ | $8$ |\n| $80{-}89$ | $2$ |\n\nDraw a histogram that represents the same data set." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147327 1072 #uuid "51d95da8-553b-5e4b-beea-6639a6752e9b" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147327 1074 "From the frequency table:\n\n- $\\color{blue}6$ students had weights in the range $50{-}59$ kilograms,\n- $\\color{blue}10$ students had weights in the range $60{-}69$ kilograms,\n- $\\color{blue}8$ students had weights in the range $70{-}79$ kilograms, and\n- $\\color{blue}2$ students had weights in the range $80{-}89$ kilograms.\n\nThe histogram that represents this information is the following:\n\n![](images/2d/2dccbe5c0c3927c35ae013b4ec56b913f402c518db254871482ced1fbb3880c5.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147328 1069 "e-2814" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147328 1071 "The histogram below shows the height distribution, to the nearest centimeter, of a group of high school baseball players.\n\n![](images/a2/a28a445a08e30673a44cc3ea5df934c64c0d36fd29c99f46e35caf1e4fad5b9c.png)\n\nThe frequency table below represents the same information. From left to right, what is missing from the table?\n\n| Height (cm) | Number of Players |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $161{-}170$ | $6$ |\n| $000-000$ | $0$ |\n| $181{-}190$ | $3$ |" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147328 1072 #uuid "9176ae5c-36a7-59fc-a98e-adacf9e42d6d" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147328 1074 "The second column of the histogram tells us that there are $\\color{blue}9$ players whose height is in the range of ${\\color{blue}171{-}180} \\, \\text{cm}$.\n\n![](images/c8/c8dc8396fe0584c3a023ee9ad5baf2ab3e5d98c84fa4e7745ec091b2531b7bc9.png)\n\nTherefore, the missing parts are $\\color{blue}171{-}180$ and $\\color{blue}9$.\n\n| Height (cm) | Number of Players |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $161{-}170$ | $6$ |\n| $171-180$ | $9$ |\n| $181{-}190$ | $3$ |" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147329 1069 "e-2815" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147329 1071 "The boxplot below shows the distribution of the weekly waste produced by families in a residential building. Describe the skewness of the distribution.\n\n![](images/21/2116cdb5e180e0cdd832b132287a07bb30095c215ba6547760c36607c2f3e49e.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147329 1072 #uuid "384159d4-c7a2-5337-bb1a-c11a58094e73" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147329 1074 "Notice that the median is closer to the upper quartile than the lower quartile. So, we have a longer tail on the left, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b5/b5ae59c7ae9cb3fc092de80f73382c8822530b3f4cae386013772f59a34c0a67.png)\n\nTherefore, the distribution is *left-skewed*." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147330 1069 "e-2817" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147330 1071 "In which of the following distributions might it be preferable to use the median instead of the mean to measure the center?\n\n![](images/47/47b42aa626eb8c9f13372558602c76fadd01718ffb92b03c002219cc31307f2f.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147330 1072 #uuid "900941e5-23dd-539c-8c6f-5717f27d7a76" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147330 1074 "The mean is sensitive to skew and outliers, whereas the median is resistant to skew and outliers.\n\nSo, we should use the median instead of the mean when we are measuring the center of a skewed distribution or a distribution with outliers.\n\nAmong the given options, all distributions are symmetric except for the following, which is left-skewed.\n\n![](images/3a/3aad7cebc5cc440cc475a84a97a0f256a0f649adb4833937cf4bab0ebb2302da.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147331 1069 "e-2818" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147331 1071 "![](images/f8/f8c8c19fd41a2b3c2e7cab66078902db8141d93abf566e86817d7ce3bf12da67.png)\n\nThe dot plot above shows how much time each student in Mr. Johnson's class spent studying math on a particular day. Each dot represents a single student.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The distribution is approximately symmetric.\n2. The mean of the distribution equals $20\\, \\text{min}$.\n3. The mean of the distribution exceeds $20 \\, \\text{min}$.\n4. The mean of the distribution is less than $20 \\, \\text{min}$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147331 1072 #uuid "d0d9550d-8bdb-5b44-b2c4-a453849c8266" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147331 1074 "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The distribution is left-skewed (since we have a longer tail on the left). It is not symmetric.![](images/fa/fab16210f6457bd730ff416b7a58915c3829a9f17c700d66edd7bc98ba91b8f5.png)\n\n- Statement III is true, while statements II and IV are false. All of the data is situated between $5$ and $35$ minutes. If the data were symmetric, then the mean would be given by the midpoint, which is\n$\\dfrac{5+35}{2} = 20 \\, \\text{min}$.\nHowever, since the distribution is left-skewed, most of the data points are concentrated on the right-hand side. This indicates that the mean of our distribution lies to the right of the midpoint.![](images/90/90cbf3a184fe151676242768d9ee03fb68a6da3a1d846d998f688395faa4623f.png)\nSo, the mean is *greater than* $20$ minutes.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147332 1069 "e-2820" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147332 1071 "The data set below shows the number of vanilla ice creams sold by a particular ice cream parlor every day during a certain week.\n$4, \\: 5, \\: 6, \\: 12, \\: 13, \\: 13, \\: 15$\nWhich of the following gives the box plot for this data?\n\n![](images/a8/a83a8fccb8c040fe69ef827066b402fef94589376c38405e8a025de62f638d94.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147332 1072 #uuid "f3430c81-af60-5b76-9a37-b66cbcf3fc5b" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147332 1074 "Notice that our data set is already arranged from smallest to greatest, where we have\n\n$$\nsmallest value = 4, greatest value = 15\n$$\n\nSince the number of data points is odd ($7$), the median of the data set is the middle number. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{median} = \\color{blue}12\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the quartiles by considering the lower and upper halves of the distribution:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{4, \\: 5, \\: 6,}_{\\text{lower half}} \\: {\\color{blue}12}, \\: \\underbrace{13, \\: 13, \\: 15}_{\\text{upper half}}\n$$\n\nThe lower quartile is the median of the lower half. In this case, it's the middle number:\n\n$$\n\\text{lower quartile} = 5\n$$\n\nThe upper quartile is the median of the upper half. In this case, it's the middle number:\n\n$$\n\\text{upper quartile} = 13\n$$\n\nFinally, we draw the corresponding boxplot, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bd69426498e43ecc4bdd4fa4f7954451fface95bc37a87abc16729fa58c85f46.png)\n\nTherefore, the boxplot representing this data is diagram $\\textrm I$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147333 1069 "e-2821" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147333 1071 "Which of the following is true regarding the data set represented by the histogram below?\n\n![](images/16/16a2738294ec59a0414b0e92af9f79afefc47945559b8dc5bc8668cc7a40adbc.png)\n\n1. The distribution has outliers.\n2. The median is not sensitive to the skew of the distribution.\n3. The mean is the best measure of the center for the given distribution." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147333 1072 #uuid "6f4137db-e7ed-52cc-bf5e-af5e3c85d2de" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147333 1074 "First, let's reсall the following facts.\n\n- The mean is sensitive to skew and outliers.\n- The median is not sensitive to skew nor outliers.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Our distribution has outliers.\n- Statement II is true. Our distribution is right-skewed, but the median is not sensitive to the skew.\n- Statement III is false. Since the distribution is skewed and has outliers, the median is the best measure of the center.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147334 1069 "e-2822" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147334 1071 "Tina sorted out her collection of science fiction books into four groups according to their number of pages. The histogram below gives the corresponding distribution. How many of Tina's science fiction books have at most $249$ pages?\n\n![](images/18/18cd5e8e9c20d772fd0ca5a44401f70f7391b4f2adb6a67e2a85bee922496ee0.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147334 1072 #uuid "b3e10737-5e88-5d58-b432-4f667549263f" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147334 1074 "The first and second bars correspond to the books that have between $\\color{blue} 150{-}199$ and $\\color{blue} 200{-}249$ pages, respectively.\n\nThe height of the first bar is $\\color{red}15$ (halfway between $10$ and $20$) and the second bar has a height of $\\color{red}35$ (halfway between $30$ and $40$).\n\n![](images/c0/c05bf6c07b068ecc2adc3ad4ddf0a0a3c35ec1f12324bcb5b13e83044c5d063a.png)\n\nTherefore, Tina has\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}15} + {\\color{red}35} = 50\n$$\n\nbooks that have at most $249$ pages." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147335 1069 "e-2826" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147335 1071 "Members of a gym were asked how much time they spend working out on a typical day. The histogram below shows the results. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The distribution is left-skewed.\n2. The distribution is symmetric.\n3. The distribution is right-skewed.\n4. The mean lies between $100$ and $119$ minutes.\n\n![](images/68/681c1122ab1cba8e835c54b44a4c2f0a519ce0c50d7559fd748a2b13f2d841d5.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147335 1072 #uuid "096da154-4697-50b1-9eac-1fc491d45f26" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147335 1074 "Let's look at the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statements I and III are false. The left-hand side is a mirror image of the right-hand side. Therefore, the distribution is symmetric.\n\n![](images/23/2323b0543a963c1f3804bb5f6e57ab6fe52e3cce5bf71899565d70bfd79c7e7d.png)\n\n- Statement IV is false. When a distribution is symmetric, and the corresponding histogram has an odd number of bars, the mean and the median always lie within the central column. Therefore, the mean time lies between $80$ and $99$ minutes.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147336 1069 "e-3016" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147336 1071 "How many faces does the cube shown below have?\n\n![](images/93/931007418b0e7f9af99bb34831fbd50119ab5d0468e0039405bb84cb33fbe9fe.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147336 1072 #uuid "dd8a337c-4d71-5ec3-8d50-a78280e3f907" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147336 1074 "The cube has $6$ square faces:\n\n![](images/e8/e86115935aa177dcab50125df50bf9df825e9a47cf5c94d9fd28f587943e3b5a.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147337 1069 "e-3017" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147337 1071 "How many vertices does the rectangular prism shown below have?\n\n![](images/dc/dc430f51134ef0b94a2e8c0932816f80f64321601a3b815f47cb67acd8f028a1.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147337 1072 #uuid "307cc2a7-a855-56ef-af46-c899d88e3145" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147337 1074 "The vertices of a polyhedron are the corners where edges meet.\n\nThe rectangular prism has $8$ vertices in total, as shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fea0e779f9738891cf8eb06b4a76c1211f6af08d37a009948c01c2faaa396c02.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147338 1069 "e-3018" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147338 1071 "How many edges does the triangular pyramid shown below have?\n\n![](images/8b/8b4e642b2fb6f0aec8cf65702c802d4c2b31300a3aa3fef652979eede9bd8974.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147338 1072 #uuid "38109a6b-2d68-5f74-8550-eb3cf8c30a1e" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147338 1074 "Edges are straight line segments that connect the vertices of a polyhedron.\n\nThe triangular pyramid has $6$ edges in total, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d6/d6d6b1249a73f0297cbb1e8efc7ca07ce07bf7ca0a5fb09372ee932aed9a2c26.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147339 1069 "e-3892" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147339 1071 "At a shopping mall, the rate at which people are entering the mall is given by\n\n$r(t) = 300t^2+50$,\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in people per hour, and $t>0$ is the number of hours since the mall opened. In addition, people are leaving the mall at a steady rate of $25$ people per hour.\n\n1. Calculate the number of people that entered the mall in the first two hours.\n2. Calculate the net change in the number of people inside the mall in the first 2 hours." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147339 1072 #uuid "a3030491-423d-5633-8208-b373bfee3b06" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147339 1074 "**Part 1**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of\n\n$$\nr(t) = 300t^2+50\n$$\n\nTo find out how many people entered the mall between $t=0$ and $t=2$, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{2}r(t)dt &= \\int_{0}^{2}300t^{2} + 50dt \\\\\n&= 100t^{3} + \\left.50t\\right|_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= (100 \\cdot 2^{3} + 50(2)) - (0) \\\\\n&= (800 + 100) \\\\\n&= 900\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Part 2**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of\n\n$$\nr(t) = 300t^2+50\n$$\n\npeople per hour, and are leaving the mall at a rate of $25$ people per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of people inside the mall, which we'll call $R(t)$, is given by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(t) &= r(t) - 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **net change** in the number of people, we integrate $R(t)$ between $t=0$ and $t=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{2}R(t)dt &= \\int_{0}^{2}r(t) - 25dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}r(t)dt_(⏟)_(900) - \\int_{0}^{2}25dt \\\\\n&= 900 - [25t]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 900 - 50 \\\\\n&= 850\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, over the first two hours, there was a net increase of $850$ people." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147340 1069 "e-3893" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147340 1071 "The rate at which guests are arriving at a restaurant one evening is given by the function\n\n$r(t) = 6t^2+2$,\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in guests per hour and $t > 0$ is the time in hours since the restaurant opened. Guests are also leaving at a steady rate of $6$ guests per hour. If there were $50$ guests in the restaurant when it opened, how many guests will be in the restaurant after $1$ hour?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147340 1072 #uuid "d007e9ad-3fd3-5ae2-864a-ef89ea01785c" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147340 1074 "Guests are entering the restaurant at a rate of $6t^2+2$ guests per hour and are leaving at a rate of $6$ guests per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of guests (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(t) &= r(t) - 6 \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} + 2 - 6 \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total number of guests** after $t$ hours (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(t) &= T(0) + \\int_{0}^{t}R(x)dx \\\\\n&= 50 + \\int_{0}^{t}(6x^{2} - 4)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration.\n\nTo find out the number of guests after $1$ hour, we substitute $t=1$ into the above and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(1) &= 50 + \\int_{0}^{1}(6x^{2} - 4)dx \\\\\n&= 50 + \\left.[2x^{3} - 4x]\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 50 + [(2(1)^{3} - 4(1)) - (0)] \\\\\n&= 50 - 2 \\\\\n&= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147341 1069 "e-3894" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147341 1071 "In example 2, we saw that the number of guests in a restaurant is given by\n\n$T(t) = 50 + \\displaystyle \\int_0^t \\left(6x^2-4\\right)\\,\\textrm d x\\,$.\n\nGiven that $T(t)$ has a local minimum for some $t>0$, find the time when the number of guests is at its minimum value." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147341 1072 #uuid "74125c40-ecdd-5453-9c49-13567fb4621c" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147341 1074 "To find the time when the number of guests is minimized, we need to solve\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $T(t)$ with respect to $t$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dT}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(50 + \\int_{0}^{t}(6x^{2} - 4)dx) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(50) + \\frac{d}{dt}\\int_{0}^{t}(6x^{2} - 4)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}\\int_{0}^{t}(6x^{2} - 4)dx \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n6t^2-4 = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad t = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nSo we have $2$ critical points\n\n$$\nt=\\sqrt{\\dfrac 2 3}\n$$\n\nand $t=0$ (the endpoint). But since the local minimum exists for $t>0$,\n\n$$\nt=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nmust be the minimum value." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147342 1069 "e-3895" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147342 1071 "The function $F(h)=40 e^{-0.5h}$ gives the number of dust particles in the air, in millions per cubic foot, at a height of $h$ feet above the sea level in a particular region of the world. Consider a vertical column of air with a height of $100 \\, \\text{ft}$ and a constant horizontal cross-section of $5$ square feet. How many dust particles does the column contain?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147342 1072 #uuid "899e7110-091c-5a7d-b93b-bec16eafbf43" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147342 1074 "Here, $F(h)$ is a density function for the number of dust particles in the air, and its units are millions of particles per $1 \\, \\text{ft}^3$.\n\nThe mass of a single vertical column measuring\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{1\\,\\textrm{ft}^2}_{\\textrm{cross section}} \\times \\:\\: \\underbrace{100\\, \\textrm{ft}}_{\\textrm{height}}\n$$\n\nis given by the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{100} F(h) \\, \\text{d}h\n$$\n\nHowever, since we want the mass of $5$ vertical columns (since that will give a cross-section of $5\\,\\textrm{ft}^2)$, we need to multiply the above integral by $5$. So the required mass is given by\n\n$$\n5 \\int_{0}^{100} F(h) \\, \\text{d}h\n$$\n\nCarrying out the integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= 5\\int_{0}^{100}40e^{-0.5d}dh \\\\\n&= 200\\int_{0}^{100}e^{-0.5d}dh \\\\\n&= \\left.200[- \\frac{1}{0.5} \\cdot e^{-0.5d}]\\right|_{0}^{100} \\\\\n&= \\left.- 400e^{-0.5d}\\right|_{0}^{100} \\\\\n&=-400(e^{-50} - 1) \\\\\n&= 400(1 - e^{-50})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the column contains\n\n$$\n400 \\left(1-e^{-50} \\right)\n$$\n\nmillion particles.\n\n**Note:** We can say that the number of particles is around $400$ million since the number $e^{-50}$ inside the parenthesis is extremely small (it's almost zero)." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147343 1069 "e-3896" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147343 1071 "Suppose that the density of a certain substance in the ocean, in milligrams per cubic meter, at a depth of $h$ meters can be modeled by the function\n${30 - h,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le h \\le 20 \\\\ 10e^{2 - 0.1h},\\end{vmatrix}h > 20$.\nWhich integral expression gives the number of milligrams of the substance in a vertical column of water that has a height of $30 \\, \\text{m}$ and a constant horizontal cross-section of $4$ square meters?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147343 1072 #uuid "c0a20fe5-e524-586b-8015-45965b32da68" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147343 1074 "Here, the function $c(h)$ is a density function for the mass of the substance, and its units are $\\text{mg}/\\text{m}^3$.\n\nThe mass of a single vertical column measuring\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{1\\,\\textrm{m}^2}_{\\textrm{cross section}} \\times \\:\\: \\underbrace{30\\, \\textrm{m}}_{\\textrm{height}}\n$$\n\nis given by the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{30} c(h) \\, \\text{d}h\n$$\n\nHowever, since we want the mass of $4$ vertical columns (since that will give a cross-section of $4\\,\\textrm{m}^2)$, we need to multiply the above integral by $4$. So the required mass is given by\n\n$$\nM = 4 \\int_{0}^{30} c(h) \\, \\text{d}h\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= 4\\int_{0}^{30}c(h)dh \\\\\n&= 4(\\int_{0}^{20}c(h)dh + \\int_{20}^{30}c(h)dh) \\\\\n&= 4[\\int_{0}^{20}(30 - h)dh + \\int_{20}^{30}10e^{2 - 0.1h}dh] \\\\\n&= 4\\int_{0}^{20}(30 - h)dh + 40\\int_{20}^{30}e^{2 - 0.1h}dh\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147344 1069 "e-3897" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147344 1071 "A grain storage tank has a total height of $15$ feet. The area of its horizontal cross-section at height $h$ feet is modeled by the function\n$A(h)= 3e^{-0.2h}$,\nwhere $A(h)$ is measured in square feet. What is the total volume of the tank?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147344 1072 #uuid "89cef9e6-397c-5a5d-a4c9-034fe17d815d" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147344 1074 "Here, $A(h)$ is the density function for the volume of the tank because the accumulation of area results in volume. Therefore, the volume $V$ of the tank can be found by integrating $A(h)$ from $h=0$ to $h=15{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\int_{0}^{15}A(h)dh \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{15}3e^{-0.2h}dh \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot - \\frac{1}{0.2} \\cdot \\left.[e^{-0.2h}]\\right|_{0}^{15} \\\\\n&= \\left.- 15e^{-0.2h}\\right|_{0}^{15} \\\\\n&= 15(1 - e^{-3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the volume of the tank is\n\n$$\n15 \\left(1 - e^{-3} \\right)\n$$\n\ncubic feet." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147345 1069 "e-3898" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147345 1071 "The area, in square meters, of a horizontal cross-section of a water storage tank at height $h$ meters is modeled by the function\n\n$A(h)=\\dfrac{20}{1+0.25h^2}$.\n\nWater is pumped into the tank. When the height of the water is $2$ meters, the height is increasing at the rate of $3$ meters per hour. Find the rate at which the volume of water in the tank is changing with respect to time when the height of the water is $2\\,\\text{m}$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147345 1072 #uuid "c0870133-92d4-5ac7-bcfd-3b5360fe7f76" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147345 1074 "The function $A(h)$ is a density function for the volume of the tank because an accumulation of area results in volume. Therefore, the volume $V(h)$ of the tank at height $h$ is given by the following:\n\n$$\nV(h) = \\int_{0}^{h} A(x) \\, \\text{d}x\n$$\n\n**Important**: Notice that we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. This is to ensure that $V(h)$ is a function of $h$ only, and so we don't get confused. When calculating a definite integral, it does not matter which variable we use!\n\nOur task is to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nso we differentiate the above with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{0}^{h} A(x) \\, \\text{d}x\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d h}{\\textrm d t}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d h}\\int_{0}^{h} A(x) \\, \\text{d}x\n$$\n\nUsing the second fundamental theorem of calculus, the derivative of the integral simplifies to $A(h)$, and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot A(h)\n$$\n\nNow, we use the given information:\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} = 3 \\, \\text{m/h}$\n- $A(2) = \\dfrac{20}{1+0.25(2)^2} =10 \\, \\text{m}^2$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{dV}{dt}\\right|_{h = 2} &= \\frac{dh}{dt} \\cdot \\left.A(h)\\right|_{h = 2} \\\\\n&= 3m/h \\cdot 10m^{2} \\\\\n&= 30m^{3}/h\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of water is changing at a rate of $30$ cubic meters per hour." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147346 1069 "e-4904" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147346 1071 "![](images/b4/b4906893158bb5dc9f55363dcf2edc2ae1d30d8acff7121041d4a849ee73cbce.png)\n\nWhat inequality corresponds to the highlighted region shown on the number line above?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147346 1072 #uuid "35fd1c34-48d0-5ba8-b274-86de413500f8" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147346 1074 "The red segment contains all values between $-2$ and $0$. In addition,\n\n- the closed circle at $-2$ implies that $x$ can be equal to $-2$, while\n- the open circle at $0$ implies that $x$ cannot be equal to $0$.\n\nTherefore, the highlighted region represents\n\n$$\n-2 \\leq x < 0\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147347 1069 "e-5917" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147347 1071 "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+4z=0$ and $2x+4y+2z+18=0$ is given by the line\n\n$\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 0, a_2, a_3\\rangle + t \\mathbf b, \\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nCalculate $a_2+a_3$, and find a possible direction vector $\\mathbf b$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147347 1072 #uuid "fb408a64-6d03-594d-92bd-b95c2a5c95a8" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147347 1074 "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0$. Substituting this into the first equation gives\n\n$$\n0+2y+4z=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y=-2z\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ and $y=-2z$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 4y + 2z + 18 &= 0 \\\\\n2(0) + 4(-2z) + 2z + 18 &= 0 \\\\\n- 6z &= -18 \\\\\nz &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2z=-6$ and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,-6,3\\rangle\n$$\n\nis a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+4z=0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 1,2,4 \\rangle$.\n- For the plane $2x+4y+2z+18=0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 2,4,2 \\rangle$.\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn_{1} \\cdot n_{2} &= ⟨1, 2, 4⟩ \\cdot ⟨2, 4, 2⟩ \\\\\n&= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}4 \\\\\n&= 2 \\mid 4 \\mid 2 \\\\\n&=-12i + 6j \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 12, 6, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} =\\dfrac{1}{6}(\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2) = \\langle -2, 1, 0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= a + tb \\\\\nr(t) &= ⟨0,-6, 3⟩ + t⟨ - 2, 1, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\na_2 +a_3 = -6+3 = -3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf b = \\langle -2,1,0\\rangle\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147348 1069 "e-5920" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147348 1071 "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n${2x + 4y + 5 = 0; 3y - 2z - 7 = 0$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147348 1072 #uuid "68b206e1-07ee-5591-ac08-0109cb2063cf" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147348 1074 "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y +5= 0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,4,0 \\rangle$.\n- For the plane $3y-2z-7= 0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 0,3,-2 \\rangle$.\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= n_{1} \\cdot n_{2} \\\\\n&= ⟨2, 4, 0⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 3,-2⟩ \\\\\n&= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}0 \\\\\n&= 0 \\mid 3 \\mid - 2 \\\\\n&=-8i + 4j + 6k \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 8, 4, 6⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147349 1069 "e-5921" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147349 1071 "Given the two planes $4x-2y=0$ and $-2x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $\\mathbf{0}$.\n2. The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n3. The planes intersect on a straight line." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147349 1072 #uuid "ae2be3b1-50fb-5abe-9f18-002a88f1de61" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147349 1074 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $4x-2y=0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 4,-2,0 \\rangle$.\n- For the plane $-2x+y+1=0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -2,1,0 \\rangle$.\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n$n_{1} \\cdot n_{2}|= ⟨4,-2, 0⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 2, 1, 0⟩; n|= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}0; - 2 \\mid 1 \\mid 0; = 0$.\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2 =\\mathbf{0}$, the vectors $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ are parallel. However, the first plane passes through the origin $O(0,0,0)$ but the second plane doesn't: $-2(0)+(0)+1=1 \\neq 0$ So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the planes are parallel but not coincident, they cannot intersect each other.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147350 1069 "e-6615" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147350 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$y'' + 9y = 6\\cos x$.\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\cos(3x)$ and $y_2 = \\sin(3x)$.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution\n$y_p(x) = u_1(x) y_1 + u_2(x) y_2$\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n${u_{1}^{′}y_{1}^{′} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2}^{′} = 6\\cos x; u_{1}^{′}y_{1} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2} = 0$\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle u_2(x) = \\displaystyle \\int g(x)\\,\\textrm d x$, find the function $g$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147350 1072 #uuid "cb472181-da8e-5252-9c80-4d336e2ccf9c" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147350 1074 "We notice that the forcing term is\n\n$$\nf(x) = 6\\cos x\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos(3x), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin(3x)\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(y_{1}, y_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= y_{1}y_{2}^{′} - y_{1}^{′}y_{2} \\\\\n&= (\\cos (3x))(\\sin (3x))^{′} - (\\cos (3x))^{′}(\\sin (3x)) \\\\\n&= (\\cos (3x))(3\\cos (3x)) - (-3\\sin (3x))(\\sin (3x)) \\\\\n&= 3\\cos^{2} (3x) + 3\\sin^{2} (3x) \\\\\n&= 3 \\ne 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are linearly independent.\n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n{u_{1}^{′}(\\cos (3x))^{′} + u_{2}^{′}(\\sin (3x))^{′} = 6\\cos x; u_{1}^{′}\\cos (3x) + u_{2}^{′}\\sin (3x) = 0⟹{-3u_{1}^{′}\\sin (3x) + 3u_{2}^{′}\\cos (3x) = 6\\cos x; u_{1}^{′}\\cos (3x) + u_{2}^{′}\\sin (3x) = 0\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for $u_2$. We start by eliminating $u_1'$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\nu_1' = -\\dfrac{u_2'\\sin(3x)}{\\cos(3x)}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3u_{1}^{′}\\sin (3x) + 3u_{2}^{′}\\cos (3x) &= 6\\cos x \\\\\n- 3(- (u_{2}^{′}\\sin (3x))/(\\cos (3x)))\\sin (3x) + 3u_{2}^{′}\\cos (3x) &= 6\\cos x \\\\\n3u_{2}^{′}(\\sin^{2} (3x))/(\\cos (3x)) + 3u_{2}^{′}\\cos (3x) &= 6\\cos x \\\\\n3u_{2}^{′}(\\sin^{2} (3x) + \\cos^{2} (3x))/(\\cos (3x)) &= 6\\cos x \\\\\n3u_{2}^{′}(1)/(\\cos (3x)) &= 6\\cos x \\\\\nu_{2}^{′} &= 2\\cos x\\cos (3x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intu_{2}^{′}dx &= \\int2\\cos x\\cos (3x)dx \\\\\nu_{2} &= \\int2\\cos x\\cos (3x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x) = 2\\cos x \\cos(3x)\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147351 1069 "e-6619" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147351 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing simple harmonic motion?\n\n1. $y'' + 81y = 0$\n2. $y'' + 9y'+81y = 0$\n3. $y'' - 81y = 0$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147351 1072 #uuid "54c15528-aec0-5554-b3b0-f928f3a6d3d5" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147351 1074 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- Equation I takes the form\n$y'' + \\omega^2 y = 0$\nwith\n$\\omega^2 = 81 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\omega = 9$.\nTherefore, equation I represents SHM.\n- Equation II does not take the form\n$y'' + \\omega^2 y = 0$\nbecause it has a $y'$ term.\n- Equation III does not take the form\n$y'' + \\omega^2 y = 0$.\nIt would require $\\omega^2 = -81$, which is not possible for any real number $\\omega$ since $\\omega^2 \\geq 0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147352 1069 "e-6620" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147352 1071 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 100y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the oscillation?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147352 1072 #uuid "4c6cb7ad-3d0d-5d85-bda7-a9c8874ce25b" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147352 1074 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere the period $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\omega}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 100\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 10\n$$\n\nso the period (in seconds) is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{10} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\,\\textrm{s}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147353 1069 "e-6621" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147353 1071 "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 9 y = 0$.\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)$?\n\n1. $y = R\\cos(3t+\\phi_1)$\n2. $y = R\\sin(9t+\\phi_2)$\n3. $y = A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3t}$\n\nNote that $R,\\phi_1,\\phi_2, A$ and $B$ are constants." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147353 1072 #uuid "2ca66468-091b-5f69-99eb-2d347486becd" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147353 1074 "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2y = 0\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1. $y = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n2. $y = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n3. $y =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 9\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 3\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\omega=3$ into the three different forms of the general solution gives the following:\n\n1. $y = R\\cos{(3t + \\phi_1)}$\n2. $y = R\\sin{(3t + \\phi_2)}$\n3. $y =A\\cos{3t} + B\\sin{3 t}$\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III could be used to represent $y(t)$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147354 1069 "e-6622" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147354 1071 "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 36y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $3\\,\\textrm{m/s}$, find the amplitude of the oscillation." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147354 1072 #uuid "2e1c98d9-bce3-5b6a-bbef-ee32fa2a2448" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147354 1074 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nand the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi)}\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 36\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 6\n$$\n\nso the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{(6t+\\phi)}\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0) = 0$. Substituting this into the equation above gives\n\n$$\nR\\sin\\phi = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\phi = 0\n$$\n\nSince $\\phi=0$, our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny = R\\sin{6 t}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $3\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ when $t=0$, which means that $y'(0) = 3$. Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = 6R\\cos{6t}\n$$\n\nand substituting $y'(0) = 3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n3 = 6R\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad R=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147355 1069 "e-6623" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147355 1071 "A particle of mass $36\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2$. The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.\n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The governing differential equation is $y'' + \\dfrac{1}{2}y = 0$\n2. The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi}\\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}$\n3. The general solution can be expressed as $y = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)}$, where $R$ is the amplitude and $\\phi$ is the phase." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147355 1072 #uuid "7489abc3-36e8-5c4d-a2cf-a2bcb14b63ed" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147355 1074 "Let's look at each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m=36$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k=9$ has\n$\\omega^2 = \\dfrac{k}{m} = \\dfrac{9}{36} = \\dfrac{1}{4}$,\nso its equation of motion is\n$y'' + \\dfrac{1}{4}y = 0$,\nwhich is different from the equation in statement I.\n- Statement II is true. The frequency $f$ (measured in $s^{-1}$) is given by\n$f = \\dfrac{\\omega}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)}{2\\pi} = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi} \\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- Statement III is true. The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms:\n$A\\cos ωt + B\\sin ωt, R\\cos (ωt + ϕ_{1}), R\\sin (ωt + ϕ_{2})$.\nWriting $\\omega = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $\\phi_1 = \\phi$ in the form $R\\sin{(\\omega t+\\phi_2)}$ gives\n$y(t) = R\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}t+\\phi\\right)}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147356 1069 "e-6624" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147356 1071 "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?\n\n1. $-2x^2y''+6xy'-5y=0$\n2. $xy''+2y'-3xy=0$\n3. $y''-3x^2y=\\sqrt{x}$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147356 1072 #uuid "7887c216-958f-5dfe-8889-b4c3033fbafe" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147356 1074 "The second-order Cauchy-Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\nax^2y''+bxy'+cy=f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$, and $c$ are constants. With that in mind, let's inspect each of the given equations.\n\n- Equation I is a Cauchy-Euler equation with $a=-2$, $b=6$, $c=-5$, and $f(x)=0$.\n- Equation II is *not* a Cauchy-Euler equation because the $y''$ is not multiplied by an $x^2$. Instead, it is multiplied by an $x$.\n- Equation III is *not* a Cauchy-Euler equation because the $y''$ term is not multiplied by an $x^2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147357 1069 "e-6625" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147357 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$3t^2 y''+8ty' -2y = 0, \\quad t\\gt 0$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147357 1072 #uuid "08304289-e9fc-5914-a6e7-187ea5a40031" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147357 1074 "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form\n\n$$\ny=t^\\lambda\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda t^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)t^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3t^{2} \\cdot λ(λ - 1)t^{λ - 2} + 8t \\cdot λt^{λ - 1} - 2t^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\n3λ(λ - 1)t^{λ} + 8λt^{λ} - 2t^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nt^{λ}[3λ(λ - 1) + 8λ - 2] &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3λ(λ - 1) + 8λ - 2 &= 0 \\\\\n3λ^{2} + 5λ - 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 2)(3λ - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(t) &= c_{1}t^{-2} + c_{2}t^{1/3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{c_{1}}{t^{2}} + c_{2}t^{1/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147358 1069 "e-6626" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147358 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$x^2 y'' +2 xy' -6 y= 0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y=\\dfrac{c_1}{x^3}+ c_2x^{2}$,\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$x^2 y'' +2 xy' -6 y= 0, \\quad y(1)= 0, \\quad y'(1) = 5, \\quad x \\gt 0$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147358 1072 #uuid "d86f4d20-8ebe-52ea-bf49-f0cdcd0a2600" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147358 1074 "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=0$. Substituting $y(1)= 0$ into the general solution gives\n$0 = c_1+c_2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad c_2 = -c_1$.\n- Then, we apply the condition $y'(1) = 5$. Differentiating $y$, we get\n$y^{′}|=-3\\frac{c_{1}}{x^{4}} + 2c_{2}x$.\nSubstituting $y'(1)=5$ and $c_2=-c_1$ into the above gives\n$5|=-3c_{1} + 2c_{2}; 5|=-3c_{1} + 2(-c_{1}); 5|=-5c_{1}; c_{1}|=-1⇒c_{2} = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=x^{2}-\\dfrac{1}{x^3}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147359 1069 "e-6627" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147359 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$2x^2 y'' + 6 xy' + 2y = 0, \\qquad x>0$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147359 1072 #uuid "10aa6257-c3f8-59c6-802e-b4a29fca49ab" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147359 1074 "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form\n\n$$\ny=x^\\lambda\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} \\cdot λ(λ - 1)x^{λ - 2} + 6x \\cdot λx^{λ - 1} + 2x^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\n2λ(λ - 1)x^{λ} + 6λx^{λ} + 2x^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nx^{λ}[2λ(λ - 1) + 6λ + 2] &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2λ(λ - 1) + 6λ + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n2λ^{2} + 4λ + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n2(λ^{2} + 2λ + 1) &= 0 \\\\\n2(λ + 1)^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation has a double root\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= c_{1}x^{-1} + c_{2}x^{-1}\\ln x \\\\\n&= \\frac{c_{1}}{x} + \\frac{c_{2}\\ln x}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147360 1069 "e-6628" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147360 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$x^2 y'' - 19 xy' + 100y = 0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y =c_1 x^{10}+ c_2x ^{10}\\ln x$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$x^2 y'' - 19 xy' + 100y = 0, \\quad y(1) = 0,\\quad y'(1)=1, \\quad x>0$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147360 1072 #uuid "ff0b0603-e0bf-526a-9b4b-d2a4886ba2c3" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147360 1074 "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=0$. Substituting $y(1)= 0$ into the general solution gives\n$0= c_1$.\n- Then, we apply the condition $y'(1) = 1$. Differentiating $y$, we get\n$y^{′}|= 10c_{1}x^{9} + c_{2}x^{9} + 10c_{2}x^{9}\\ln x$.\n\nSubstituting $y'(1)=1$ and $c_1 = 0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 10c_{1} + c_{2} \\\\\n1 &= 0 + c_{2} \\\\\nc_{2} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x) = x ^{10}\\ln x\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147361 1069 "e-6647" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147361 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{-3t}-e^{5t}$ for $s>5$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147361 1072 #uuid "c012d177-e70b-5332-b5b2-91bf9b829eca" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147361 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\alpha f(t) + \\beta g(t)\\} = \\alpha\\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\} + \\beta\\cdot \\mathcal L \\{g(t)\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{e^{-3t} - e^{5t}} &= L{e^{-3t}} + L{-e^{5t}} \\\\\n&= L{e^{-3t}} - L{e^{5t}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\left\\{e^{kt}\\right\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-k}, \\qquad s > k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{e^{-3t}} &= \\frac{1}{s + 3}, s > - 3 \\\\\nL{e^{5t}} &= \\frac{1}{s - 5}, s > 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f(t)} &= L{e^{-3t}} - L{e^{5t}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s + 3} - \\frac{1}{s - 5} \\\\\n&= ((s - 5) - (s + 3))/((s + 3)(s - 5)) \\\\\n&= (-8)/((s + 3)(s - 5)) \\\\\n&= - (8)/((s + 3)(s - 5))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\mathcal L\\{e^{-3t}\\}$ is defined for $s\\in (-3,\\infty)$ and $\\mathcal L \\{e^{5t}\\}$ is defined for $s\\in (5,\\infty)$, we have that $\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(-3,\\infty) \\cap (5,\\infty) = (5,\\infty)\n$$\n\nthat is, $s > 5$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147362 1069 "e-6648" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147362 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of $f'(t)$ for $s > 3$ given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous over $[0,\\infty)$, $f(0)=0$, and\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = \\dfrac{6}{s^2-5s+6}, \\quad s>3$.\nAssume that $f'$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147362 1072 #uuid "25c2b366-fa9e-538f-9971-d3a47e7a6384" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147362 1074 "Suppose\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(t)$ is a continuous function of exponential order $a$ on $[0,\\infty)$. If $f'(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f'(t) \\right\\} = s \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t) \\right\\} - f(0)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the Laplace transform of $f'(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f^{′}(t)} &= sL{f(t)} - f(0) \\\\\n&= s \\cdot \\frac{6}{s^{2} - 5s + 6} - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{6s}{s^{2} - 5s + 6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s > 3$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147363 1069 "e-6649" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147363 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f''(t) \\right\\}$ for $s > 0$, given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous on $[0,\\infty)$, $f'(0)=2$, $f(0)=-1$, and\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = \\dfrac{2 - s}{s^2+4}, \\quad s>0$.\nAssume that $f'$ is continuous and $f''$ piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147363 1072 #uuid "d058e1e9-5881-568b-bb28-bab5d6299f53" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147363 1074 "Suppose\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(t)$ is a continuous function of exponential order $a$ on $[0,\\infty)$. If $f'(t)$ is continuous and $f''(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f^{″}(t)} &= s^{2}L{f(t)} - sf(0) - f^{′}(0)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the Laplace transform of $f''(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f^{″}(t)} &= s^{2}L{f(t)} - sf(0) - f^{′}(0) \\\\\n&= s^{2} \\cdot \\frac{2 - s}{s^{2} + 4} - s \\cdot (-1) - 2 \\\\\n&= (s^{2}(2 - s))/(s^{2} + 4) + s - 2 \\\\\n&= - (s^{2}(s - 2))/(s^{2} + 4) + (s - 2) \\\\\n&= (s - 2)(-\\frac{s^{2}}{s^{2} + 4} + 1) \\\\\n&= (s - 2)(\\frac{4}{s^{2} + 4}) \\\\\n&= (4(s - 2))/(s^{2} + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s > 0$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147364 1069 "e-6653" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147364 1071 "Find the inverse Laplace transform for $F(s) = \\dfrac{3}{s^2 + 9}$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147364 1072 #uuid "d34aecf1-9491-5cd5-88bc-c276b8df3f77" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147364 1074 "From the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{\\sin 3t} &= \\frac{3}{s^{2} + 3^{2}} = \\frac{3}{s^{2} + 9}, s > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{-1}{\\frac{3}{s^{2} + 9}} &= L^{-1}{L{\\sin 3t}} \\\\\n&= \\sin 3t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147365 1069 "e-6654" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147365 1071 "Compute $\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\dfrac{e^{-3s}}{7}\\right\\}$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147365 1072 #uuid "6887b220-606d-5923-88cb-d13c4606394d" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147365 1074 "From the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{δ_{3}(t)} &= e^{-3s}, s > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\delta_3(t) = \\delta(t-3)\n$$\n\nis the Dirac delta function.\n\nTherefore, using the multiplicative property of the inverse Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{-1}{\\frac{e^{-3s}}{7}} &= \\frac{1}{7}L^{-1}{e^{-3s}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7}L^{-1}{L{δ_{3}(t)}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7}δ_{3}(t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147366 1069 "e-6655" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147366 1071 "Find $\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{F(s)\\right\\}$ for $F(s)=\\dfrac{4e^{-s}-2}{s}$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147366 1072 #uuid "54edc81f-fd2e-5ca8-8779-b3a7a5548e14" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147366 1074 "First, we decompose the fraction into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4e^{-s}-2}{s} =\\dfrac{4e^{-s}}{s} - \\dfrac{2}{s}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{u_{1}(t)} &= \\frac{e^{-s}}{s}, s > 0, \\\\\nL{1} &= \\frac{1}{s}, s > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nu_1(t)=u(t-1)\n$$\n\nis the unit step function.\n\nTherefore, using the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{-1}{\\frac{4e^{-s}}{s} - \\frac{2}{s}} &= 4L^{-1}{\\frac{e^{-s}}{s}} - 2L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\\n&= 4u_{1}(t) - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\dfrac{4e^{-s}-2}{s}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{F(s)\\right\\} = f(t)= 4 u_1(t) - 2\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147367 1069 "e-6656" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147367 1071 "Find the inverse Laplace transform for $F(s)=\\dfrac{3s}{(s-4)(s+2)}$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147367 1072 #uuid "b7251e69-4dfa-57be-9760-cfc323056148" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147367 1074 "First, we write $F(s)$ as a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3s}{(s-4)(s+2)} = \\dfrac{2}{s-4} + \\dfrac{1}{s+2}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{e^{4t}} &= \\frac{1}{s - 4}, s > 4, \\\\\nL{e^{-2t}} &= \\frac{1}{s + 2}, s > - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{-1}{\\frac{2}{s - 4} + \\frac{1}{s + 2}} &= 2L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s - 4}} + L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s + 2}} \\\\\n&= 2e^{4t} + e^{-2t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\dfrac{3s}{(s-4)(s+2)}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{F(s) \\right\\} = f(t)=2e^{4t} + e^{-2t}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147368 1069 "e-6657" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147368 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ for $s > 1$ if\n$y' + y = 2e^t, \\qquad y(0)=-1$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147368 1072 #uuid "700f5e8f-33f6-568a-ad11-d2664336725d" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147368 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{y^{′} + y} &= L{2e^{t}} \\\\\nL{y^{′}} + L{y} &= 2L{e^{t}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\left\\{y' \\right\\}\n$$\n\nwe use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-1$:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y'\\right\\} = sY(s) - y(0) = sY(s) + 1\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L \\{e^{at}\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-a}, \\qquad s > a\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\{e^t \\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{y^{′}} + L{y} &= 2L{e^{t}} \\\\\n(sY(s) + 1) + Y(s) &= \\frac{2}{s - 1} \\\\\n(s + 1)Y(s) &= \\frac{2}{s - 1} - 1 \\\\\n(s + 1)Y(s) &= (2 - (s - 1))/(s - 1) \\\\\nY(s) &= \\frac{3 - s}{s^{2} - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147369 1069 "e-6661" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147369 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem.\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t} + 4y = 3e^{-t}, \\qquad y(0)=2$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms.\n\nLet $Y(s) = \\mathcal L \\{y(t)\\}$. Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y$, we have\n\n$AAAAAAAA_{A}^{A}$\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n$AAAAAAAA_{A}^{A}$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$AAAAAAAA_{A}^{A}$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147369 1072 #uuid "409c5aa3-5c53-5ab4-b2f7-bcfe01a14914" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147369 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{y'\\} + 4\\mathcal{L}\\{y\\} = 3\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-t}\\}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{y'\\} = sY(s) - y(0)\n$$\n\nand the standard transform\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\} = \\frac{1}{s-a}, \\qquad s > a\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(sY(s) - 2) + 4Y(s) &= 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{s + 1} \\\\\n(s + 4)Y(s) - 2 &= \\frac{3}{s + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s)$. First, add $2$ to the right-hand side and find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(s + 4)Y(s) &= 2 + \\frac{3}{s + 1} \\\\\n(s + 4)Y(s) &= (2(s + 1))/(s + 1) + \\frac{3}{s + 1} \\\\\n(s + 4)Y(s) &= \\frac{2s + 5}{s + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing by $(s+4)$, we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s) =\\dfrac{2s+5}{(s + 4)(s+1)}\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. We write $Y(s)$ in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2s+5}{(s+4)(s+1)} = \\dfrac{A}{s+4} + \\dfrac{B}{s+1}\n$$\n\nTo find the constants $A$ and $B$, we first clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n2s+5 = A(s+1) + B(s+4)\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B$.\n\n- Setting $s = -4$, we have\n$2(-4)+5 = A(-4+1) + B(0) \\implies -3 = -3A \\implies A = 1$.\n- Setting $s = -1$, we have\n$2(-1)+5 = A(0) + B(-1+4) \\implies 3 = 3B \\implies B = 1$.\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s+4} + \\dfrac{1}{s+1}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s + 4}} &= e^{-4t} \\\\\nL^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s + 1}} &= e^{-t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(t) &= L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s + 4}} + L^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s + 1}} \\\\\ny(t) &= e^{-4t} + e^{-t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147370 1069 "e-6663" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147370 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y(t)\\right\\}$ for $s\\geq\\sqrt5$ if\n$y'' - 5y = 4e^{-3t}, \\quad y(0)=2, \\quad y'(0)=-1$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147370 1072 #uuid "8165df56-763a-5f36-9e21-9ed7b0e50190" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147370 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{y^{″} - 5y} &= L{4e^{-3t}} \\\\\nL{y^{″}} - 5L{y} &= 4L{e^{-3t}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y''\\right\\}\n$$\n\nwe use the formula for the Laplace transform of a second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y''\\right\\}=s^2\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y\\right\\}-sy(0)-y'(0)=s^2Y(s)-sy(0)-y'(0)\n$$\n\nTaking into account the given initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y''\\right\\}=s^2Y(s)-2s+1\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{-3t}\\right\\}=\\dfrac{1}{s+3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can solve for $Y(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{y^{″}} - 5L{y} &= 4L{e^{-3t}} \\\\\n(s^{2}Y(s) - 2s + 1) - 5Y(s) &= \\frac{4}{s + 3} \\\\\n(s^{2} - 5)Y(s) &= \\frac{4}{s + 3} + 2s - 1 \\\\\n(s^{2} - 5)Y(s) &= (4 + (2s - 1)(s + 3))/(s + 3) \\\\\n(s^{2} - 5)Y(s) &= \\frac{2s^{2} + 5s + 1}{s + 3} \\\\\nY(s) &= (2s^{2} + 5s + 1)/((s + 3)(s^{2} - 5))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147371 1069 "e-6666" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147371 1071 "Find $y(t)$ if the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n$y'' + 6y = 2\\sin {2t}, \\quad y(0) = 0, \\quad y'(0) = 2$\n\nis given by\n\n$s^2Y(s) - 2 +6 Y(s) = 2\\left(\\dfrac{2}{s^2 + 4}\\right)$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147371 1072 #uuid "cfc54894-5add-5272-bf47-6495f5f4d288" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147371 1074 "Using the transformed equation, we can solve for $Y(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns^{2}Y(s) - 2 + 6Y(s) &= 2(\\frac{2}{s^{2} + 4}), \\\\\n(s^{2} + 6)Y(s) &= 2 + \\frac{4}{s^{2} + 4} \\\\\n(s^{2} + 6)Y(s) &= \\frac{2s^{2} + 12}{s^{2} + 4} \\\\\n(s^{2} + 6)Y(s) &= (2(s^{2} + 6))/(s^{2} + 4) \\\\\nY(s) &= \\frac{2}{s^{2} + 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\nL{\\sin 2t} = \\frac{2}{s^{2} + 4}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL^{-1}{Y(s)} &= L^{-1}{\\frac{2}{s^{2} + 4}} \\\\\ny(t) &= \\sin 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\sin {2t}\n$$\n\nis the solution of the original differential equation." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147372 1069 "e-6667" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147372 1071 "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in $x^3$, of the initial value problem\n$y''=y+e^{-4x}, \\qquad y(0)=0, \\quad y'(0)=2$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147372 1072 #uuid "ce1e321e-5e31-520b-8cea-e8a55292f27c" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147372 1074 "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x) = y(0) + xy'(0) + \\dfrac{x^2}{2!}y''(0) + \\dfrac{x^3}{3!}y^{(3)}(0)+\\cdots\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0)$ and $y'(0)$, so we need to calculate $y''(0)$ and $y^{(3)}(0)$.\n\n- We know that $y(0)=0$, so we substitute this into the given equation to find $y''(0)$:\n$y^{″}(0)|= y(0) + e^{-4(0)}; = 0 + 1; = 1$\n- Next, to find an expression for $y^{(3)}(x)$, we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x$:\n$y^{(3)}=y'-4e^{-4x}$\nWe can now find $y^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n$y^{(3)}(0)|= y^{′}(0) - 4e^{-4(0)}; = 2 - 4; =-2$\n\nFinally, we substitute the obtained values in the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= y(0) + xy^{′}(0) + \\frac{x^{2}}{2!}y^{″}(0) + \\frac{x^{3}}{3!}y^{(3)}(0) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 0 + x \\cdot 2 + \\frac{x^{2}}{2!} \\cdot 1 + \\frac{x^{3}}{3!} \\cdot (-2) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 2x + \\frac{x^{2}}{2} - \\frac{x^{3}}{3} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147373 1069 "e-6668" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147373 1071 "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=2$, in ascending powers of $(x-2)$ up to and including the term in $(x-2)^3$, of the initial value problem\n$y''=y-2xy', \\qquad y(2)=0, \\quad y'(2)=1$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147373 1072 #uuid "f47e7d68-6a0c-54ae-b2ea-bf26c9f35011" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147373 1074 "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=2$:\n\n$$\ny(x) = y(2) + (x-2)y'(2) + \\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{2!}y''(2) + \\dfrac{(x-2)^3}{3!}y^{(3)}(2)+\\cdots\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(2)$ and $y'(2)$, so we need to calculate $y''(2)$ and $y^{(3)}(2)$.\n\n- We substitute $y(2)=0$ and $y'(2)=1$ into the given equation to find $y''(2)$:\n$y^{″}(2)|= y(2) - 2(2)y^{′}(2); = 0 - 4; =-4$\n- Next, to find an expression for $y^{(3)}(x)$, we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x$:\n$y^{(3)}(x)=-y' -2y' - 2xy'' = -3y'- 2xy''$\nWe can now find $y^{(3)}(2)$ by substituting in the known values:\n$y^{(3)}(2)|=-3y^{′}(2) - 2(2)y^{″}(2); =-3 + 16; = 13$\n\nFinally, we substitute the obtained values in the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= y(2) + (x - 2)y^{′}(2) + ((x - 2)^{2})/(2!)y^{″}(2) + ((x - 2)^{3})/(3!)y^{(3)}(2) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 0 + (x - 2) \\cdot 1 + ((x - 2)^{2})/(2) \\cdot (-4) + ((x - 2)^{3})/(6) \\cdot 13 + … \\\\\n&= (x - 2) - 2(x - 2)^{2} + (13(x - 2)^{3})/(6) + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147374 1069 "e-6669" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147374 1071 "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in $x^3$, of the initial value problem\n$y'=2x-y \\cos x, \\qquad y(0)=4$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147374 1072 #uuid "4fd1a1de-847d-5285-a1b3-9b1fcc70f231" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147374 1074 "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x) = y(0) + xy'(0) + \\dfrac{x^2}{2!}y''(0) + \\dfrac{x^3}{3!}y^{(3)}(0)+\\cdots\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0)$, so we need to calculate $y'(0)$, $y''(0)$, and $y^{(3)}(0)$.\n\n- We know that $y(0)=4$, so we substitute this into the given equation to find $y'(0)$:\n$y^{′}(0)|= 2(0) - y(0)\\cos 0; = 0 - 4(1); =-4$\n- Next, to find an expression for $y''(x)$, we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x$:\n$y''(x)=2-y'\\cos x + y \\sin x$\nWe can now find $y''(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n$y^{″}(0)|= 2 - y^{′}(0)\\cos 0 + y(0)\\sin 0; = 2 - (-4)(1) + 0; = 6$\n- To find an expression for $y^{(3)}(x)$, we differentiate $y''(x)$ with respect to $x$:\n$y^{(3)}(x)=-y''\\cos x + 2y'\\sin x + y \\cos x$\nWe can now find $y^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n$y^{(3)}(0)|=-y^{″}(0)\\cos 0 + y^{′}(0)\\sin 0 + y(0)\\cos 0; = - (6)(1) + (-4)(0) + (4)(1); =-2$\n\nFinally, we substitute the obtained values in the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= y(0) + xy^{′}(0) + \\frac{x^{2}}{2!}y^{″}(0) + \\frac{x^{3}}{3!}y^{(3)}(0) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 4 + x \\cdot (-4) + \\frac{x^{2}}{2} \\cdot 6 + \\frac{x^{3}}{6} \\cdot (-2) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 4 - 4x + 3x^{2} - \\frac{x^{3}}{3} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147375 1069 "e-6671" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147375 1071 "Let $y =\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n}$ be a series solution to the equation $y'-3y=0$.\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients $a_n$, where $n=0,1,2 \\ldots$?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147375 1072 #uuid "f47ee36e-c497-5f63-9e2b-b873af5600e9" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147375 1074 "First, we find $y'(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′}(x) &= (∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n})^{′} \\\\\n&= (a_{0} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n})^{′} \\\\\n&= 0 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and $y'(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1} - 3∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m = n-1$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(m = 0)^(∞)(m + 1)a_{m + 1}x^{m} - 3∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)(n + 1)a_{n + 1}x^{n} - 3∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0$, we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)(n + 1)a_{n + 1}x^{n} - ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)3a_{n}x^{n} &= 0 \\\\\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)[(n + 1)a_{n + 1}x^{n} - 3a_{n}x^{n}] &= 0 \\\\\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)[(n + 1)a_{n + 1} - 3a_{n}]x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n(n+1) a_{n+1} -3 a_n = 0\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} =\\dfrac{3 a_n}{n+1}, \\qquad n= 0,1,2 \\ldots\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147376 1069 "e-6672" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147376 1071 "Let $y =\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n}$ be a series solution to the equation $2y''+y=0$. Find $f(n)$, given that the recurrence relation for $a_n$ can be written as\n\n$a_{n+2} = \\dfrac{a_n}{f(n)}, \\qquad n= 0,1,2\\ldots$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147376 1072 #uuid "5466a51e-7dc5-55e6-a269-869cc7b94e83" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147376 1074 "First, we find $y'(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′}(x) &= (∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n})^{′} \\\\\n&= (a_{0} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n})^{′} \\\\\n&= 0 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find $y''(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{″}(x) &= (∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1})^{′} \\\\\n&= (a_{1} + ∑_(n = 2)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1})^{′} \\\\\n&= 0 + ∑_(n = 2)^(∞)n(n - 1)a_{n}x^{n - 2} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 2)^(∞)n(n - 1)a_{n}x^{n - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and $y''(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{n=2}^\\infty n (n-1) a_{n} x^{n-2} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m = n-2$. This gives\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty (m+2) (m+1) a_{m+2} x^{m} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n2\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty (n+2) (n+1) a_{n+2} x^{n} +\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{n} = 0\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0$, we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)2(n + 2)(n + 1)a_{n + 2}x^{n} + ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n} &= 0 \\\\\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)[2(n + 2)(n + 1)a_{n + 2}x^{n} + a_{n}x^{n}] &= 0 \\\\\n∑_(n = 0)^(∞)[2(n + 2)(n + 1)a_{n + 2} + a_{n}]x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n2(n+2)(n+1) a_{n+2} +a_n = 0\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\na_{n+2} = -\\dfrac{a_n}{2(n+1)(n+2)}, \\qquad n= 0,1,2 \\ldots\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(n)=-2(n+1)(n+2)\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147377 1069 "e-6673" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147377 1071 "Let $y =\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^n}$ be a series solution to the equation $y' +4xy = 0$.\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients $a_n$, where $n=1,2,3 \\ldots$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147377 1072 #uuid "d8617bae-7f0f-50d3-8b1a-23b7622956b0" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147377 1074 "First, we find $y'(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′}(x) &= (∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n})^{′} \\\\\n&= (a_{0} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n})^{′} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and $y'(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1} + 4x∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}x^{n} &= 0 \\\\\n∑_(n = 1)^(∞)na_{n}x^{n - 1} + ∑_(n = 0)^(∞)4a_{n}x^{n + 1} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m = n-1$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty (m+1) a_{m+1} x^{m} + \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty 4a_n x^{n+1} =0\n$$\n\nNow, in the second series, we change the index of the summation by writing\n\n$$\nm = n+1\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\sum_{m=0}^\\infty (m+1) a_{m+1} x^{m} + \\sum_{m=1}^\\infty 4a_{m-1} x^{m} =0\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty (n+1) a_{n+1} x^{n} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty 4 a_{n-1} x^{n} =0\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n$. So, we take out the term $n = 0$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\na_1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (n+1) a_{n+1} x^{n} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty 4a_{n-1} x^{n} = 0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[(n + 1)a_{n + 1}x^{n} + 4a_{n - 1}x^{n}] &= 0 \\\\\na_{1} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)[(n + 1)a_{n + 1} + 4a_{n - 1}]x^{n} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of $x^n$, we obtain\n\n$$\na_1 = 0, \\qquad (n+1) a_{n+1} = -4a_{n-1}\n$$\n\nSolving for $a_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = -\\dfrac{4a_{n-1}}{n+1}, \\quad n=1,2,3 \\ldots\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = -\\dfrac{4a_{n-1}}{n+1}, \\quad a_1=0, \\quad n=1,2,3 \\ldots\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index equals to $0$, since $a_1=0$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147378 1069 "e-6724" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147378 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?\n\n1. $y''+4y' - y = 0$\n2. $y''+ 4 = 0$\n3. $y''+y'+4y = 0$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147378 1072 #uuid "18371fa9-696a-5a3a-b7e6-400f14d2fe62" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147378 1074 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- Equation I does not take the form\n$y'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0$.\nIt would require $\\omega^2=-1$, which is not possible for any real number $\\omega$ since $\\omega^2 \\geq 0$.\n- Equation II does not take the form\n$y'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0$\nbecause it has a constant term, $4$.\n- Equation III takes the form\n$y'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0$\nwith\n$\\omega^2 = 4 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\omega = 2$\nand $b = 1$. Therefore, equation III represents damped harmonic motion.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147379 1069 "e-6725" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147379 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y'' +y'+2 y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n2. $y'' +2y'+ y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n3. $y'' +4y'+3y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147379 1072 #uuid "365ec1d4-0301-5dfe-a731-35f12ea4d3a5" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147379 1074 "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $\\mathcal{D} > 0$ leads to heavy damping,\n- $\\mathcal{D} = 0$ leads to critical damping, and\n- $\\mathcal{D} < 0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation $y'' +y'+2 y = 0$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n$\\lambda^2 + \\lambda + 2 = 0$.\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is:\n$D = 1^{2} - 4(1)(2)|=-7 < 0$,\nwhich corresponds to light damping. Therefore, statement I is correct.\n- The differential equation $y'' +2y'+y = 0$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n$\\lambda^2 +2\\lambda + 1 = 0$.\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is\n$D = 2^{2} - 4(1)(1)|= 0$,\nwhich corresponds to critical damping. Therefore, statement II is correct.\n- The differential equation $y'' +4y'+3 y$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n$\\lambda^2 +4\\lambda + 3 = 0$.\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is\n$D = 4^{2} - 4(1)(3)|= 4 > 0$,\nwhich corresponds to heavy damping. Therefore, statement III is correct.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147380 1069 "e-6726" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147380 1071 "A particle of mass $5\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 15\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2$. The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c = 10\\, \\textrm{kg/s}$. The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147380 1072 #uuid "0458947c-70c8-5f88-9e52-68c8950afd7c" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147380 1074 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm y'' =-cy' -k y\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$, $k$, and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n5 y'' =-10y' -15 y\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5y^{″} + 10y^{′} + 15y &= 0 \\\\\ny^{″} + 2y^{′} + 3y &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147381 1069 "e-6727" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147381 1071 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 2y' + 5y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147381 1072 #uuid "c93a18f3-6d09-5d88-8a90-434fc08d3874" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147381 1074 "In order to find the period of the damped oscillation, we need to find the solution and then find the period of the sinusoidal terms in the solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we first find the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + 2\\lambda + 5 = 0\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 2λ + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} + 2λ + 1) - 1 + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 1)^{2} + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 1)^{2} &= -4 \\\\\nλ + 1 &= ± 2\\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= -1 ± 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny_c =e^{-t}\\left(A\\cos{2t} + B\\sin{2t}\\right)\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of sinusoidal frequency is\n\n$$\n\\Omega = 2\\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}\n$$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the sinusoidal frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\Omega}\n$$\n\nSo, the period is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{2} = \\pi \\,\\textrm{s}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147382 1069 "e-6731" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147382 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t > 0$. Determine whether each equation is forced or free, and whether it is damped.\n\n1. $y''+y'+3y = t$\n2. $y''+ 4y = e^t$\n3. $y''+y'+3y = 0$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147382 1072 #uuid "bd1d2ef2-9472-5e57-b071-fbbaf80023da" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147382 1074 "The differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = f(t)\n$$\n\nis forced if $f(t)$ is not identical to zero, and damped if $b>0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's classify each equation.\n\n- Equation I represents forced harmonic motion with damping. The forcing function $f(t)=t$ is not always zero, and there is a damping term with $b=1>0$.\n- Equation II represents forced harmonic motion without damping. The forcing function $f(t)=e^t$ is not always zero, but there is no damping term since $b=0$.\n- Equation III represents free (non-forced) harmonic motion with damping. The forcing function $f(t)=0$ is always zero, and there is a damping term with $b=1>0$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147383 1069 "e-6732" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147383 1071 "A particle of mass $3\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 6\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2$. The particle is also subject to the resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity with a damping coefficient $c = 9\\, \\textrm{kg/s}$, and an external force given by $F(t)=3t^2\\,\\textrm{N}$.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147383 1072 #uuid "11a44451-5b9f-5892-b321-925b2a393551" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147383 1074 "The general equation of forced harmonic motion with damping is\n\n$$\nm y'' =-cy' -k y + F(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$, $k$, $c$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n3 y'' =-9y' -6 y+3t^2\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\ny'' + 3y'+2y = t^2\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147384 1069 "e-6733" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147384 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$y''+2y' + 5 y = 50t$.\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)$?\n\n1. $y =Ae^{-t}+Be^{-2t}+25t+5$\n2. $y = R e ^ {-t} \\cos \\left(2t + \\phi \\right) +10t - 4$\n3. $y =e^{-t}\\left(A\\cos{2t} + B\\sin{2t}\\right)+10 t-4$\n\nNote that $A$ and $B$ are constants." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147384 1072 #uuid "aa77f4ef-67b6-5041-9a3b-1a36ffd5582d" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147384 1074 "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t) = y_c(t)+y_p(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution $y_c(t)$, which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\ny''+2y' + 5 y = 0\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 2λ + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} + 2λ + 1) - 1 + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 1)^{2} + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 1)^{2} &= -4 \\\\\nλ + 1 &= ± 2\\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= -1 ± 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complementary function $y_c(t)$ is can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{c}(t) &= e^{-t}(A\\cos 2t + B\\sin 2t), \\\\\ny_{c}(t) &= Re^{-t}\\cos (2t + ϕ), \\\\\ny_{c}(t) &= Re^{-t}\\sin (2t + ϕ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution $y_p(t)$. Note that the external force\n\n$$\nf(t)=50t\n$$\n\nis a linear function. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a linear function,\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha = 10$ and $\\beta = -4$. So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(t)= 10 t - 4\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(t) &= e^{-t}(A\\cos 2t + B\\sin 2t) + 10t - 4, \\\\\ny(t) &= Re^{-t}\\cos (2t + ϕ) + 10t - 4, \\\\\ny(t) &= Re^{-t}\\sin (2t + ϕ) + 10t - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly II and III match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147385 1069 "e-6734" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147385 1071 "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + y =2e^{-t}$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\pi$ seconds." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147385 1072 #uuid "20b53dff-43b9-5256-ab7d-67aff56db408" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147385 1074 "This is an initial value problem. Since the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and $y'(0)=0$. So, we want to solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' + y =2e^{-t}, \\quad y(0)=0, \\quad y'(0)=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the general solution of the differential equation. The complementary solution $y_c(t)$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 1\n$$\n\nso we have\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{t} + B\\sin{t}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution $y_p(t)$. Note that the external force\n\n$$\nf(t) = 2e^{-t}\n$$\n\nis an exponential function, and the constant factor in the exponent $(-1)$ is not a root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular exponential solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{-t}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nSubstituting $y_p$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha=1$. Therefore, the particular solution $y_p(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) =e^{-t}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\cos{t} + B\\sin{t} + e^{-t}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. Substituting $y(0)=0$ gives $A=-1$, and differentiating $y$ and substituting $y'(0)=0$ gives $B=1$.\n\nSo, our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=-\\cos t + \\sin{t} +e^{-t}\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\pi$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\pi$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(π) &= -\\cos π + \\sin π + e^{- π} \\\\\n&= (1 + e^{- π})m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147386 1069 "e-6736" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147386 1071 "Given that for $s > 0$,\n$L{\\sin ωt} = \\frac{ω}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, L{\\cos ωt} = \\frac{s}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}$,\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t) = 3\\cos{9t} - 2\\sin{9t}$ for $s > 0$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147386 1072 #uuid "db1ffc3b-835c-5b53-bd41-802a7841e3ba" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147386 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\alpha f(t) + \\beta g(t)\\} = \\alpha\\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\} + \\beta\\cdot \\mathcal L \\{g(t)\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{3\\cos 9t - 2\\sin 9t} &= L{3\\cos 9t} + L{-2\\sin 9t} \\\\\n&= 3L{\\cos 9t} - 2L{\\sin 9t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}L{\\sin ωt} &= \\frac{ω}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0, \\\\ L{\\cos ωt}|= \\frac{s}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, & s > 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{\\cos 9t} &= \\frac{s}{s^{2} + 81}, s > 0 \\\\\nL{\\sin 9t} &= \\frac{9}{s^{2} + 81}, s > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f(t)} &= 3L{\\cos 9t} - 2L{\\sin 9t} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot \\frac{s}{s^{2} + 81} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{9}{s^{2} + 81} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3s}{s^{2} + 81} - \\frac{18}{s^{2} + 81} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3s - 18}{s^{2} + 81}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147387 1069 "e-8085" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147387 1071 "What is the most common value in the data set represented by the dot plot below?\n\n![](images/42/421ccc9dee6e510c80a9c4a4925a046f93096e8a5948119f4527d806732b9b1b.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147387 1072 #uuid "228a8064-f107-551b-9206-3c8893943dd0" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147387 1074 "The plot tells us the following:\n\n- the value $2$ appears three times (there are $3$ dots over $2$)\n- the value $3$ appears twice (there are $2$ dots over $3$)\n- the value $4$ appears four times (there are $4$ dots over $4$)\n- the value $5$ appears zero times (there are no dots over $5$)\n- the value $\\color{red}6$ appears five times (there are $5$ dots over $6$)\n- the value $7$ appears twice (there are $2$ dots over $7$)\n\nTherefore, the most common value among the given data set is $6$.\n\n![](images/3a/3ae14ab25d3d4a6819b3be8186ad7bca29cab32c8c34ae16e611ed3d4d32de78.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147388 1069 "e-8533" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147388 1071 "Find the phase line of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y^2 + 2y$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147388 1072 #uuid "842d6fb8-d895-584f-9a49-5d90409f1ba2" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147388 1074 "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} + 2y &= 0 \\\\\ny(y + 2) &= 0 \\\\\ny &= -2, 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=0$.\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)< 0$.\n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $y$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(y)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (0, \\infty)$, and negative for $y\\in (-2,0)$.\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y) > 0$\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y) < 0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/69/6940ca72733614d6916831fc0316ae75ca0bb07f75082c36be3897ebf9fd55d5.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147389 1069 "e-8534" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147389 1071 "The slope field of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y)$ is shown below. What is the phase line of the given equation?\n\n![](images/d5/d5db048e8e777aa02eb9b3fe22c9279b831b8510125b40cd361ae330fd1371a8.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147389 1072 #uuid "45254b75-3db0-5c50-970a-4eea7db883f6" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147389 1074 "From the slope field, we can deduce the following:\n\n- $y = 0$ and $y = 3$ are equilibrium solutions.\n- For all $y \\in (0,3)$, the slopes of the slope field are negative. Therefore, $f(y) < 0$ in this region.\n- For all $y \\in (-\\infty,0) \\cup (3,\\infty)$, the slopes of the slope field are positive. Therefore, $f(y) > 0$ in this region.\n\nNow, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y) > 0$\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y) < 0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/09/09fbbcef280dc3d6c0b68e2de23643fae8cbda246863d1b1d325ccb1c6368b4d.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147390 1069 "e-8535" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147390 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = f(y)$, where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown below. What is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?\n\n![](images/55/5506c77f5f257d4198cc665b3af34b900d37704fa2f09a53197462df694d018d.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147390 1072 #uuid "adebd9bd-412d-5e0e-ab21-0087a5c35d9c" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147390 1074 "From the graph of the function $f(y)$, we can deduce the following information:\n\n- The solutions to $f(y) = 0$ are $y = -2$ and $y = 0$. Therefore $y = -2, 0$ are the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n- $f(y) >0$ when $y \\in (-\\infty,-2) \\cup (-2,0)$.\n- $f(y)< 0$ when $y \\in (0, \\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y) > 0$\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y) < 0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/fc/fcc9f998111493167284cf5844645ac2201a230b073a9336604fb790ba16bad4.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147391 1069 "e-8536" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147391 1071 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of $y'= y^3-2y^2$?\n\n1. $y=0$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a node\n2. $y=0$ is a node, and $y=2$ is a source\n3. $y=0$ is a node, and $y=2$ is a sink" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147391 1072 #uuid "eed50b24-e97c-51c9-b625-3327783b6374" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147391 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve\n\n$$\nf(y)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(y) &= 0 \\\\\ny^{3} - 2y^{2} &= 0 \\\\\ny^{2}(y - 2) &= 0 \\\\\ny &= 0, 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2$.\n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y^2$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $y-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(y)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (2,\\infty)$, and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty,0) \\cup (0,2)$.\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](images/42/420aaceb6f78b8a6aa55f8e0a2a9a87e604875180a9321ff34bd72ca2384ec09.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=0$ is a node because it is neither a source nor a sink.\n- $y=2$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=0$ is a node, and $y=2$ is a source\"." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147392 1069 "e-8537" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147392 1071 "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y= -2$ is a source\n- $y= 3$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?\n\n![](images/07/07fcd4a3db9ed24d7e0c391095e5950c11be4b5dac2dcc5b4cda229d69f2aaa4.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147392 1072 #uuid "dba1deed-4311-54fe-a690-8d89d4cb9592" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147392 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information.\n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-2$ and $y=3$.\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-2$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n![](images/0a/0af6403f55ca8193993fee474183676ad4edfb0b99b4bf4707993ff512700358.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](images/3f/3faccf2319817824a473b127ee7eff831ae7de82d9198b541b4d89dd7ef58ec2.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2$ and $y=3$.\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to converge toward this line." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147393 1069 "e-8538" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147393 1071 "![](images/87/87dbc31b08ad8735b7a8e9f18bc74c448a221fe665acca3d40208786f20459ce.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y= -3$ is a sink.\n2. $y= -1$ is a node.\n3. $y= 2$ is a source." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147393 1072 #uuid "7aa0a0a8-05d4-532c-a830-f1df43514fdf" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147393 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/41/413ddd3248fb53bbd623794970ee2b4a561d17e2659bb72cf864436d9fffa792.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=-3$ is a source.\n- Statement II is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a sink.\n- Statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147394 1069 "e-8586" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147394 1071 "![](images/d4/d4aac304110ed4743b58ba786141cecc404791130bf35884f8fbefe94c18fbd6.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}= y^2 -4$\n2. $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4-y^2$\n3. $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}= (y-2)^2$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147394 1072 #uuid "fba1d686-de7e-5e8e-ae0b-1f4f04fa79ba" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147394 1074 "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y)\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=2$.\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y \\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (2, \\infty)$.\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-2,2)$.\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering $y'= y^2 -4$.\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y+2)(y-2)$, so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,2$. So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows:\n$(-\\infty,-2)$$(-2,2)$$(2,\\infty)$$y+2$${\\color{red}-}$${\\color{blue}+}$${\\color{blue}+}$$y-2$${\\color{red}-}$${\\color{red}-}$${\\color{blue}+}$$f(y)$${\\color{blue}+}$${\\color{red}-}$${\\color{blue}+}$\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-2,2)$, and $f(y)$ is positive for $y \\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (2,\\infty)$. This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I has the given phase line.\n- Next, we consider $y'=4-y^2$.\n\nFirst, $f(y)=4-y^2$, so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,2$. So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y) < 0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty, -2)$.\nThis does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n- Finally, we consider $y'=(y-2)^2$.\nIn this case, $f(y)= (y-2)^2$, and the equilibrium solution is $y=2$ only.\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147395 1069 "e-8650" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147395 1071 "![](images/f4/f495896e6df702a60eea6825d7d72c31e978d92d68ec7b1779fe565a8dc9b946.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y=1$ is a source\n2. $y=-2$ is a sink\n3. $y=-2$ is a node" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147395 1072 #uuid "bb252f4f-ef82-52bc-a485-f88f0de96173" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147395 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=1$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from $y=1$. This means that $y = 1$ is a source.\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points downward too. In other words, both arrows point in the *same* direction on either side of $y=-2$. This means that $y=-2$ is a node.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147396 1069 "e-8963" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147396 1071 "The substitution $z=y^m$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}$\ninto an equation of the form $z'(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x)$. What is the function $Q(x)$?" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147396 1072 #uuid "d1946905-5833-58c2-9a0a-4dfd04eb922b" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147396 1074 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = x^3, \\qquad q(x) = 1, \\qquad n=-\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by $y^{1/2}$, and we get\n\n$$\ny^{1/2} \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y^{3/2}= 1.\\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} = y^{3/2}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{3}{2}y^{1/2} \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y^{1/2}\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation $(\\ast)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^3\\,z = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{3}{2}x^3z = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nQ(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147397 1069 "e-8965" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147397 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{x^2}{y}$." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147397 1072 #uuid "047ea3c1-8ebf-5e26-9f28-c5a87e7cf997" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147397 1074 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad q(x) = x^2, \\qquad n=-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by $y^{-1}$, and we get\n\n$$\ny \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + \\dfrac{y^{2}}{x}= x^2\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} = y^{2}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{1}{x}z = x^2\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{2}{x} z =2x^2\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x) = \\frac{2}{x}, Q(x) = 2x^{2}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= e^{\\intP(x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{\\int(2/x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{2\\ln x} \\\\\n&= x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{dz}{dx} + \\frac{2}{x}z) &= x^{2} \\cdot (2x^{2}) \\\\\nx^{2} \\cdot \\frac{dz}{dx} + 2xz &= 2x^{4} \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(zx^{2}) &= 2x^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{d}{dx}(zx^{2})dx &= \\int2x^{4}dx \\\\\nzx^{2} &= \\frac{2x^{5}}{5} + C_{1} \\\\\nz &= \\frac{2x^{5} + C}{5x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5C_1$.\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z=y^{2}$. This results in the general solution\n\n$$\ny^{2} =\\dfrac{2x^5 + C}{5x^2}\n$$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147398 1069 "e-8975" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147398 1071 "What is the name of the solid shown below?\n\n![](images/43/4397eb1e78f99d0f6cda1515a4aef23d1a9e44235623c086f5ae849b4863fb45.png)" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147398 1072 #uuid "4447e034-350f-5e6b-a501-0f57a3888bd0" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147398 1074 "The solid is a **right cone**.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The solid has one circular base and one vertex. So, it's a cone.\n- The axis that passes through the center of the circle and the vertex is perpendicular to the base. So, it's right." 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147399 1069 "e-9069" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147399 1071 "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n1. $y\\,y' + x^2y^2 = e^x y^4$\n2. $xy' + x^3y = -x^2 y^3$\n3. $y' + y\\cos{x} = e^{y+1}$" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147399 1072 #uuid "b5189ec2-ea1c-5036-9543-b963bd072ab7" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147399 1074 "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n$y' + x^2y = e^x y^3$\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n$p(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) = e^x, \\qquad n=3$.\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n$y' + x^2y = -xy^3$\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n$p(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) =-x, \\qquad n=3$.\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x)y^n$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147400 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048831 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147400 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049374 13194139533363 17592186045585 true] [17592186147400 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147938 13194139533363 1